#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3629
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A **VM** is the durable user machine. A **placement** binds it to one host and\nstorage allocation. A **generation** fences superseded commands. An **epoch**\nfences ownership or session changes. A **receipt** records a specific effect;\nreceipt delivery is distinct from the effect itself. A **profile** selects guest\ncapabilities and isolation policy. An **image** is an immutable boot specification."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3633
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A Lilly delete with unconfirmed physical cleanup retains its durable fence and\nretries with delays growing to thirty seconds. Pool-lock contention is not a\ncleanup failure: it is retried on the next actor tick without that backoff.\nThe dataset precondition uses an identity observation (ADR-0143). It needs the\nsame identity, policy, snapshot and coherent accounting as a capacity sample,\nbut tolerates usage-counter drift, because pool-wide `available` moves with any\nwrite. Earlier exact-usage checks produced 5–23 minute production deletes\nunder pool writes. Each unconfirmed attempt reports a fixed `stage`.\nStage `destroy` with `lilly.zfs_namespace_cleanup_unconfirmed` exposed the\nremaining tail: Nix build sandboxes (user namespaces) held locked copies of a\nguest dataset, which ordinary unmount cannot remove, so deletion waited for the\nbuild. Lilly hosts therefore run `nix-daemon` in a private mount namespace\nwithout pool mounts (ADR-0144). The same lock affected services with private\nuser namespaces (Prometheus, VictoriaLogs, rootless containers) on Overalls, so\n`lilly-pool-propagation` makes every pool mount recursively private before Lilly\nstarts; guest mounts then propagate into no other namespace. Namespaces created\nwhile a guest is mounted still copy it, and no machine test yet covers this\nsecond path. Repeated cleanup failures never\nestablish deletion or retire the actor; a newer generation bypasses the old\nretry deadline. A generated [actor retry property](repo:lilly/test/vm/actor_test.exs)\nchecks this local journal and scheduling path. Its controlled executor does not\nestablish native ZFS cleanup or Marshall allocation release."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3637
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A caller waits on a message for its turn and only then takes the native pool\nlock. The native lock still excludes and still provides crash custody; the\nqueue only decides who goes next. The two pool sections of a create may wait\nup to 30 s for their turn, so a burst of creates drains in arrival order\ninstead of returning `storage_busy` after one second and retrying on a later\ntick. A disk delete waits the same 30 s, so it keeps its place in the queue\ninstead of rejoining at the back. Creates waiting together are prepared together. The create that gets\nthe turn also takes up to 15 queued creates for the same pool. It prepares the\nwhole batch with one pool snapshot, one allocation audit and one re-read after\nthe batch's own mutations, and answers each waiting create with that create's\nown result. A create's readiness check is batched the same way: one owner\nlisting, mount table, staged-import scan and identity observation for the\nbatch, then each create's own checks. One create failing does not fail the\nothers, but a failed shared read fails the whole batch. A crashed queue\nloses only that ordering. A separate BEAM without the\nqueue, such as an operator `lilly eval` tool or a machine-test probe, contends\nonly on the native lock, polling it for at most two seconds. Preparation,\nreadiness and owned-identity checks grant no capacity credit, so their snapshots\nuse identity usage. They keep every identity, ownership, snapshot, policy and\naccounting check, but usage counters that move between the two reads no longer\nresample them. Only an exact snapshot (the capacity report and backups) returns\nallocation credit. Usage counters moving between the\ntwo exact reads are resampled up to three times, 25 ms apart, and the latest\ncoherent tree is then the observation, so ordinary guest writes never withhold\ncapacity; the pool lock is awaited 200 ms before the snapshot reports it held.\nReal transient contention (the pool lock still held) is reported as `busy`:\nMarshall keeps the previous numbers untouched and, while the last real\nmeasurement is within a fifteen-second busy grace, extends their freshness;\npast that the row ages out of the five-second capacity window like a late\nreport, and the grace plus the capacity window can never exceed host authority.\nEvery other withholding is `unavailable` and revokes capacity freshness at once\n(ADR-0031, ADR-0163). Pool\naccounting requires unique dataset GUIDs, and snapshot GUIDs unique within\ntheir dataset: `zfs receive` preserves snapshot GUIDs, so a same-pool clone copy\nlegitimately repeats its source's `lilly-b` GUID. Requiring pool-wide snapshot\nuniqueness had rejected every observation of a pool holding such a copy, so the\nsource could not pass restart readiness and the pool's capacity was withheld.\nThe compatible-reader release is healthy on both enrolled hosts and Marshall has\nthe additive `pool_id` audit column. Boot readiness was enabled on both hosts at\n`c98a375c`, and the template-producer release enabled sealed templates (32 GiB\nper pool) on both hosts after cross-host identical guest images were observed and\na templates-off baseline was taken; ADR-0159 then removed both switches. A\nreader migration or one-off uncached SSH canary does not establish sealed\ntemplate publication on the deployed pools."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3641
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A crash or capacity refusal leaves a pending/failed thread with a recovery path.\nRetry retains the admission key while the same machine is recoverable; replacement\nadmission requires completed physical cleanup before a fresh key is assigned.\nArchive/delete requests record deleting intent and use Compute's lifecycle\ncoordinator. The thread remains deleting until retained VM history reports\nphysical archival; a desired deleted state alone is insufficient. An authorized\nreopen clears the retired binding, retains the original create request key,\nassigns a fresh machine key and queues admission atomically. Durable\n`ThreadGeneration` rows retain each machine/root/thread identity. PaperTrail and\ngeneration rows preserve the old identities; authorized archived transcript\nreads can select an earlier generation through the read-only workspace selector.\nThe selected generation repeats root, owner and thread checks and cannot prompt,\nopen a terminal or mutate a machine.\nArchiving also cancels any pending human planning question with an `archived`\nreason before deletion intent or retained archival is committed."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3645
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A current signed-in user reads the Control room's bounded, redacted projections\nwithout administrator elevation. An administrator mutation requires current\npersisted membership, a live stored login, an unexpired session-bound passkey\nreceipt and its current Cedar action. The fifteen-minute receipt uses database\ntime and never renews through activity. The continuation is socket-local;\nnavigation or reconnect discards it and requires a new explicit submission.\nFinal merged packaged browser checks passed ordinary/remembered reads, no-key\nSecurity return and explicit retry, the enrolled-key modal and colocated\ncross-replica revocation. Native timed expiry, machine-page recovery and complete\nstale-action acceptance remain separate gaps. Expiry\nremoves privileged authority; ordinary reads continue while the login remains\nvalid."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3649
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A failed SSH signing attempt retains its public generation. Matching retries\ntransition it back to pending, so an unavailable private signer does not append\na history row on every guest synchronization attempt. Changed keys and renewal\nwindows still allocate a new generation.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3653
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A failed placement whose desired state is still `running` keeps its command,\nbut Lilly does not retry it on its own. Once a VM actor has durably recorded\n(or re-observed unchanged) a failed outcome for the exact current command, it\nsettles: its one-second tick and unchanged reconcile requests start no further\nwork, so the failure fence, its locks and its cleanup run once rather than\nevery second. The durable journal still carries that failed outcome to Marshall\nuntil acknowledged, exactly as before. Recovery stays with Marshall: a crash\nwithin the restart budget, an owner start or retry, and a deletion each arrive\nas a newer command, which the actor drives at once. A Lilly or actor restart\nre-observes the failure once, so readiness still rests on an observation made\nby the current runtime, then settles again without appending an outcome.\nJournal contention, retryable results and unconfirmed cleanup (`unknown`) do\nnot settle and are retried on the tick as before. Lilly logs `vm.failed` (VM,\nplacement and the closed reason `execution_failed` or `crashed`) only after the\njournal records a new failed outcome: once per generation and reason, again\nafter a recorded recovery or for a newer command's failure, and not again after\na Lilly restart over the same journaled failure. On 2026-10-17 a failed\nplacement was re-driven, and `vm.failed` emitted, every second until the VMs\nwere deleted. The\n[actor failure-event property](repo:lilly/test/vm/actor_failure_event_test.exs)\nchecks generated failure, recovery, restart and newer-command histories, and\nthe [settled-failure property](repo:lilly/test/vm/actor_failure_settled_test.exs)\nchecks that ticks and unchanged reconcile requests start no work after a\nrecorded failure, that a replacement actor drives it exactly once, that a newer\ncommand or deletion still drives, and that the actor's real timer starts no work\nfor two periods. Both use a controlled executor; no native machine test\nexercises a settled failure."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3657
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A firmware-booted guest without agents ([ADR-0170](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0170-lilly-firmware-guest-launch.md))\nlearns its address from Firecracker's MMDS instead of the kernel command line:\nLilly seeds `169.254.169.254/lilly` with the placement identity, the `/32`, the\ngateway and the MAC before launch, and the guest's bootstrap task applies exactly\nthat address with an on-link gateway route. MMDS frames are answered inside the\nVMM and never reach the tap, so the per-tap source guard and MAC check are\nunchanged; a guest that configures any other address stays unreachable. The\nbootstrap resolves public names through the same resolvers as Linux guests\n(1.1.1.1, 9.9.9.9) and sends only `.internal` names to Lilly's private resolver\nat 169.254.254.1, which it reaches through the gateway. Windows guest agents (`windows-v1`,\n[ADR-0171](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0171-windows-guest-agents.md)) talk to\nLilly over virtio-vsock using the same jail-local endpoint and fixed port\nprotocols as Linux agents. Windows discovers the address family through the\nvirtio-win `viosock` provider. Native FreeBSD/illumos rows reserve that transport\nshape, but stock kernels lack the required AF_VSOCK/virtio-vsock support.\nThe custom socket/virtio wrappers and shared bounded frame/credit core now\nexist, with target compilation and Linux property evidence. Native host-CID\ntransport and reset acceptance remain unverified,\nand every native capability stays false under\n[ADR-0190](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0190-native-kernel-provenance-and-admission.md).\nThe firmware jail adds the vsock device only for an agent set that speaks these\nprotocols, and the agentless image keeps none. Every guest response remains\nbound to host CID 2, guest CID 3 and the current boot identity."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3661
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A fresh complete public object may serve a single byte range. A supplied If-Range\nmust match its strong ETag or Last-Modified value; weak or mismatched validators\ngo upstream. Range/If-Range bypass new storage admission; they do not remove\ncredential or other privacy refusals during lookup. Short, overflowing, aborted\nor owner-interrupted writes cannot publish. Store restart discards unfinished\nwrites and recovers complete objects, while new writes await fresh safe pressure.\nAn active reader pins its object during pressure eviction until close or process\nloss; a later observation reclaims it."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3665
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A fresh magic-link sign-in mints its authoritative browser login inside the\nsign-in transaction, which serializes against revocation through the\ninstallation latch and the account row lock. A sign-in committed before an MFA\nreset, retirement or **Sign out everywhere** revocation is always revoked with\nthe account's other credentials; MFA reset and retirement also revoke the link\nitself, so a sign-in ordered after them is refused. Ordinary **Sign out\neverywhere** is not a credential ban: a magic-link sign-in ordered after it\nremains an intentional fresh email sign-in. The strategy's own post-commit\nissuance is never the returned credential and is revoked before the action\nreturns, and a login revoked between issuance and the final server callback is\nrefused with a generic message, never an error page. The\n[login race review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-login-races.md)\nrecords both orders across three independent control planes and the native\nexpired-ceremony refusal with a cross-replica fresh retry."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3673
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A generated SQL-backed [prompt history](repo:marshall/test/marshall/agents/prompt_test.exs)\nstarts a stopped synthetic VM, repeats the same prompt key, then submits changed\ntext under that key. It retains one turn, prompt event, admission and wake audit\nreceipt, and one agent start operation. Concurrent same-key submissions, lost\nreplies and physical guest wake are not established by this property."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3677
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A generated [model claim history](repo:marshall/test/marshall/agents/model_calls_test.exs)\nchecks repeated host polls and claims aged on either side of the ten-minute\nexpiry threshold. A live claim keeps its identity and completes once; an\nexpired claim becomes uncertain, fails its turn and refuses a late completion.\nThe test uses local PostgreSQL and a synthetic provider response. Concurrent\nclaimants, lost network replies and actual provider effects remain unverified."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3681
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A generated local recovery history holds a completed executor result in a\nsuspended actor's mailbox while its journal still contains only attempt intent.\nActor death and two replacement lifetimes preserve one completed launch and the\nexact recovered outcome identity and sequence. The four-property file passed;\nthe [recovery review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-lilly-completed-launch-recovery.md)\nlimits this evidence to actual actors and a durable controlled executor. Native\nsystemd, Firecracker, ZFS and full application death remain open."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3685
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A guard blocked behind exclusive intent custody must acquire its shared lock\nbefore reading the current intent. Stopped, foreign, superseded or unsafe intent\nmust refuse exec; unchanged intent must execute. The generated kernel-witnessed\nwaiter property and exec-marker control passed in a fresh127-second native package\nwith three library properties and six integration checks, rustfmt and Clippy.\nThis verifies local lock/intent ordering, not journal/systemd/Firecracker recovery.\nSee the [waiting-intent review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-05-launch-guard-waiting-intent.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3689
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A guest whose boot bundle declares no agents (a firmware-booted image,\n[ADR-0170](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0170-lilly-firmware-guest-launch.md)) never exchanges\nreadiness: Lilly records trust as `skipped` for the exact boot instead of\ndelivering it, advances provisioning to the terminal `unattested` phase (a boot\nchallenge, never a boot UUID, no path to `ready`) and reports the VM running\nwithout a `guest_ready` fact. Marshall admits only the physical service level\nfor such an operation. Whether a boot exchanges readiness and trust, and how\nlong readiness may take, is decided per agent set by the protocol's capability\ntable ([ADR-0171](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0171-windows-guest-agents.md)):\nLinux guests keep the 120 s budget (2400 s for Inception), `windows-v1` guests\nget 600 s (matching Marshall's provision service level), and the budget is\nrecorded once at the first launch of a boot and never extended by a relaunch.\nWhen a trust or completion transition reaches a boot whose journal has no\ndeadline yet, Lilly takes the budget from the validated placement-local\nbundle's agent set, so a FreeBSD or illumos guest never falls back to the Linux\nbudget; an unreadable bundle keeps that legacy fallback.\nThe two completions are exclusive: the ready commit refuses an agent set\nwithout readiness, and the `unattested` commit refuses an agent set that\nproves readiness, so an agent guest is never reported running without its\nready line."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3693
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A launch runs as six steps: begin (claim and running intent), disk, boot\nrecords, jail, logging and network, and commit\n([ADR-0176](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0176-host-concurrency-single-writers-and-effects.md)\ndecision 2). Each step holds the placement lease only for its own duration and\nwaits for it in lilly-hostd's queue for at most two seconds, so a boot probe or\nobservation between steps no longer waits behind a whole create. Before acting,\nevery step after begin re-reads its fence: the exact intent begin set, no\ndeletion tombstone, a stopped unit and no launch attempt at or beyond that\nrevision. A changed fence stops the launch as preempted before that step's\neffects. Commit checks the actor's commit grant, persists `launch-attempt.bin`\nand starts the unit in one section, so a launch is attempted at most once per\nrevision. A launch that stops between steps starts again from begin; each step\nis idempotent (an existing correctly bound disk is reused)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3697
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A lost Marshall or database cannot grant new editor authority. Existing Audi\ncommunication expires within its thirty-second lease unless current-account,\nstored-login, write and exact execution/release authority renews. Hints prompt an\nearlier renewal. A closed/expired lease or changed account, gateway, host,\nplacement or execution cannot revive; reconnect requires fresh admission. A\nLilly restart replaces only the provisioner's control session: while Lilly\nreconnects, editor and web renewal answer `unavailable`, which Audi retries\ninside the unchanged lease, and the first report on the new session lets\nrenewal succeed with the same binding\n([ADR-0192](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0192-staged-detached-ingress.md)).\nIf Lilly does not return within the lease, the stream ends at its deadline. Relay\nclosure is not proof that a guest terminal or extension process terminated."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3701
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A minimal role fragment uses runtime file paths for private material:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3705
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A mutation validates the actor, locks relevant rows, checks current authority,\nrecords intent and work, and commits. Outcome diagnostics follow commit. Retry\nwith the original idempotency key after an uncertain response. A new key denotes\na new request and may create a second resource."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3709
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A new revision whose system closure and manifest equal the active ones still\nstages every candidate secret bundle privately. Byte-identical bundles keep the\nverify-only path; any difference (a secret-only change) takes preflight,\nmigration and activation, and an unresolvable bundle fails at `stage` without\ntouching live bundles. During activation, once every consumer has stopped, the\nindependent secret-preparation units stop in one transaction under their own\n300-second deadline, while consumers keep one-at-a-time dependent stops. This\ncorrects overalls activations that ran past a single sequential deadline and\nrestored the previous system after several minutes (ADR-0036, 2026-10-07). A\nswitch that exits non-zero after the candidate system is already active (for\nexample a logged-in user's manager failing to reload) and leaves no newly\nfailed system unit records `update.switch_incomplete` and proceeds to role\nrestoration and health checks; a newly failed unit still fails activation."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3713
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A new verified registration may reuse the released email while cleanup is pending.\nIt receives a fresh UUID and inherits no administrator role, passkeys, SSH keys,\nsharing grants, team membership or machines. Earlier email links remain refused.\nMachines merely shared with the retired account survive. Use **Users**\nfor invitation and typed retirement."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3717
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A newer stop or delete that arrives earlier kills the task and starts at once.\nKilling the task closes its native lock owners, which kill and reap their\nutilities before releasing their locks, as on any caller death. The\npreempted attempt persists no launch attempt and leaves only the\ncrash-recoverable claims that a crash at the same point would leave. Each\npreemption is recorded as `vm.execution_preempted`."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3721
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A pin is a personal ordering preference recorded by\n[ADR-0193](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0193-per-user-machine-pins.md) as a\n`Marshall.Access.Pin` row per user and machine. Its state machine has two states,\n`pinned` and `unpinned`; `unpin` and `repin` are the only transitions, and the row\nis archived (not transitioned) when the machine is archived. One live row exists\nper user/machine pair, so repinning reuses it and refreshes `pinned_at`."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3725
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A provision/start outcome may include `guest_ready: true` only after current\nboot readiness succeeds. The flag is a coherent running physical receipt tied\nto the admitted operation, VM, placement, execution epoch, generation and host.\nMarshall rejects a readiness flag on a stop, delete or resize operation. Readiness\narrival uses the committed physical-outcome timestamp for service-level deadlines.\nRetained outcomes lacking the flag stay unknown; lifecycle diagnostics do not\nsupply physical authority. Independent trace/log outages cannot delay receipt\nacknowledgement or guest lifetime. Native control-plus-collector outage execution\nremains required to verify this coordinated version-4 rollout."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3729
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A record stays decodable after its image kind leaves the protocol's file sets\n(`template-record-outlives-image-kind`). Identities are validated as 2-4 closed\nartifact names that include `manifest.json`, and the reader, not the record,\nrefuses a kind this release cannot verify, which quarantines the template so\nit can retire. Retirement destroys a template only when every file in its root\nis one of the record's own sealed identities (or that file's `.pending` copy)\n(`template-destroy-known-files`). On 2026-10-16 one FreeBSD template sealed\nbefore the kernel `rootfs.ufs` kind was removed failed to decode. Because\nreading the catalog halts on the first undecodable record, every template\nread, build and sweep on both hosts reported `template_unconfirmed` from about\n14:00 until this rule shipped. A property test covers the name rule and a unit\ntest covers the retired kind; no native retirement of a retired kind is\nrecorded yet."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3733
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A remembered browser credential creates a new ordinary login. Passkey verification\nis requested when the user starts a privileged mutation or passkey change; a\nremembered login alone does not provide that step-up or fresh-email authority for\nfirst enrollment. **Sign out everywhere** revokes both ordinary and remembered\nbrowser credentials. Exchange and revocation serialize on the account row,\nincluding across replicas. Fresh email sign-in and separate SSH/service\ncredentials remain independent. Ordinary sign-out revokes the supplied browser\nand remembered cookies even if the browser login has expired."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3737
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A repeated Start advances logical generation even when the confined VMM remains\nrunning. Lilly adopts the boot journal into that generation only under placement\nownership, after proving the existing native process. The same placement and\nexecution epoch retain their boot challenge; missing, corrupt, foreign or\nregressing journals refuse adoption. Current-generation trust and readiness\nremain required before acknowledgement. The\n[journal properties](repo:lilly/test/vm/boot_generation_adoption_property_test.exs)\npassed isolated generated histories. The repaired clone fleet passed repeated\npublic Start with unchanged native process identity and guest inheritance. The\ncomplete native clone matrix also passed. Native foreign/corrupt ownership\nrefusal remains outside this recorded scope."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3745
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A running VM in steady state is not reconciled every second (ADR-0176). An actor\ncounts as steady when all of the following hold:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3749
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A running boot without a readiness journal for its current generation, such as\none whose logical generation advanced, acquires one only under the placement lock\nafter the readiness claim confirms matching physical confinement and the exact\ncurrent boot record; the new journal supersedes the previous generation's record\n([provisioning journal](repo:lilly/lib/lilly/vm/provisioning.ex)). Cached ready\nrecords still require confinement; unfinished records retain their original\ndeadline. The original readiness rollout verified 20 retained Overalls boots and five Dungarees boots\nthrough passive boot-bound polls before declaring activation. Both hosts then\nactivated `c98a375c` healthy; passive post-switch checks verified ready journals\nfor 17 Overalls and five Dungarees current retained boots. Three deletions preceded\nactivation. Historical terminal measurements retained the same digest. This does not\nauthorize resetting expired progress or rewriting finished SLO failures."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3753
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A running guest receives a bounded boot-specific sync request. No freeze occurs;\nan unconfirmed flush is recorded and capture can continue. Guest snapshots are\ncrash-consistent, not a transaction across applications and hosts. Stopped guests\nare not started for backup."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3757
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A sealed file's recorded identity includes its device number, but Linux ZFS\ngives a dataset a new device number each time it is mounted (a throwaway pool\nshowed 202 then 203 for the same inode with another mount in between). Every\ntemplate was therefore quarantined after any remount, including each host\nreboot and the root-era handover, and the next create on that image failed\n`template_quarantined` before a rebuild. `Image.same_files?/2` now ignores\nthe device number only (`sealed-identity-survives-remount`); inode, size,\nmode, owner, links and nanosecond times still match exactly, and the caller\nstill pins the filesystem by dataset GUID and mountpoint before and after. The\nzfs-guest handover subtest reproduced the quarantine before the fix and passed\nafter it; that run's last subtest, the Inception clone, still fails."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3761
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A separate configured control starts the real default-callback loopback daemon,\nchecks seven direct children, readiness, private status socket and an actual SSH\nbanner, then stops the application and removes its owned socket. This establishes\nlocal startup only. Certificate negotiation/client CA trust, account proof and\nauthorized Marshall management, production release/systemd startup, native\nisolation, concurrent file replacement and hosted faults remain separate.\nThe server's embedded-CA signature check does not choose which CA a client may\ntrust. Evidence: [boot properties](repo:porsche/test/host_certificate_boot_property_test.exs)\nand [execution review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-29-antithesis-property-audit.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3765
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A separate test-only FreeBSD probe passed one actual Firecracker pause,\nfull-snapshot and resume transport event. An idle socket with no peer lost its\nidentity authority with `ECONNRESET`; pending accept/read/write operations woke\nwith errors and fresh sockets refused until a new VM. The native review binds\nits distinct test image and transport token. This establishes that one live\nsocket-fencing history, without granting reset-race/DMA/physical safety,\nproduction-service or fleet acceptance. The separate exact OmniOS `45983bb7`\ndirect host-KVM reset receipt records its own pause/full-snapshot/resume event,\nthree API responses 204, current-CID revalidation and idle/read/accept errors\n131 plus write error 32. It does not inherit the FreeBSD test image's identity\nor establish repeated/racing reset or DMA safety.\nThe persisted `native-freebsd-reset` nested-KVM gate subsequently passed in\n232 seconds with exact test image/kernel/probe/firmware/VMM receipts and the\nsame observed socket outcomes. The API remains a private fixture Unix socket;\nno ordinary guest TCP or production control authority is added."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3769
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A sequential native control verifies three usable NixOS guests and exactly 15 GiB\nof disk ownership on one actual pool. An earlier rejected candidate incorrectly\nexpected another 4 GiB-root request to refuse. The corrected control passed:\nit read the actual 16 GiB quota, requested a valid 17 GiB root through the same\nauthorized action, and observed refusal without another VM or changed ownership.\nThe recorded `n99x4` native execution on `0cfa6302` passed all four subtests. It\nalso passed both guest distributions' certificate-gated readiness, completed\nwrong-boot refusal and retained-data restart. The control\ndoes not establish the historical concurrent capacity race or a three-guest cap.\nAll fleet variants retain the 16 GiB disks and matching enrollment descriptors\nintroduced by the unified SSH CA integration. Actual private-service enablement\nalso supplies its synthetic SSH CA signer regardless of the selected feature.\nSubsequent `aaba77ee` integration preserves its separately verified gateway\nstartup retry policy; the native and full application results retain their\n`0cfa6302` provenance. The seven evaluated variants are configuration evidence,\nnot seven rerun feature suites."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3773
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A source-bearing restart also requires its durable image's matching manifest4\nidentity and source2 capability, including when resize supplies its own command\nsnapshot. The stored VM source and retained immutable image determine the new\nsnapshot; a caller cannot select another image for a sourced start. A disabled current\ncapable image remains eligible; staged image availability is independent of\nretained boot custody."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3777
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A steady actor skips per-second ticks and reconciles on a 10-second backstop,\nwell inside the 30-second watched deadline. Before this, each running VM took\nthe placement, intent and network locks and ran `systemctl show` once a second.\nAny of these ends steadiness at once:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3781
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A team has exactly one owner. Only that owner can appoint or remove admins;\nadmins can manage ordinary members. Membership edits cannot replace or remove\nthe owner, including an edit issued by an admin. Ownership changes through a\nconsent offer to an existing member: acceptance demotes the old owner to admin\nand promotes the target in one transaction. Decline and cancellation preserve\nownership; terminal offers cannot be reused. Membership and transfer mutations\nread current membership, so an open page cannot retain its former owner or member\nauthority after transfer or removal.\nMembers may read and use team machines through guest SSH, personal terminals,\nprivate guest HTTP/WebSockets and profile-permitted agent operations, including\ntheir existing prompt-triggered wake. Team web use creates no per-machine grant;\ndirect web-only sharing still grants neither machine metadata nor terminal/SSH use. Owners and admins may also manage the machines. Ordinary start/stop/delete,\nresize, clone, relocation and per-machine sharing require management authority.\nTeam members do not acquire Customer access: only the immutable Customer owner\nmay use that machine. Removing membership removes subsequent shared-resource\nauthority; re-adding it requires a fresh decision."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3785
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A terminal waiting for viewer credit cannot retain the shared drain mutex and\nblock a distinct session. Generated [neighbor histories](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/terminald/src/neighbor_property_tests.rs)\nrequire observed live queue pressure, successive fresh executed output from\nanother PTY and further progress after explicit retirement of the stalled owner.\nOctal command encoding prevents echoed input from satisfying the output oracle.\nBoth clients remain open through exact survivor identity and permit checks.\nConfigured witnesses cover zero credit and default watermark minus one, exact\nand plus one, with generated pump-read boundaries. The refined native47-test/Clippy gate passed4.66seconds;\nits [execution review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-terminald-neighbor-pressure.md)\nrecords the exact local scope and preserves the narrower initial result. These local observations do not prove\ncontinuous pressure between checkpoints, complete byte fidelity, throughput or\nauthenticated vsock/VM/browser composition."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3789
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ACME account contact email."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3790
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0149 separates diagnostic delivery from business execution. Stopping a log\nexporter, trace spool, Collector or Grafana cannot stop guests, roll back a domain\ntransaction or make diagnostic acknowledgment a prerequisite for lifecycle progress.\nDiagnostic loss is explicit. A trace or log is supporting evidence, never proof that\na command committed or that a disconnected host is dead."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3794
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0151's one-way rename of retained `antithesis` profile values to `standard`\ncompleted on both hosts; the 2026-10-11 preflight found no retained\n`antithesis` value, and ADR-0159 removed the Lilly startup step. Lilly acquires\nits host lock and validates the command store's retained host/version binding\nbefore reading commands or starting actors. A missing lock, foreign store binding\nor unreadable or malformed stored command refuses startup through the normal\nlock-release path. Correct the binding or preserved source explicitly; never\nerase evidence to force startup. The\n[custody review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-profile-migration-custody.md)\nrecords the historical migration scope; ADR-0159 pruned the applied migration\nfile. ADR-0147's accepted fresh-install\nrationale remains intact; ADR-0151 is superseded by ADR-0159."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3798
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0154 keeps only changes whose effect was measured with the boot profile\n(1-vCPU fixture; seconds since guest kernel start):"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3802
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0159 removed Lilly's one-way retained-profile rewrite after it completed on\nboth hosts. Lilly reads stored commands only under its live host lock after\npersistent command-store host/version validation; a foreign store refuses\nstartup. No startup step rewrites private state, and the lifecycle envelope\nloader validates schema, not all native Dataset bindings."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3806
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0160 accepts fixed fifteen-minute administrator step-up and exact inline\naction continuation for enrolled administrators. No-key administrators visit\nSecurity for first enrollment, return, and explicitly retry. Marshall implements\nthe fifteen-minute receipt, enrolled-key modal and setup return path. Final\nmerged packaged checks passed ordinary access, no-key return/explicit retry,\ncontinuation/cancellation/focus restoration and colocated cross-replica revocation.\nNative timed expiry and machine-page recovery remain unexecuted. Older tests retain their prior\nfive-minute/redirect scope. Ordinary signed-in reads remain independent of privileged\nauthority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3810
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0160 requires privileged browser submissions to retain the exact pending\naction while inline passkey step-up completes. The continuation binds actor,\nstored login, resource, action and canonical arguments, expires after five minutes, and can be\nconsumed once. No workflow, catalog intent or audit row is admitted merely by\nopening the ceremony. Verification permits the same action to retry with fresh\nlogin, role, fifteen-minute receipt, Cedar and workflow-generation checks."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3814
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0178/0179 add no deployable service or listener. Marshall's Agents domain\nretains snapshots, checkpoints, planning interactions and managed skill receipts\nin its existing PostgreSQL authority. Ziggy's authenticated pull path executes\ncompaction requests through the same provider admission and accounting boundary.\nThe existing guest executor performs context reads and skill materialization\nthrough Audi's current VM binding and lease checks. Linux uses the existing\nguest updater; Windows receives compatible code through rebuilt images.\nEnablement requires compatible consumers and executors, not only an installed\ndatabase schema. Declared placement does not establish native or deployed\navailability; see the [context review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-agent-context-and-planning.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3818
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0178/0179 extend the accepted thread workflow with immutable context\npreparation, compaction and planning. Admission captures the selected mode,\nreasoning, directory, configuration owner and skill identities. Guest collection\nruns outside the database transaction; only complete digest-verified content\nunder the current executor fence becomes an attached snapshot. A changed source\nretries collection twice, then fails explicitly. Runtime and native evidence\nare tracked in the [context review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-agent-context-and-planning.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3822
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0179's managed-skill restoration coordinator is implemented in Marshall's\nexisting agent loop. It advances a bounded round-robin cursor across replacement\nmachine generations, suppresses duplicate active or terminal restoration\nreceipts, and emits catalogued failure outcomes before continuing the scan.\nFocused authority and restoration scheduling cases pass; native guest restart,\nphysical reopen and independent-host recovery remain separate acceptance gaps."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3826
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ADR-0185 sends Underclass provider traffic through a dedicated tailnet node and\nthe operator's exit node in Australia. The host's own tailnet node and every\nother service keep direct egress. These sensitive operations are root-only:\njoining the egress node (`sudo underclass-egress up`) and changing its exit\nnode."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3830
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
