#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5370
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Creating a terminal selects its pane once. Switching to another pane is retained\nacross session-list refreshes. A delayed ticket reply can attach only the currently\nselected pane while LiveView is connected; switching away cannot create a hidden\nattachment. Malformed replies preserve the pending request for retry. The\nnative browser journey also checks the settled selected pane owns the only open\nor connecting attachment; closing sockets do not count. A workspace keeps at most four\nlive emulators: the selected pane and the most recently selected others. A\npane builds its emulator only when first selected; a less recent pane without a\nsocket or pending writes disposes its emulator and, when selected again,\nreplays the retained window from cursor zero. Fifty shells on one page therefore\ncost four emulators, not fifty screen-reader trees. Panes use xterm's DOM\nrenderer: no pane holds a WebGL context, so a page cannot exhaust the browser's\ncontext limit and no renderer canvas can paint above the share or new-terminal\ndialogs. A pane without an open attachment reports `connecting`,\nnever a stale `live`, and the selected detached pane keeps requesting a ticket\nuntil it attaches: a request deferred by draining writes or a still-closing\nsocket retries instead of stranding the pane. Complete NixOS and Ubuntu\njourneys on base `3f679eff`, captured tree `e710ef1e`, passed these checks through\ngraphics, replay and whole-Marshall recovery. The terminal and boot log fill the responsive\nworkspace panel. The boot log is the guest's read-only serial console: both NixOS\nand Ubuntu guests disable the getty generator (`systemd.getty_auto=0`) and mask\nthe serial and console getty units, so no login prompt is offered there. The\nconsole carries the fixed line `lilly-guest boot complete`, then the bounded boot\nreport (ADR-0154), which ends with the fixed line `lilly-boot-report: end`; an\nautoscrolled pane therefore shows the report's end, not the boot-complete line.\nLilly reads the log as its service user: the root boot-log writer hands the\nprivate file to that uid. Reading that host file has its own authority path:\ncurrent Cedar VM read, a fresh authenticated protocol-2 host stream and the\nexact current runnable placement/execution. It does not require the image's\nguest terminal capability or a ready/running guest outcome. A pending or failed\nboot can expose retained diagnostics while guest session operations remain\ndisabled. Each response holds at most 16 KiB plus a cursor; it returns no guest\nsocket path or boot challenge and never dials the guest. A newer command or\nchanged boot/log incarnation refuses the read. Sparse serial output remains\nobservation, not proof of the failed guest stage.\nThe machine navigation has no Boot log tab. A machine whose image has no\nguest agents, whose only console is host diagnostics, links `Boot log` from\nits connection explanation; the link opens\n`/machines/:id/terminals?view=boot-log` directly, and that URL stays\navailable for every machine reader. This diagnostic view selects\nthe read-only serial pane and skips guest-session listing and automatic shell\ncreation, so pending and failed boots do not depend on a guest terminal.\nA local browser fixture passed desktop/mobile and both themes, grid, contrast,\norigin-only resources, repeated EOF polling, disconnect/reconnect and\nhide/destroy lifecycle. It exercises the actual browser view against a local\nWebSocket fixture; a complete NixOS journey through a pending/failed native\nguest and real Lilly log authority remains unrecorded. The\n[diagnostic review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-17-host-boot-log-authorization.md)\nkeeps those scopes separate.\nInteractive access\nremains the vsock terminal and Audi-fenced SSH. Personal opening requires compatible guest account helpers and\nterminald; activating a host release does not upgrade an existing guest disk.\nWindows guests with the `windows-v1` agent set run the same terminald source\nunder ConPTY with an identical wire protocol, so this terminal path is\nunchanged for them; their identity mapping and fidelity difference are\ndescribed under [Guests](#guests)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5374
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Creation admits an immutable image and shape, reserves capacity and an address,\ncreates durable placement intent, and dispatches provisioning. Lilly validates\nnative storage, builds owned drives, installs isolated networking, and starts the\njailed VMM. Readiness requires current physical evidence; job completion alone is\ninsufficient. That evidence is the running unit, a single VMM in its cgroup with\nthe expected UID/GID, no capabilities, no-new-privileges and seccomp, the same\njail root, network namespace and executable, the configured CPU, memory and swap\nlimits, and a running API answer. When a check fails Lilly logs\n`vm.observation_failed` with the placement and the first failing check's closed\natom (`unit_not_running`, `cgroup_unreadable`, `vmm_process_ambiguous`,\n`identity_mismatch`, `limits_mismatch`, `api_unavailable` or\n`unit_stopped_during_check`), once per change of failing check and never with\npaths, PIDs or command output; the guest stays unconfirmed. Guest trust delivery\nand its receipt recheck name the first unconfirmed step the same way: the\nexecutor retries with a closed `trust_*` reason on `vm.execution_deferred`\n(`trust_lock_unavailable`, `trust_claim_mismatch`, `trust_boot_unconfirmed`,\n`trust_boot_changed`, `trust_vmm_unconfirmed`, `trust_provisioning_unconfirmed`,\n`trust_journal_unreadable`, `trust_state_unwritten`, `trust_attempts_invalid` or\n`trust_delivery_failed`). Every stage retries; only three failed deliveries for\none boot fail the execution. Like the other readiness steps, trust waits up to\ntwo seconds in lilly-hostd's FIFO queue for the placement and journal leases, so\nthe actor's per-tick boot probe and outcome journal writes cannot starve it: a\nsingle-attempt trust lock once left every guest adopted after a Lilly restart\ndeferred indefinitely."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5378
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Creation, start, stop, deletion, relocation and request controls wait up to 120\nseconds for the matching physical result. A request being accepted or acknowledged is not proof that the machine\nhas reached the requested state. `--no-wait` returns the accepted request without\nwaiting; `--timeout SECONDS` changes the wait limit. Timing out or disconnecting\ndoes not cancel accepted work."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5382
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Credential preparation services required before startup."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5383
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Credentials are managed from the sign-in and SSH key sections of the account\npage at `/settings` (ADR-0196), one LiveView behind the security mount and the\nauthentication privacy tag. A first\npasskey enrollment requires an email link completed within the previous five\nminutes; every further registration, rename or removal requires a fresh passkey\nverification. Accounts admit up to ten passkeys, up to ten retained\nsingle-use sixty-second ceremony challenges, and up to one hundred SSH keys;\nchallenge history prunes on expiry. Renaming a passkey changes only its\ndisplayed label: the credential identity and key material never change, and\nlabels are never visible to other accounts or administrative projections.\nRemoving a passkey other than the one the current elevation proved against\nkeeps MFA and elevation; removing exactly the proving credential ends the\nsession's elevation and completed MFA until another retained passkey verifies\nin the same login. An ordinary account removing its last passkey disables MFA,\ninvalidates the credential generation and revokes every stored session,\nincluding the one making the change. Administrators cannot remove their last\npasskey. Passkey registration, verification, removal and administrative reset\nenter the append-only account audit; the elevation-gated label rename changes\nno authority and is deliberately not audited. Undersized or malformed ceremony\npayloads consume their single-use challenge without credential or MFA changes,\nand failed ceremonies never activate MFA.\nThe [credential management review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-credential-management-histories.md)\nrecords generated real-ceremony histories against an independent raw-SQL\noracle, exact refusal bounds and session effects, and committed-inventory\nrestoration of unrelated fixtures."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5387
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Current manage and provisioning authority are checked at submission and again at\nactivation. Manager withdrawal blocks queued copying and further user actions.\nAlready admitted restoration and cleanup continue after withdrawal. A cancelled\nrequest key retains its original destination identity and cannot be retried into\na new copy; a new intentional request requires a new key."
msgstr ""

