#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8312
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Source2 and manifest4 are a coordinated application/image cutover under\n[ADR-0157](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0157-multi-provider-authenticated-repository-launch.md).\nThe one-time database migration converted stored GitHub source rows into current\nprovider/revision fields and disabled obsolete image catalog entries. It did not\nrewrite immutable execution snapshots, guest source records or Lilly journals.\nADR-0159 pruned those applied data statements; a fresh installation creates the\nprovider/revision columns directly.\nSelect newly built source-capable guest images and matching applications together.\nObsolete source-bearing retained guests require recreation for current source\ndelivery. The migration has no automatic down path; rollback needs the pre-cutover\ndatabase backup and matching revision. This does not establish safe historical\nrestore alongside newer retained guest disks: permanent guest UUID/UID reservations\nand the sequence also require reconciliation under\n[ADR-0158](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0158-durable-guest-identities.md).\nSource eligibility for an unsourced retained start does not waive generated-identity\nimage/recreation requirements. No production cutover is recorded here."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8316
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Source2 restart eligibility comes from the durable image manifest4 identity and\nexplicit source2 capability. An unavailable capability cannot roll back an\naccepted crash, confirmed resized sizes or the next fresh host report. Recovery\nrefuses the restart and releases only confirmed stopped compute, retaining disk\nand address. A confirmed resize completes with a fixed\n`workspace_source_unavailable` failure so ordinary cleanup remains possible.\nExisting command snapshots, boot bundles and journals remain immutable; obsolete\nsourced guests require recreation on a current image. See [lifecycle](#lifecycle)\nand the [source2 review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-launcher.md)\nfor component and native evidence boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8320
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Stable per-host random delay in seconds; spreads database and S3 load."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8324
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Stable private peer IP (not its public round-robin hostname)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8328
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Stale native capacity samples pause cache writes without evicting completed\nobjects or stopping their readers when independent current boot and local mount\nchecks confirm unchanged custody. Stale samples cannot initialize a cache or\nauthorize writes. Invalid or changed mount custody stops the optional cache\nsubtree. Fresh observations resume writes. Only headroom below the 15 percent\nfloor (or unsafe guest-parent headroom) evicts objects. A late observation, or\nthe 15-20 percent band while writes are paused, only pauses writes and keeps every\ncomplete object (ADR-0140). The pressure publisher has a bounded\none-CPU budget; the three-second freshness deadline remains unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8332
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Standard guests include development and diagnostic tools, Podman with Docker\ncompatibility, and the guest agent executor. Network policy still applies to APT,\ncontainers, tools, and nested workloads. The private CLI update path uses a seed\nsupervisor, boot checks and periodic checks; it obtains Marshall-authorized\nartifacts rather than accepting arbitrary download locations. An activated CLI\nthat fails to start or does not register within ninety seconds is replaced by\nits saved predecessor. Both normal handoff and boot after interrupted activation\nuse this recovery path. Current registration still requires a new Marshall\nexecutor fence before tool work. A registered but semantically faulty executable\nhas no automatic rollback."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8336
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Stop retains the disk. `rm MACHINE` deletes the machine and its disk. A name is\navailable for reuse only after cleanup is confirmed. Use `exit` to leave the prompt."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8344
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Stopping preserves drives and machine identity. A later start requires admission\nagainst current host capacity. Lilly's per-VM actors and independent launchers\nallow guests to survive the main Lilly process restarting. Crash recovery is\nbounded and suppressed during maintenance. A crash or resize restart that finds\nthe host's capacity report merely late keeps its compute hold, and the next\naccepted report decides the restart. A late report is not a capacity shortfall\n(ADR-0140). Repeated crashes should be diagnosed,\nnot hidden by unlimited restart loops."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8348
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Store refuses ingestion when free disk falls below this many bytes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8356
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Submit workflows through authorized machine controls or the workflow’s supported\nPorsche command. Resize uses the browser controls. Keep the returned operation identity. Retry an uncertain submission with its original\nidempotency key. Inspect both workflow progress and cleanup obligations."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8360
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Supplementary Unix groups."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8361
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Supply `hostId`, `controlPlane`, `credentialFile`, `egressInterface`, enrolled\n`pools`, and installed image manifests. `hostId` and pool GUIDs come from enrollment;\nthey are not generated during service activation. Configure guest CPU/RAM budgets,\nhost overheads, protected networks, ingress, private services, and public trust\nroots. Keep the transfer listener separate from administrative SSH."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8377
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Systemd owns personal workers and mode-0600 sockets independently of the root\nbroker. Broker restart preserves them and saved personal data only when the\nroot-owned launch digest still matches UID/name/protocol/commit/release/origin.\nChanged legitimate identity requires successful stop before replacement;\nunanswered metadata and foreign socket custody refuse without stopping it.\nIf the worker exits,\nthe next freshly authorized broker use starts a new personal process without\ndeleting home or extension data. Upstream extension-host activity, its five-minute\nidle timer and 1500-second reconnection grace govern automatic shutdown, rather\nthan an exact timestamp of the last raw relay close."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8381
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "TCP 4444 is reserved for the editor. Ordinary primary/alternate web and peer\nconnectors refuse it, and PostgreSQL refuses any endpoint row that names it. Only the dedicated Lilly\neditor packet mark admits a connection, using the profile's derived gateway source.\nThe guest broker additionally binds the exact boot IPv4 and eth0 and checks source,\ntyped preface, account and immutable release before starting the worker. Guest\nheaders cannot replace this identity. Native packet-mark and full Firecracker\nacceptance remain separate from the local protocol/network properties in\n[scenario coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8385
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "TCP ports reachable from any source at this address."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8386
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tailnet HTTPS port reserved for Underclass on this host."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8387
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tailnet exit node (MagicDNS name or Tailscale IP) that all Underclass upstream\ntraffic leaves through. Null keeps direct upstream egress. When set, Underclass\nreaches only loopback and refuses to start until the egress node is logged in\nand has selected this exit node."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8391
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Team creation reserves an active name and creates one owner membership. Reusing\na name succeeds only for the original creator while they still own that team.\nAnother account, a former owner, or a different current owner receives a creation\nerror without a new membership. Choose an available name to create a separate\nteam. Archived names may acquire a new team identity; retained history is preserved.\nSSH retries with the same request ID return their pinned result, including after\nname reuse; the team domain's key alone is not a durable request receipt.\nThe [creation verification](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-team-creation-verification.md)\nrecords generated histories, committed three-node collisions and native browser\nrefusals with ordinary accounts."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8395
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Team-owned integrations are automatically available to eligible machines whose\nowners currently belong to the team, including personally owned machines without\na team tag. Removal refuses subsequent private HTTP authorization; no explicit\nattachment is created by this automatic path. Customer private-service restrictions\nstill apply. Only the team owner/admin may configure the integration."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8399
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Teams SSH commands report a name or transfer-state conflict with exit 2; choose\nan available name or a valid current transition before issuing a new request.\nMalformed names and missing references also exit 2. Names contain 1–63 lowercase\nASCII letters, digits or internal hyphens, start with a letter and end with a\nletter or digit. Missing membership or foreign authority exits 3. These permanent\nrefusals leave team, audit and request rows unchanged. Unexpected storage or\napplication failures remain opaque and retryable (exit 5).\nThe same accepted request ID keeps its historical result after ownership transfer,\nremoval or name reuse; it does not grant current access. A changed semantic input\nwith that ID conflicts. The [SSH verification](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-team-ssh-verification.md)\ndistinguishes generated HTTP checks and packaged CLI metadata execution; the\nresource review above records the subsequent native shared-resource journey."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8403
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Template recovery resumes published staging, verification and sealing intents\nunder the exclusive image lease. A create interrupted before private mountpoint\npermissions are set may repair those permissions only after matching native\ndataset shape, owner nonce and any recorded GUID. Retirement confirms absence\nbefore writing the deleted tombstone. Quarantine replacement creates a fresh\nincarnation after confirmed retirement and rehashes source bytes. Foreign GUIDs,\nowner tags, snapshots or unexpected files are refused without recursive cleanup.\nTemplate state records survive release changes. Lifecycle journals accept only\nthe state-only v2 envelope; corrupt or other-version primary state fails closed\nwithout rewriting, and diagnostics cannot gate actors or outcome ACKs."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8407
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Template-backed image verification relies on trusted host ownership, a local\ncreation nonce, exact dataset GUID, a read-only ZFS seal, native reader exclusion\nand nanosecond artifact identities. File timestamps alone do not prove arbitrary\nprivileged tampering absent. Warm use refuses changed seals and identities; it\ndoes not trust a path or image name alone. The Rust helper has no setuid bit or\ncapabilities and receives authority only from Lilly's existing native lock owner.\nLilly accepts its launch guard and boot-log writer only as separate trusted local\nexecutables. Their Nix release links may span multiple packages; executable and\nancestor-directory links must be root-owned and bounded, and the final executable\nmust have trusted ownership, ancestry and mode. Missing or excessive links\nrefuse startup. The boot-log writer runs as root, because it forks the guarded\njailer chain, and opens `boot.log` without following a final symlink. It writes\nonly a single-link 0600 regular file owned by itself or the configured reader.\nWith an unprivileged Lilly, hostd passes the service uid as `--reader-uid` and\nthe writer changes the file's owner to it through the open descriptor, so only\nLilly can read console bytes and a planted link or foreign file is refused\n(exit 74) before any console byte is written to it.\nBrowser boot-log access separately rechecks Cedar VM read permission, current\nplacement/execution and fresh authenticated host-stream authority. Lilly accepts\nonly its assigned stream caller, validates the newest runnable command and\nbinds the private file to the retained boot/launch incarnation before returning\nat most 16 KiB. Pending/failed guest bootstrap does not grant input or guest\nterminal authority; image terminal capability remains independently enforced.\nResponses contain no jailed guest socket path or boot challenge, and console\nbytes enter no diagnostic event or database payload. Changed, missing or foreign\nidentity refuses rather than opening another path.\nTemplate diagnostics leave the native postcommit boundary through the independent\nobservability plane. Validated host/pool/record identities support correlation;\nno trace, timestamp or historical event can create a placement, prove current\nreadiness or release capacity commitments."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8411
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tempo trace retention in days, subject to its monitored storage quota."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8415
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Terminal opening progresses from pending to ready, or through cancellation to\nfailure. Session termination separately records pending, confirmed, or retired\nkill authority. A disconnected socket is not proof that the PTY exited. Geometry\nchanges require a writable acknowledged attachment. Only the session creator with\ncurrent VM write access can attach writable; explicitly shared operators remain\nread-only. Tickets bind one actor, session and issued mode for thirty seconds and\nare consumed once. Input and resize recheck stored login and current session,\nCedar and execution authority before dispatch. Idle and output-only attachments\nrecheck every second; revocation does not promise instantaneous output cutoff.\nA committed revocation also emits a control hint (ADR-0141) through\nPostgreSQL to every Marshall node. Examples are a grant or team change, a\ntoken, passkey, MFA or administrator change, a terminal session share or kill,\nor a machine transition. Each attached viewer subscribed to its user or\nmachine rechecks at once. The one-second poll remains the backstop for a lost\nhint, and a notifier reconnect makes every viewer recheck.\nA failed recheck closes the attachment at once while the database answers. If\nthe recheck fails because this Marshall cannot reach the database (a `SELECT 1`\nprobe also fails), it is inconclusive (ADR-0140): the last confirmed authority\nholds for at most 30 seconds, then the attachment closes as\n`authority_unconfirmed`. Revocation latency is therefore up to 30 seconds only\nwhile that node has lost its database.\nThe terminal page applies the same rule: while the database does not answer,\nit never reports the machine as missing, and a server error page retries\nitself every three seconds until the database returns. A reconnecting page\nwaits up to 40 seconds for the database before mounting (the browser's join\ntimeout is 60 seconds), so it keeps its open terminals instead of reloading.\nA login recheck that fails only because the database does not answer neither\nsigns the user out nor leaves the page; the triggering action is dropped or\nretried a second later. Login and terminal-session lookups distinguish a\ndefinite answer (missing, revoked, expired or foreign) from a lookup that\nfailed: only the former refuses, so a pool timeout on an otherwise reachable\ndatabase is still inconclusive.\nThe browser bounds only the WebSocket handshake of a terminal attachment;\nafter the socket opens, the server's own five-second attach limit decides,\nso a slow page that has not yet handled the attached frame keeps its socket\ninstead of restarting scrollback replay. Attached terminal sockets also\noutlive LiveView disconnects: a reconnecting page only pauses ticket requests,\nbecause the server authorizes and rechecks each attachment on its own.\nRestored authority permits an explicit fresh attachment and cannot revive a closed\nsocket. Pending kill or a changed execution invalidates the old registry session.\nThe [attachment-history review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-terminal-attachment-verification.md)\nrecords generated real WebSock processes against SQL-backed authority and synthetic\nrelay dispatch; the native guest results below retain their original scope.\nGenerated [terminald byte histories](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/terminald/src/property_tests.rs)\ncheck exact raw scrollback retention through append, resize and cursor replay,\nincluding default 1 MiB and maximum 8 MiB rings. Growing capacity cannot recover\npreviously discarded bytes. Stream histories preserve frame order across split\nand coalesced input; partial headers remain incomplete and complete invalid or\noversized headers refuse before payload arrival. The sandboxed native gate passed\nClippy and 43 tests, including five properties. These pure checks do not prove\nvsock authentication, PTY backpressure or the shared 64 MiB session budget; see the\n[recorded scope](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-terminald-byte-histories.md)."
msgstr ""

