#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9135
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Antithesis [local QEMU TCG boot record](repo:antithesis/verification/guest-boot.md)\nuses the production NixOS guest services in a q35-adapted image. Exact port\n1024 readiness succeeded at epochs 3, 4 and 5; epoch 4 reused the same qcow2\noverlay. After stopping the guest, an offline read found retained\n`/etc/machine-id` bytes matching the first ready boot, and `qemu-img check`\nfound no errors. The initial image had reached multi-user and port 1026 flush\nbut missed readiness because q35 named its NIC `enp0s3`; `net.ifnames=0`\nrepaired that fixture mismatch. The runner stopped its owned QEMU/backend\nprocesses and removed its TAP. This local software-emulator result does not\nexercise hosted Lilly's unavailable guest executor, workspace delivery,\nFirecracker/KVM, ZFS or physical host isolation."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9139
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Control room's Lilly node page provides an advisory destination preview.\nPreview records no campaign, reservation or job. Starting evacuation requires a\ncurrent administrator with a stored login and recent passkey elevation. It fixes\nthe current workload/placement/epoch cohort and excludes new admissions to that\nhost while keeping existing serving enabled. Maintenance drain instead stops\nguests in place; it retains their disks on the host."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9143
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Firecracker growth fixtures compare exact EFI directory/file inventories\ninstead of the whole FAT partition hash: pinned CloudHv creates the root regular\n`/NvVars` firmware store on first boot. Only that exact regular file, bounded to\n1 MiB, may differ. Boot/configuration file paths, sizes and bytes remain exact;\nunrelated entries or an `NvVars` directory refuse. Other protected partitions\nretain whole-partition digests, and GPT identities/geometry remain exact. The\nrecorded retained FreeBSD ESP comparison and 21 host boundary tests establish\nthis oracle's scope; the separate 334-second machine result above establishes\nthe stated native growth history."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9151
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Lilly events also carry the internal `stage` atom of the check that\nrefused (for example `zfs_snapshot_source_unconfirmed` beside the wire reason\n`source_unconfirmed`), and snapshot cleanup passes that closed atom through\ninstead of folding every refusal into one. The first reasoned production\ndiagnostic showed why that prune never confirmed: the source had been\nrestarted after capture with a start command carrying a repository workspace\nsource, and the claim comparison pinned the full spec struct against the claim\nits stopped predecessor journaled without one. Claim, snapshot-source and\nadoption checks now compare the spec's persisted storage shape\n(`Lilly.VM.Spec.storage/1`), which never includes the guest-delivery source."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9155
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Machines page and the Overview order each user's pinned machines first\n([ADR-0193](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0193-per-user-machine-pins.md)).\nThe pinned section is a separate bounded read (at most twenty rows, newest pin\nfirst) that applies the same per-row Cedar read decision and projection as the\nkeyset page; the page then excludes those ids, so a machine appears once and\npaging neither repeats nor skips. The pinned section is shown on the first page\nonly. The Overview fills its six preview slots with pinned machines first; each\npreview lists vCPU, memory, disk size and whether the machine is personal or shared."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9159
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Overalls operator chooses whether these immutable archives are available. If\nthe deployment has disabled its browser extension package, the download links return an unavailable response.\nSee the browser distribution notes for package and build details.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9167
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Rust `lilly-image-copy` helper attempts whole-file `FICLONE`, then kernel\ncopy offload (`copy_file_range`), then sparse userspace copying (ADR-0143). Both\ncopies transfer only the source's data extents (`lseek` `SEEK_DATA`/`SEEK_HOLE`)\nand size the destination afterwards, so holes stay holes: the 64 GiB Windows\ndisk with about 22 GB of data moves 22 GB (ADR-0173); a filesystem that cannot\nreport holes is one data extent, as before. Each copy and flush runs within the\npool's image I/O deadline (`services.lilly.imageIoTimeoutSeconds`, default\n300 s) and fails closed when it expires; only the nested Windows test raises\nit, because its shared store reports no holes. Only `image_copy` and `sync` may run\nlonger than the 600 s every other host utility is held to. They run through\nthe placement's own lock owner, under a shared lease on the image template,\nso a long copy blocks only that placement and exclusive work on that\ntemplate. The pool lock covers only the claim and the final readiness step.\nIn production, burst latency comes from preparation that the pool lock\nserializes, not from copies (ADR-0176). Each\nfallback happens only for recognized unsupported errors raised before any byte\nmoves; a partial offload, short read or repeated interruption fails without\npublishing. Permission, space and I/O failures remain failures. It refuses\noverwrite and symlink sources/destinations, checks source identity, and flushes\nthe independent destination. VM datasets and boot bundles remain usable after\ntemplate retirement. `FICLONE` refuses sources on another mount, and production\nimages live under the separately mounted `/nix/store` of the same pool, so a\nmeasured 2026-10-05 create used `copy` for 25.4 of 33.8 seconds. Offload lets\nZFS share blocks across datasets. The native image-copy test reproduces the\nbind-mounted shape and records `offload` with all 8 MiB counted in the pool's\n`bcloneused`. `offload` alone does not prove sharing, because the kernel may\ncopy, and an enabled block-cloning feature is not evidence either."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9171
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The SSH CA change requires the certificate database migrations and updated\nMarshall, gateways, client and new guest images as one coordinated development\nrelease. Deliver the encrypted signer before activating Marshall. Gateway startup\nobtains a certificate with its own service credential. Missing initial authority\nkeeps the listener closed and retries every five seconds without exhausting a\nstart limit. On Overalls, gateway activation also starts the fleet API relay socket;\nremote gateways recover independently when Marshall returns. Daily renewal jobs\nreceive their own systemd credentials. Confirm the public policy, gateway readiness and a\nfresh installer session on both guest distributions. Old writable disks are not\nupgraded by this release."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9175
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Standard spelling is shared by the protocol, CLI and empty-database schema.\nIts address and permission algorithms are unchanged. This revision targets fresh\ninstallations; existing databases and Lilly journals are not migrated and mixed\nold/new releases are unsupported. Independent deployment planning is required."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9179
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The TCP port Audiobookshelf will listen on."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9180
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Ubuntu diagnostic capture reached a guest shell but clone status remained\n`waiting`: provisioning-readiness and Inception success paths had bypassed the\nsupervised source handoff. The correction routes them through the existing\nhandoff while retaining their exact running/readiness observation. Component and\ncurrent native rerun results are recorded separately in the\n[review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-launcher.md); the failed\ncapture does not establish provider checkout.\nDiagnostics carry only VM, placement and reason identifiers; URLs and refs are\nnever logged. Advertised-ref capture is bounded to thirty seconds and 1 MiB;\ntimeout/overflow cancels its owned git helpers, and malformed listings refuse\nwithout falling back to a commit. Checkout uses the advertised/full-object SHA1\nor SHA256 format; mixed listings refuse, and abbreviated objects negotiate the\nformat through a clone. The source2 identity acquires no object-format field.\nAny web page can navigate a signed-in user to a launch link; the\nresult is at most one default OSS machine per fresh visit, owned by that user.\nCurrent images use manifest4 with `workspace_source_version: 2` only for a boot-enabled\nreceiver, otherwise null. Installed binaries alone do not grant capability. Obsolete\nimages are disabled for new creation; source-v1 receiver records and execution\nsnapshots are never rewritten or decoded as source2. Recreate sourced machines\nrequiring the current service. Retained restart/adoption keeps its portable boot\nbundle and does not recreate images. A new sourced start command still validates\nthe durable image record's identity/capability before committing or dispatching;\nan obsolete receiver refuses and requires recreation. A disabled current capable\nimage remains usable for retained starts; unsourced starts do not acquire this\ncapability requirement. The earlier corrected Ubuntu capture passed cross-tab sign-in/MFA,\nfour exact initial GitHub checkouts and retained stop/start without re-clone.\nTen additional GitHub/GitLab/Codeberg cases passed before Codeberg change remained\n`cloning` and the run failed. Bitbucket, public downloads and CLI were not reached;\nhistorical GitHub results retain their earlier revision in\n[scenario coverage](#coverage).\nThe Codeberg change timeout was reproduced with exact dnsmasq 2.93 and the\nproduction-code musl API client: the fixture returned AAAA REFUSED alongside its\nsuccessful A answer. Declaring the fixed provider domains authoritative locally\nreturns an empty NOERROR AAAA answer and repairs the focused API probe without\nchanging guest trust or production resolution. The native fixture now checks\nfixed provider TLS, API shape and fork transport before the unchanged guest-owned\ndone/HEAD assertions. Only fixed stages, indices, exit codes and clone-status\nvalues leave those diagnostics. The repaired NixOS native family passed all twenty\nprovider/revision combinations, cross-tab sign-in/required MFA, retained restart,\npublic downloads and packaged CLI, with all twelve subtests successful. Exact\nguest-owned done/HEAD/shallow assertions and Codeberg/Bitbucket API probes passed\nunder installed guest trust. The latest frozen-source Ubuntu run passed browser\nsign-in/MFA/deduplication but failed initial guest startup before any checkout;\nits runtime cause remains under diagnosis and complete family acceptance is\nunresolved. Local\nprovider fixtures do not establish live-provider or production acceptance.\nThese incoming frozen browser-feature captures precede ADR-0158 integration.\nThey do not establish the latest combined source2/generated-identity native\nsource; its component and lifecycle validation is recorded separately."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9184
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Ubuntu receiver skips `update-ca-certificates` only when all of these hold:\n- the installed marker equals the delivered digest;\n- the managed `overalls-platform/overalls-I.crt` files are byte-equal to the\n  delivered roots, in order;\n- the system bundle carries every root;\n- every `/etc/ssl/certs/overalls-I.pem` link resolves to its managed file."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9200
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Windows guest spike ([ADR-0169](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0169-firmware-booted-windows-guests.md))\nadds build-time requirements without touching fleet activation. The builder\nneeds the `kvm` system feature (`requiredSystemFeatures = [\"kvm\"]`, the same\nmechanism `pkgs.vmTools` and the NixOS test framework use) because Windows is\ninstalled under QEMU inside the sandbox, and it downloads the roughly 6 GiB\nWindows Server 2025 evaluation ISO as a fixed-output fetch pinned in\n[iso.json](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/windows/iso.json). Installation takes 20 to\n40 minutes and is not reproducible, so rebuilds happen only when an input pin\nchanges. Production use needs licensed media swapped into the pin file; the\nevaluation licence covers 180 days of guest uptime."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9204
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [2026-09-25 production acceptance](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-production-clone-relocation.md)\npassed creation and guest access but encountered this retained-slot condition.\nThose attempts did not establish clone independence or physical movement. The\nnext-day recovery review records supported recovery of a fresh clone, independent\ncontents and both relocation directions. The original older clone disappeared\nexternally before recovery; acceptance of the additional readiness fix remains\nseparate from that historical evidence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9208
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [2026-10-02 review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-02-https-egress-production.md)\nrecords passing one-host production acceptance and native HTTPS full/range cache\nrecovery after proxy restart with the origin stopped. Ordinary system roots\nverified the production signer on both guest distributions. A first Ubuntu\nprovisioning request failed on an incoherent ZFS observation and recovered through\na separate start; its failure remains recorded. These results do not establish\nindependent-host relocation, universal provisioning recovery or every HTTP input.\nA fresh three-guest campaign on healthy `99f13b26` also passed the complete\nHTTPS/cache/refusal matrix after the namespace-cleanup repair. Earlier unexplained\nSSH closures remain failed evidence; a later passing campaign does not close\nthat transport-stability gap."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9212
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [2026-10-13 reset review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-reset.md)\nrecords removal of the old `antithesis/` tree before a fresh whole-repository\nrebuild. Historical harness, research and local mutation artifacts remain linked\nat the full captured baseline revision. Their recorded execution and limits are\npreserved; they are not current setup instructions or fresh hosted acceptance.\nLilly's existing generated actor and fake-command properties retain their test\npaths and assertion names, using an ordinary test-support fixture. The scenario\nledger therefore retains its bounded property evidence and native gaps.\nFresh research, setup, workload execution, hosted baselines and mutation results\nmust acquire their own source/build identities and execution records. Emulated\nguest journeys, hosted user-space services and native Firecracker/ZFS checks\nretain separate verification scopes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9216
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [CLI verification review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-agent-cli-verification.md)\ndistinguishes generated HTTP/LiveView histories, existing synthetic HTTPS CLI\ncoverage and the dedicated ordinary-browser/packaged-CLI/guest journey. The\ncaptured native journey passed explicit consent, exact guest tool completion,\nStandard capability refusal and stale approval after sharing withdrawal.\nAn earlier executor interruption remains unexplained; the passing capture\npredates the later Underclass package pin. Deterministic fixture model responses\ndo not establish live-provider availability or production capacity."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9220
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [Control room verification](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-control-room-verification.md)\nadds generated role/elevation/session histories and a packaged browser journey.\nThe native journey uses verified TLS and a virtual WebAuthn authenticator; it\nchecks reads, one audited role-change path, demotion, revocation and expiry.\nExecution status is recorded in [scenario coverage](#coverage). One replica does\nnot establish cross-replica revocation propagation or physical authenticator behavior."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9224
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [FreeBSD transport machine gate](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/native-transport/default.nix)\nnow exists: it boots the source-built candidate under patched Firecracker in\nnested KVM, checks the executing build token and CID2/3, rejects oversized\nlength/disconnected histories, reconnects and compares nine payload sizes from\nzero through 1 MiB across the credit-window boundary. It records exact disk,\nfirmware and VMM digests while keeping admission false. The newer recorded\n139-second execution passed token/CID, native ABI and immutable buffer/capacity\nrefusals. The final ordinary catalog gate subsequently passed in 246 seconds\nwith exact image `19658a4a`, token, firmware and VMM receipts in the review.\nIt does not test the complete native\nservices, wrong-CID injection, device reset, physical isolation or recovery.\nA separate test-only FreeBSD image passed one actual Firecracker pause,\nfull-snapshot and resume transport event: idle socket identity refused with\n`ECONNRESET`, pending accept/read/write calls woke with errors and fresh sockets\nrefused until a new VM. Exact test image/token/firmware/VMM evidence is in the\nnative review. This is separate from the final service image and does not\nestablish reset-race stress, DMA reclamation, physical or OmniOS behavior.\nThe persisted `native-freebsd-reset` nested-KVM gate subsequently passed in\n232 seconds with the same socket outcomes and exact image/kernel/probe/firmware/\nVMM receipts in the review. Both native test probes cross-compile; only FreeBSD\nhas recorded event execution. Its private fixture Unix API adds no production\ncontrol or ordinary guest TCP surface."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9228
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [activation review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-17-native-image-admission.md)\nrecords the sealed artifact and publication boundary. Current rebuilt native\npayloads do not inherit admission from that image. Missing, foreign, replayed or\nincomplete promotion evidence refuses; evidence and debugging hooks stay outside\nthe published image. The candidate's original false admission flags are retained."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9232
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [artifact review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-browser-type-gates.md)\nand the [sweep review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-browser-typing-sweep.md)\nrecord checker execution separately from historical native runs. Those runs keep\ntheir original source and assertions; this build gate creates no new property,\nphysical isolation or browser-runtime evidence. Commands for building the\nartifacts without machines are in the [test catalog](repo:nix/verification/tests/README.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9236
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [browser launcher review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-launcher.md)\nrecords Weaver source comparison, current source2/manifest4 and authentication\nresume evidence. The replacement minimized immutable browser package/check passed in 10 seconds,\nincluding strict typing, all ten Node tests, deterministic archives, Mozilla lint\nand real Chromium four-provider actions with query/hash sentinels removed and\nunsupported-click refusal when its URL was initially withheld. A fresh pinned developer build matched archives and\nmanifest byte for byte. Pinned Firefox 155.0.1 temporarily installed the exact final\nimmutable XPI and passed settings, metadata and four actual toolbar actions on\nfixed local TLS pages with query/hash sentinels and browser certificate bypass.\nAll launches contained only provider origin/path. ADR-0156 is implemented;\npublic provider network, permanent signing, Android and production activation\nremain outside recorded acceptance. The final privacy correction removes source\nquery/fragment fields before extension transmission or capture storage/POST and\nrefuses URL user information. Four pure capture security tests and both source\nand test strict TypeScript checks passed. The four regressions are now enforced\nby `workspace.check`, whose final test/type invocation passed separately after the\nfull component gate.\nThe final check-only removal of an unnecessary HOME override passed a fresh\nsandbox check in 9 seconds, preserving the same immutable release and archive\nbytes. The exact release's five HTTP/host tests passed again, seed 735823,\nincluding public metadata/byte equality and unsafe-file refusal. These checks\ndo not establish deployed public HTTPS."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9240
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [clone matrix review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-clone-matrix.md)\nrecords the completed profile and shape matrix. OSS and Customer sources clone\nthrough the same coordinator with the destination row and offered workload\ndescriptor equal to the source's captured architecture, image, profile and\nrequested sizes; a clone after a completed growth captures the grown disk. An\nimage disabled or rotated after creation refuses fresh admission but never\nblocks an existing workload's clone, which reads its placement-local boot\nbundle instead of the catalog. Non-inception clones stay independent of the\nconfigured inception image; reclassifying an existing machine's image into\nthat configuration fails closed at activation without a copy. A physically\nunconfirmed copy keeps its retained snapshot and commitments until an explicit\nretry re-offers the same intent; a destination that fails after publication\ncloses clone retry and cancellation and moves to ordinary lifecycle recovery.\nA stopped source natively stays stopped through the whole clone journey, and\nits disk keeps its native identity."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9244
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [coding CLI review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-native-guest-coding-clis.md)\nand [scenario ledger](#coverage) distinguish route properties, packaged guest\nexecution and isolated fixture inference from production provider acceptance,\nsustained load and physical-host fault behavior. Installing the tools alone\nestablishes none of those runtime boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9248
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [competing activation property](repo:marshall/test/marshall/compute/clone_restoration_history_test.exs)\nchecks queued identity, one/two/four allocation-free activation refusals, explicit\nretry, successful activation after verified cleanup and unchanged original release\ntimestamps. Its reviewed combined suite passed 14 checks in a SQL rollback sandbox.\nSynthetic outcomes establish database authority, not native source restoration;\nindependent-process, uncertain-transport and relocation competitor histories remain\nopen. See the [review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-02-clone-competing-activation.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9252
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [executor-loss review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-executor-loss-histories.md)\ndistinguishes generated SQL dispatch histories from the native started-effect\nfixture. The native fixture requires real effect, durable start, exact process\nloss, actual re-registration and ordinary browser-approved retry. The captured\nstrict fixture passed once in 675 seconds on the staged branch tree; native\npre-start loss and the earlier unexplained interruption remain explicit gaps.\nThis fixture does not change the production tool timeout.\n## User-level thread modes"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9256
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [foundation review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-harness-foundation.md)\ndescribes an isolated control fixture. The graph below is its proposed Compose\nlayout, not the deployed fleet or evidence that all containers have started.\nThe hosted campaign admits up to 32 active attempts so every selected Compose profile and its replicas can run concurrently; this operational limit does not establish a production deployment or a passing property result."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9260
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [image builder](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/windows/default.nix) installs\nWindows offline inside a KVM Nix sandbox: QEMU with OVMF boots Microsoft's\nhash-pinned evaluation ISO ([pin](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/windows/iso.json))\nwith modern-only virtio-pci devices, restricted user networking, an answer file\nand the pinned virtio-win drivers. `SetupComplete.cmd` enables Emergency\nManagement Services on COM1 at 115200 baud, disables sleep, Windows Update\nrestarts, DHCP and IPv6, writes `OVERALLS-SETUP-COMPLETE` to the serial port and\npowers off; the build fails without that marker. The result is a sparse raw GPT\ndisk, the firmware and a **version-5 manifest** (`boot.kind = firmware`,\n`pci = true`, `root.format = raw-gpt`, `guest_agents` `none` or `windows-v1`\nfrom the builder's `guestAgents` argument). Installation is not byte-stable,\nso each rebuild has a new identity; this is a recorded exception to the\nreproducible-template goals of the Linux images, and the reason the image is\nbuilt once and copied to every host before it is staged (see\n[deployment](#deployment))."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9264
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [installed guest clients](#guests) use these native protocols without API\ntranslation or custom launchers. Production hosts continue selecting only\nOpenAI. Disabled Anthropic routes return `provider_unavailable` before authority,\nspool admission or provider effects; client configuration cannot enable them.\nFixture-provider acceptance and production provider availability remain separate\nin [coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9268
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [lifetime generator repair](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-cli-history-generator.md)\nretains expiry, machine deletion and account retirement in every generated and\nshrunk lifetime history. The reproduced duplicate-exhaustion failure occurred\nwhile generating a history, before its authority assertions. These remain\nHTTP/LiveView and SQL-backed properties with synthetic execution fixtures;\nnative execution evidence retains its earlier captured scope."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9272
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [live egress workflow](repo:docs/acceptance/egress-acceptance.md) creates only recorded\ndisposable NixOS, Ubuntu and Customer fixtures through Porsche. It verifies\nordinary guest trust, the Brady signer chain, redirect and Git clone traffic,\npublic bytes/cache hits and protected-address refusals, then repeats after\nstop/start. Mutations require exact create receipts and current ownership;\nSSH failure is never a successful refusal. Deliberate outages and storage\npressure belong in isolated NixOS tests, not on the shared production host.\nTimeouts stop the local SSH/proxy attempt without cancelling admitted lifecycle\nwork; reconciliation keeps the original request ID. The Customer refusal uses\nan IPv4 destination independently verified reachable immediately before probing.\nFresh probes after healthy activation verify the deployed revision separately\nfrom publication. SSH or management transport failures remain failed evidence;\nlater successful probe stages do not establish uninterrupted transport stability\nor the earlier failure's cause. Resume admitted cleanup with its original request."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9276
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [native bootstrap](repo:lyra/src/native_bootstrap.rs) and\n[rc.d/SMF definitions](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/native-bootstrap.nix)\nconfigure networking before the native agent supervisor. Root temporarily reaches\nMMDS through a link-local address, obtains a fresh MMDSv2 identity, validates its\nactual interface MAC, profile, address slot and gateway, then removes temporary\nIPv4 state and disables IPv6. It installs the validated /32 address, paired\nInception address where required, and explicit gateway, MMDS, private and default\nroutes. Only verified resulting interface/netmask/routes permit a mode-0600\nvolatile identity receipt under a root-owned mode-0700 directory. DNS uses\n1.1.1.1 and 9.9.9.9; native TLS uses the OS CA bundle. Three bounded refusal\ntests passed. The eighteen-helper Linux/Windows/native tests and native Clippy\nbuild passed in 297 seconds. Actual bootstrap/service execution remains pending."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9280
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [native prerequisite review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-prerequisites.md)\nrecords passing source-built Galileo and Enrico release payload checks and a\nseparate disposable NixOS ZFS I/O run after its failed probe attempt. It also\nrecords two failed expanded service-role attempts: the first at the cookie\npipeline, the retry after Marshall cookie checks when Lilly startup failed and\nits owner file did not appear. The runtime-secret fixture has no passing\nrole-machine capture. The updater replay assertion and five-second ingress catalog decoder\nboundary are source/test changes without new execution evidence in this review.\nThese are prerequisite checks, not a current hosted harness or guest lifecycle\nacceptance. The ledger binds passing and failed attempts to their exact\nderivations and source hashes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9284
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [operator verification review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-backup-operator-verification.md)\ntracks generated complete-manifest, reference-retention, host-lock and refusal\nhistories through local encrypted Restic. This evidence is separate from the\nrecorded native TLS, ZFS, database/keyring and guest recovery acceptance.\nThe [custody review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-custody-backup-key.md)\nadds the exported `backup-custody` evaluation: the zero-failure key ceremony,\nthe exact wedge label and its previous-generation recovery, and\nrestore→decrypt→repository-access composition with the historical-generation\nrefusal, all synthetic."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9288
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [option reference](#options) lists every Overalls option in the default module,\nincluding nested records. Consult it for types, defaults and required fields."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9292
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [ordinary durable fake executor](repo:lilly/test/support/durable_fake_executor.ex)\naccepts stop or delete evidence only for an incarnation with a persisted launch\nmarker. A\n[generated fake command history](repo:lilly/test/vm/fake_executor_property_test.exs)\nchecks this causality through replay, generation advance and unseen commands.\nIt validates controlled-executor histories and cannot establish native Firecracker\nor ZFS effects."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9296
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [package procedure](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/vscode-web/README.md) explains source and\nsigning-key updates. Compiled policy and 19 extension-maintenance checks passed\nwith actual source-bundled validation. The final specialized Nix package passed\nits complete build and installed Chromium/PTY checks; fresh native\nNixOS/Ubuntu extension acceptance remains unrecorded. Historical results below\nretain their earlier package scope. The installed-validator maintenance derivation\nalso passed all 19 tests and Ruff/ty in nine seconds. The final standalone attempt\npassed its first browser terminal proof, then failed the new Native Policy stage.\nReplay observed the policy marker/EditorConfig result and no visible AI UI; the\nexact failing browser assertion remains unknown. A corrected per-action run\nlater passed the cold browser journey and Alice's outside-HOME policy stage,\nthen failed before invoking Bob's HOME command because its palette row was\nabsent for 30 seconds. Complete two-account controls/language execution and a\npassing rerun remain required. The\n[native fixture](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/vscode-web/default.nix)\nuses the actual package and broker with a synthetic roster/bridge. The complete\nNixOS service/Firecracker journey passed creation, tools, personal custody,\nbroker/worker loss, stop/start and revocation. Ubuntu provisioned and reached the\ncorrect editor origin but failed initial workbench visibility; its complete\njourney remains unresolved. Source inspection later identified malformed closure\ncopying without an existing `/nix/store` and retained build-user configuration\nownership. Assembly now creates the destination, preserves complete store paths\nand root custody, and asserts broker/editor/configuration placement.\nThe post-fix Ubuntu `058f13d8` run subsequently passed the initial cold editor/\nterminal/custody journey, then failed its real-guest policy phase; the NixOS run\npassed the same initial stage and also failed policy. At that historical stage\ncomplete native acceptance remained required. Recorded outcomes and broader gaps remain in\n[scenario coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9300
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [patched Firecracker](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/infrastructure/firecracker.nix) replaces the plain\nnixpkgs package in Lilly's release metadata and the development shell. It keeps\nthe `1.16.1` version string Lilly matches, so no fleet ordering constraint\nfollows from this change; hosts pick it up with their next activation like any\nother package update."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9304
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [patched static Firecracker](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/infrastructure/firecracker.nix) is the only\nVMM Lilly's release metadata and the development shell reference. Its nine\npatches keep the upstream version string and `Cargo.lock`, and widen the\nseccomp surface by exactly one vCPU-thread ioctl (`KVM_IOEVENTFD`, already\nallowed on the VMM thread):\nPVH start info carries the ACPI RSDP address, a read-only ACPI PM timer answers\nat I/O port 0x608 (not advertised to Linux guests through the FADT), a custom\nCPU template field `hyperv` installs the Hyper-V CPUID leaves while relocating\nthe KVM signature leaves to 0x40000100, virtio-pci devices start with memory\ndecoding and bus mastering enabled, a read-only CMOS clock at ports 0x70/0x71\nfollows host UTC, and a BAR the firmware or OS reprograms moves on the MMIO\nbus together with its queue doorbell ioeventfds (a sizing read keeps the\n64-bit type bit so edk2 classifies the BAR correctly), the block and net\ndevices implement virtio reset so the operating system can re-initialize a\ndevice the firmware already drove, block requests whose data spans\nseveral descriptors are transferred whole by both I/O engines, and the vsock\ndevice walks indirect descriptor tables (the virtio-win ring library posts every\nmulti-descriptor buffer through one whenever the driver holds a table, whether\nor not `VIRTIO_RING_F_INDIRECT_DESC` was negotiated; the feature is still not\noffered). Linux guests see no change: they never touch the new ports, keep their\nfirmware-less boot, never move a BAR and, not being offered the feature, never\npost an indirect table."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9308
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [post-import cache fixture](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/zfs-guest/template-post-import.exs)\npreserves exact sealed identity, including the native device field. A device-only\nchange must refuse reuse; supported fenced retirement and full hashing produce a\ndistinct ready incarnation rather than revive a quarantined one. Its cache action\ngrants no placement, intent, launch-attempt or boot authority and changes no\nproduction recovery or terminal Executor refusal. Retained-state inspection\ndemonstrated this strict refusal after another import, but did not capture the\noriginal failure instant. The complete corrected Ubuntu suite exercised exact\nreuse in the new action; focused current NixOS also passed exact reuse and ordinary\nneighbour cleanup in its current packaged prefix. Device-only replacement remains\nunexecuted in both runs, as scoped in the\n[native guest review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-native-guest-coding-clis.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9312
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [promotion constructor](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/native-promotion/freebsd.nix)\nverifies disk/firmware sizes and hashes plus hash-bound assembly and five native\nscenario evidence sets before publishing exactly `disk.raw`, `firmware` and\n`manifest.json`. Each service receipt must name the exact reviewed oracles,\nincluding the scenario's clean or abrupt shutdown checks. FreeBSD retains its\n22 approved checks; OmniOS requires 25, adding workspace ownership/unrelated-root\nrefusal, executor heartbeat withdrawal and whole-process-group cancellation.\nMissing, extra or substituted names refuse even at the expected count. The\npromotion unit gate and current sealed FreeBSD package build passed without\ngranting new native execution or OmniOS admission. Candidate receipts retain\nfalse admission flags. The sealed\ncandidate enters Nix with a reviewed recursive NAR hash through ordinary signed\nOveralls Cachix substitution. A cache miss fails with publication instructions;\nthere is no temporary-path fallback, signing secret in evaluation or automatic\nstock-image reconstruction. See the\n[publication procedure](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/native-promotion/README.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9316
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [publishing recovery property](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-backup-publication-recovery.md) tests persisted\npublishing journals through installed cleanup with retained component/manifest\nreferences and unchanged last success. Combined native execution passed in 155 seconds; synthetic references\ndo not prove upload durability or producer-death recovery.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9320
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [recipient-history review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-secret-recipient-histories.md)\nrecords independent actual decryption controls and exact refusal/retry inventories.\nBounded interruption may leave completed documents published; a reported possibly\nupdated document requires inspection and explicit retry. Native custody/rotation\nrecords retain their original scope. These checks cannot revoke previously learned\nplaintext or establish recovery-key availability after physical loss."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9324
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [relocation competitor property](repo:marshall/test/marshall/compute/relocation_competing_history_test.exs)\nnow exercises actual admission before and after both authenticated endpoint stream\nrevocation receipts. One receipt retains occupancy; two permit release while source\nrestoration and disk cleanup remain pending. It checks retained original slot IDs,\nfirst-release timestamps and complete competitor allocations under duplicate replay.\nThe combined focused run passed two properties (seed281508,49.7seconds); full\nbatch gates remain pending. Native stream cessation and\nindependent-process races remain separate gaps in the\n[history review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-02-relocation-and-workflow-backlog-histories.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9328
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [resource verification](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-team-resource-verification.md)\nrecords corrected member use and distinguishes generated control-plane histories,\nthree-node committed retirement overlap and separately recorded native execution.\nRetirement refuses a current team owner. An accepted ownership transfer ordered\nfirst therefore prevents target retirement; retirement ordered first archives its\nmembership and cancels the offer. Membership admission cannot commit after the\naccount retirement cleanup boundary.\nThe native resource check requires explicit command refusals and recognized-key\nSSH denial with fresh unauthorized control evidence; a transport failure cannot\nestablish revoked authority. It checks fresh connections, not termination of an\nalready open SSH session. The recorded native run passes shared guest use,\nmember/admin/foreign and Customer refusals, retained home after stop/start, and\nintegration removal/re-addition. It uses public HTTPS CA policy, current gateway\nidentity and strict certificates. It waits for actual successful SSH after boot\nor roster changes; a running VMM alone does not prove SSH ready. The review keeps\nthe earlier failed restart attempt and its scope.\nThe fixture selects its registered image explicitly for shared OSS, personal OSS\nand team-tagged Customer creation; a missing configured default must still refuse\nan omitted image. An HTTP admission receipt does not establish a usable guest."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9332
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [run protocol](repo:docs/history/ledgers/provisioning-fastpath.md) requires host-load and\nnative template-identity review before claiming a warm-cache speedup. A dry-run\ncustody test or completed result file alone does not prove VM isolation, physical\ncleanup, or the ≥30% and sub-10-second targets. Failed or uncertain cleanup\nretains run-owned request IDs for inspection and blocks a passing result."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9336
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [scenario ledger](#coverage) names the exact test artifacts, inspected assertion,\nevidence type, execution record where available, scope, and missing preferred tests.\nA test's existence is not a passing result. A passing mock or unit test is not proof\nthat a kernel, TLS endpoint, native VM, or physical recovery boundary held."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9340
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [scoped review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-editor.md)\nrecords passing local authority/protocol checks and the 60-second standalone\nnative fixture with synthetic gateway/roster authority. The complete NixOS\nMarshall→Audi→Lilly→Firecracker journey also passed actual login, personal editing,\npreview/terminal tools, worker recovery, saved-data stop/start, unsafe Origin\nrefusal and sign-out/WebSocket closure with fresh sign-in recovery. The corrected\nmerged NixOS snapshot passed again with current physical readiness; its exact\nrevision is recorded separately from the earlier run. Corrected Ubuntu\nprovisioned and reached the correct origin but reproduced initial workbench\nvisibility failure. Later source inspection identified closure-path/root-custody\ndefects and corrected assembly. The later startup-release full Ubuntu pass above\nsupplies its browser/control/recovery scope without retroactively explaining every\nearlier failure. A bad-gateway document observation alone does not prove\nbroker/worker health or validate the correction.\nTwo-writer native separation, broader fault histories and production remain gaps in\n[scenario coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9344
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [source roster](repo:nix/domains/system/otel-fleet/objectives.nix) binds each source\nto all enrolled hosts. Source placement changes must update that roster. The\nbaseline retains twenty objective definitions and forty burn evaluators. Fast\nfive-minute/one-hour burns consume two percent of a seven-day budget; slow\nthirty-minute/six-hour burns consume five percent. Both windows, current coverage\nand a fresh objective recording are required. No eligible objective means no\nmanufactured success or burn rate."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9348
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [state journal properties](repo:lilly/test/lifecycle/journal_test.exs)\nexercise histories beyond the former 64-event bound while diagnostics are\nunavailable, replay identity, and refusal of corrupt or non-v2 primary envelopes\nwithout rewriting.\nThese are local fsync/journal tests, not native VM lifetime or distributed\ncollector durability evidence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9352
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [step-up review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-11-privileged-administrator-step-up.md)\nrecords the current evidence boundaries and preferred real-action property plus\nNixOS browser acceptance. Hardware authenticators, physical-host separation and\nproduction cutover remain separate. Historical Control room, credential and\nCustomer tests cannot close the remaining final-snapshot gaps in [coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9356
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [subsequent login verification](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-mfa-login-verification.md)\ntracks independent login histories, committed exchange/revocation races and a\nthree-replica native browser scenario. Execution status is recorded in\n[scenario coverage](#coverage); all three native services are colocated in one VM."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9360
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [supervisor recovery review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-guest-cli-recovery.md)\nrecords generated actual-supervisor process histories with synthetic registration\nreceipts. Its dedicated native NixOS failure journey passed invalid-format and\nregistration-timeout recovery, exact prior inode/digest restoration and real\nshared-operator tool work under fresh Marshall fences. The\n[update failure matrix review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-update-failure-matrix.md)\nadds the same strict recovery journey on the Ubuntu guest and an interrupted-fetch\njourney through the real console seam on both distributions: a staged digest without\nverified persisted bytes never activates, one real check against the private manifest\nreconverges durable state, and a failed or closed-denial check never poisons the\nrejection cooldown, preserved executable identity or the Marshall executor fence.\nDirect candidate injection still bypasses artifact delivery. First boot without the\nprivate artifact service available, power-loss and I/O faults, and real guest-command\ndrain during replacement remain separate evidence.\nEarlier NixOS/Ubuntu periodic private update and tool-work results retain their\noriginal scope; they do not establish failed-candidate recovery."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9364
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [uploaded-point property](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-backup-upload-publication-histories.md)\nadds generated real local-Restic interruption after completed manifest upload,\nexact payload restoration and keep_last 1/2/3 reference retention/expiry. The full\n13-test suite passed in 1418 seconds; S3 and native mounted-reader recovery remain separate."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9368
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [user-operation verification campaign](repo:docs/history/ledgers/verification-campaign.md)\ntracks incremental property and native machine improvements, missing capabilities,\nand external acceptance boundaries. Queue entries are planned work; only explicit\nexecution records establish passing evidence for their named revision and scope."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9372
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The [verification campaign](repo:docs/history/ledgers/verification-campaign.md) tracks a current\nbase-fleet fixture gap: seven catalog variants select images without an enabled\nboot-trust receiver while Lilly requires a trust receipt. Historical passes keep\ntheir original revision and scope; they do not establish current fixture\nacceptance. The default-profile journey selects the existing complete private\nservice fixture. Shared NixOS and Ubuntu fixture repair is campaign 011."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9376
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The `NAME-base` machine installs host access without enrolled roles or update\npolling. The enrolled `NAME` output requires authenticated host keys and Git\ncustody. Role activation does not install disks."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9380
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The `backup-restic` repository uses Restic's local file backend because the\nproduction operator reports one failure status for an unavailable repository\nand for a validator refusal; a network backend would let a fault read as a\nrefusal. Antithesis does not step the wall clock backward in these runs, so\ncoordinator publication compatibility uses a labelled **STUB (harness)** for a\nsmall SDK-chosen fraction of cases: the workload preloads the image's pinned\nlibfaketime into that one coordinator process, and a fixed Restic wrapper steps\nits wall clock back 120 seconds when the host-state upload begins, after the\njournal recorded `started_at`. Restic, raw oracle reads and the production\nmodules are unchanged, and every existing `always` check still applies. Hosted\nrun `6993853fa020fd46ed8e13c6c123ac86-65-0` confirmed the defect the stub\ntargets: the coordinator recorded `completed_at` before `started_at`, published\na tag-visible manifest and then failed its own retention validator. The\ncoordinator now publishes `completed_at` no earlier than `started_at`, and the\n`backup-operators` evaluation check reproduces the step with a shim-triggered\nclock offset and requires a retention-valid, inspectable point. The stub is not evidence of\nreal kernel or host clock steps, NTP behaviour or production hosts. The `satellite-standin` **guest stand-in** is a labelled harness process\nfrom the workload image that shares only `lilly-a`'s network namespace and\nspeaks the guest-side TLS protocol to `169.254.254.1`. It is not a guest: it has\nno VM, boot, tap, placement or execution authority and mounts no secret. Its\nassertions test only private Audi's transport-only attribution with the origin\nfixture as effect witness; they say nothing about guest routing or isolation.\nThe overlay also corrects the private Audi start script, which omitted the\nrequired `connector_uid` and so could not load its configuration, and waited\non the disabled ordinary `audi.sock` instead of Lilly's private-services\n`private.sock`. Porsche refuses to listen without a Marshall-signed host\ncertificate, which Lyra fetches in production; the stand-in start script makes\nthe same reviewed `/api/ssh/v1/host-certificate` request with the gateway token\nheld in a private header file. The harness Marshall had no SSH host CA, so that\nrequest returned the opaque unavailable reply; the synthetic secret volume now\ncarries a generated ed25519 SSH host CA, and both replicas receive\n`MARSHALL_SSH_HOST_TRUST` shaped like the NixOS module's. It is test-only key\nmaterial, never a production CA. Ziggy\ncompletion, Galileo named origin and the editor login-code race need a current\nrunning VM; the controlled Lilly pool reports unavailable capacity and no Audi\neditor gateway, editor release or wildcard is configured, so their primary and\n`Sometimes` assertions stay unevaluated; the first hosted run\n(`e6bff5d6e4b57e6bb43c7a018384750d-65-0`) confirmed this, and each stop now logs a\nfixed `Satellite VM placement unexercised` reason. That run also listed twelve\naccess assertions as never hit because the satellite drivers imported the\nfixture login from the access browser module; the login now lives in the\nassertion-free `access/session.py`, so only selected callsites enter the\nroster. Ziggy and Galileo issue and\nauthenticate credentials only for an enabled host, and Marshall's reviewed\nenable action requires a fresh execution-ready report that the controlled\n`lilly-a` never sends, so satellite enrollment applies a labelled harness\n**STUB**: it sets only `lilly-a`'s administrative `enabled` flag through the\nHost `:configure` action and verifies it with the operator `show` command. The\nstub fabricates no readiness, capacity, pool credit or VM binding, so Marshall\nadmission still refuses the host; it is not evidence for the readiness gate on\nhost enablement. A failed satellite operator command reports only the CLI's\nfixed atom status line. Both profiles have rendered Compose\ngraphs that pass the selection validators and unit tests; neither has a local\n`compose up` or hosted run.\nThe [integrated-host record](repo:antithesis/verification/integrated-host-boot.json)\ncaptures two unavailable-health TCG boots and one corrected third-image boot\nwith actual ZFS and cgroup sampling. A fourth built private-service image\nrepeated the measurements, completed runtime PKI preparation and produced a\nworkspace-source-v2 guest image witness. Both measured samples remain\ndisconnected and execution-unready. A private 9p witness identified the first three images;\nfocused crash-actuator tests passed nine checks without a QEMU crash. A\nboot-bound guest-trust receipt adapter passed twelve tests and forced Dialyzer,\nbut the full integrated-mode suite failed at 7/559 and private service roles\nhave no runtime credential or terminal acceptance.\nThe earlier local base Compose diagnostic passed migration, Marshall A/B, enrollment,\nthree synthetic account/passkey creations and Lilly health. Read-only SQL\nconfirmed exactly three users and three account passkeys; the enrollment\noperator had no listening socket. The v4 host was connected, but\n`report_sequence` was 0 and `reported_at` absent, so setup did not fire in that\nproject. Diagnostic4 subsequently logged `INFO Antithesis setup complete` after\na fresh v4 report at sequence 44. Six serial commands produced 13 true SDK\nassertion records and the final SQL observation found fresh sequence 392;\nthe [foundation review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-harness-foundation.md)\nrecords local image IDs and the stale selected-manifest hashes. The controlled\ncontainer committed cgroup-derived effective fields while preserving nil pool\ncapacity and false execution readiness. Raw cgroup files were not independently\ncompared, and hosted ingestion remains unverified."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9384
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The `database-recovery` machine check uses the real release Repo on a separate\nmachine from PostgreSQL. It requires three observed lock waiters before client\nloss, then checks the existing holder and connection reclamation deadlines.\nThe old settings and a polling-only candidate both missed the 35-second bound.\nA captured candidate with one-second active disconnect polling and a 15-second\nTCP acknowledgement limit reclaimed all ten connections in 27.719 seconds; its\nhealthy 20-second query also passed. Its exact captured-source scope and inspected\nexecutable equivalence are retained. Both complete integrated NixOS and Ubuntu\nterminal journeys separately passed browser recovery, retained shell effects,\nsharing and revocation; their VM-script timers were 702.42 and 683.84 seconds.\nNeither result establishes physical/provider recovery or production load. See the\n[execution scope](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-terminal-workspace-verification.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9388
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The `freebsd-v1` and `illumos-v1` image families reserve the existing jail-local\nvirtio-vsock transport. `lyra.native` now packages nine bootstrap, kernel-probe,\nagent, flush, readiness, trust, workspace and SSH helper binaries per OS. The separate\n`terminald.native` package cross-links the shared terminal daemon for both OSes.\nTheir manifests identify all eighteen production Lyra executables and both terminal daemons\nby exact bytes, SHA-256, guest interpreter and reviewed native shared libraries;\nLinux dependencies, runtime search paths and executable stacks refuse packaging.\nFreeBSD uses `/libexec/ld-elf.so.1`; illumos uses `/usr/lib/amd64/ld.so.1`.\nThe immutable official OmniOS r151050 userland SDK verifies pinned manifests,\ncompressed/plain payload digests and its final NAR hash. Its build-time fetch\nhas the documented public fixed-output TLS authentication limitation;\nruntime TLS verification remains required."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9392
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The `guest-boot-profile` and `ubuntu-guest-boot-profile` NixOS machine checks\nboot the trust-enabled images through Lilly's real trust delivery three times:\nwith the image roots, with an added root and with that root retired. Each boot\nprints the report. After each boot the guest bundle must hold exactly the\ndelivered roots. On Ubuntu, OpenSSL CApath verification must accept every\npresent root and refuse the retired one. `zfs-guest` and `ubuntu-guest` assert\nthe offsets and report on their first boot. Property tests in\n`lilly/native/boot-log` cover chunking independence and bounded retention.\nAbsolute numbers from these nested-KVM fixtures are slower than fleet hardware,\nso compare runs of the same check."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9396
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The `log-store` machine check exercises classified journald export through\nverified TLS and path-scoped credentials, plus Grafana's provisioned read-only\nVictoriaLogs datasource. The retired Marshall `/admin/logs`, `/admin/slos` and\nfleet history routes return 404. Their pre-cutover browser/client evidence remains\nhistorical; it does not establish current Grafana acceptance. The\n[observability decoupling review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-09-observability-decoupling.md)\nrecords native provisioning/query execution and remaining deployment gaps."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9400
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The accepted ADR-0160 flow keeps a requested administrator mutation on its current\npage while an inline passkey ceremony completes. Ordinary signed-in reads stay\navailable without elevation. The prompt identifies the pending action; it\nretains only its exact server-bound actor/login/resource/action/arguments and\nnever replays an arbitrary browser event after verification. A successful step-up\ncreates a fixed fifteen-minute receipt and retries once after fresh admission."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9404
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The accepted [context and guidance decisions](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0178-durable-agent-context-and-planning.md)\nadd persisted model context, planning interaction and managed skill state to\nAgents. These are resources within Marshall, not additional services. Thread\nactions still resolve the current task binding and authorize the actor before\nadmission. Guest files are collected outside transactions, validated and then\nattached as immutable snapshots under the current execution binding. Compaction\npublishes a checkpoint only after matching the context generation and eligible\nevent frontier. Pending questions and exact proposal implementation share the\ndurable turn boundary. Implementation and verification remain scoped in the\n[context review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-agent-context-and-planning.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9408
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The accepted [context and planning decision](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0178-durable-agent-context-and-planning.md)\nadds a Commands control to the top composer, composer-scoped Ctrl/Cmd+K and slash\ncompletion. Implementation and browser acceptance are tracked separately in\nthe [context review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-agent-context-and-planning.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9412
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The accepted guest-context operations read the creator's permanent guest account\nhome and the thread's selected working directory. On Windows, roster activation\ncreates a missing profile using the account SID and native profile API; context\nreads use that SID's registered `ProfileImagePath`, never the SYSTEM profile or\nan inferred home. Failed activation disables the account. Image setup creates\n`C:\\workspace` for threads without a source checkout. Linux resolves the durable\nlogin through the guest account database. These operations retain existing\nexecutor leases and placement fences and add no listener."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9416
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The accepted request retains its normalized command and resolved resources.\nRetrying cannot retarget a different machine after name reuse or silently select\na different default image. Query the original request after a lost response or\ntimeout. Request status remains subject to current account authorization, including\nafter the machine is archived."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9420
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The actor runs at most one observation at a time and never loses a request.\nA request that arrives mid-observation starts the next one as soon as it ends,\nexcept that a newer command preempts a create that has not reached its launch\ncommit (ADR-0176)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9424
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The additional `database-recovery-active` fixture holds the same row lock during\na real ninety-second server statement, observes active holder state and three\nqueued waiters, then crashes the client machine. It retains the twenty-second\nholder and thirty-five-second all-connection bounds. The initial active-holder\ncrash variant passed in 60.51 seconds with all ten sessions reclaimed in 19.518\nseconds; it predates the final matrix and seeded submission orders.\nThe final six-case matrix combines both holder states with machine crash,\nbidirectional packet loss and client-to-server packet loss. Link cases freeze\nclient userspace, retain kernel sockets and require positive DROP counters in\nthe tested direction. Seeded waiter submission permutations do not establish\nPostgreSQL queue order. All six cases passed capture\n`1790973915375-2537070-0.jsonl`, reclaiming all ten sessions in 19.432–27.882\nseconds and subsequently reacquiring the row. The\n[history review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-02-database-loss-histories.md)\nretains pre-injection fixture failures and exact directional counters. Surviving\nrelease contenders, repeated reconnect histories, uncertain commits and hosted\nfaults remain separate gaps."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9428
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The agent executor is the same [control loop](repo:lyra/src/agent.rs) Linux\nguests run, now in the Lyra library behind a small host interface;\n[`overalls-agentd-windows`](repo:lyra/src/bin/overalls-agentd-windows.rs) runs\nit as a SYSTEM task. It registers a fence with `llm.internal`, which the\nbootstrap resolves through a name-resolution rule that sends only\n`.internal` names to the local resolver at 169.254.254.1, and runs\n`exec_command` through Windows PowerShell 5.1 inside a kill-on-close job with\nthe same 20 s deadline. Tool paths must be absolute Windows paths, and\nMarshall tells the model so: for `windows-v1` images its system message and tool\ndescriptions name Windows PowerShell 5.1 and paths such as `C:\\workspace`. There is\nno self-update on Windows: a new executor ships with a rebuilt image. The\nprivate HTTPS client uses native TLS (schannel) on Windows, as Linux guests\nuse OpenSSL, because rustls rejects the deployed private leaf."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9432
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The approved FreeBSD image ID is\n`sha256:d141e4266c29da00a1378157b1d39369b1d48e577bca4ce7375e8ff6d399557b`,\nwith disk SHA-256\n`5022d714b94032253bc44b706b37426f8a247debd155fbe223fde28000228f55`.\nThe [compiled registry](repo:protocol/lib/overalls_protocol/native_admission.ex)\ncompares a complete validated manifest or portable boot descriptor with that\nreviewed identity. Marshall also binds the manifest-derived image ID to the\ndurable catalog row; Lilly repeats admission before launching. Missing identity,\nan altered artifact/provenance field, a row-ID mismatch or an unapproved native\nfamily refuses. The ordinary capacity, current-boot kernel attestation, trust\nand readiness gates still apply after image qualification."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9440
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The arrows describe the accepted interaction boundary. PostgreSQL owns admitted\ncontext and selected revisions; the guest owns its filesystem and materialized\nskill files. Ziggy owns provider execution/accounting within current admission,\nnot scheduling or checkpoint authority. A browser, PubSub hint or guest path\ncannot independently authorize a context or configuration change."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9444
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The audiobookshelf package to use."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9445
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The audit-retention sweep removes at most 1,000 expired lifecycle and PaperTrail\nrows in one transaction and schedules a maintenance-queue continuation when a\nbatch is full. A [generated backlog test](repo:marshall/test/marshall/compute/lifecycle_audit_test.exs)\nchecks empty, partial and multiple full batches against remaining expired rows\nand continuation counts while preserving the live VM. It drives the action\ndirectly; Oban execution, cron recovery after job failure and elapsed expiry\nlag remain untested."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9453
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The authorized merge combines prior main `f39d509a` with browser feature\n`a3d2b69c`, retaining stopped-only relocation, personal bundled-editor updates and\nthe canonical shared observability formatter. The exact merged finite vocabulary\nwas regenerated to restore the diagnostic `pool_identity` atom. Focused merged\nLilly checks passed 34 checks (11 properties, 23 tests), its 104-event logging gate\nand structural contracts; Observability passed its schema check and ten formatter/\nshared-library tests, seed 957221. Marshall's 164-event logging gate and all\nnineteen contract regression tests also passed. These are scoped integration\nchecks. Browser native captures below retain their immutable feature-source scope;\nmerged-main native and full component acceptance are separate."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9457
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The blocking gate compares source inventories and chapter digests against reviewed\nreceipts. It catches edits, additions, deletions, missing chapters and invalid\nreferences. The Nix check also compares the evaluated option reference. Passing\nthese checks establishes currency and structure, not factual correctness.\nGenerated build caches and `.nixos-test-history` are excluded consistently from\nGit and packaged-source inventories. Execution claims need an explicit reviewed\nrecord; the generated history file is not an acceptance receipt.\nCI-only `.github/` automation and unattended `devenv.lock`/`flake.lock` pin changes\nare excluded from review receipts; source configuration such as `devenv.nix`,\n`devenv.yaml` and `flake.nix` remains covered."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9461
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The broker binds the current boot's exact eth0 IPv4 address at TCP 4444 and admits\nonly the derived profile gateway. It verifies the typed execution/release/account\npreface and reconciles the current roster before starting a personal systemd\nworker. Root-owned systemd launch identity binds the exact personal UID/name,\nprotocol, commit, release and origin through a digest. Unchanged identity preserves\nthe worker; a legitimate changed origin/release requires successful stop before\nreplacement, keeping personal files. Metadata or foreign socket custody failure\nrefuses without stopping the worker. The native worker owns its home, server data, extension directory and\nmode-0600 Unix socket; there is no root editor fallback. Passwordless sudo remains\navailable to existing guest writers, so these personal directories do not establish\nconfidentiality against another writer or root."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9465
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The browser and Porsche SSH creation entries select OSS when no profile is\nchosen. Explicit Customer selection remains owner-only and blocks sharing,\nagent registration and guest-initiated egress. Browser drafts retain the selected\nprofile and request identity across refresh and reload. SSH retries compare the\noriginal explicit inputs before resolving defaults: changing an omitted profile\nto an explicit profile conflicts, including explicit OSS. The\n[default-profile verification review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-default-oss-verification.md)\nrecords passing generated histories and the bounded native creation journey.\nBoth OSS guests reached the local DNS/HTTP origin; the explicitly selected\nCustomer refused sharing, foreign reads, agent admission and outbound HTTP.\nThe wider packet, tool-execution and guest-user SSH matrices remain separate."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9469
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The browser extension detector is a local presentation aid, not a security\nboundary. It requests optional host access only for the exact normalized\nOveralls origin saved by the user. A packaged content script sets a stable\ninstalled marker on that origin; no source page contents, credentials or\ntelemetry are collected."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9473
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The browser extension opens supported repository pages in Overalls. The SSH guides\ndescribe Porsche v1; your operator supplies the SSH hostname for deployments\nwith Porsche enabled."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9477
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The browser launcher change passed pinned all-files ruff-format, Ruff and ty.\nImport sorting explicitly classifies the copied native `browser` helper as local,\nkeeping checkout and packaged configurations consistent when launcher dependencies\ncontain vendored Python. This classification and import ordering preserve helper\nbehavior and provide no new native editor execution evidence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9481
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The browser's approval GET changes no authority. Only the explicit form binds\nthe invocation's digest to the signed-in user and selected machine. Every CLI\nrequest rechecks current account and machine write access; a digest URL cannot\nserve as an API bearer. Browser sign-out revokes that browser login. An already\napproved CLI invocation has its own one-hour lifetime, still bounded by current\naccount, sharing and machine state. A different machine or thread, withdrawn\nwrite permission, retired account, deleted machine or expired session refuses\nsubsequent CLI use. Repeating an identical prompt request key retains its original\nturn; changing its payload refuses."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9489
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The bundled editor permanently disables built-in AI, Copilot and Chat through\nproduct policy and service guards. User, workspace and synchronized settings\ncannot enable them. Generic completion APIs and separately installed extension\nwebviews remain available; users control those extensions independently."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9493
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The bundled editor permanently refuses built-in AI/Chat capability even under\nconflicting settings or direct service calls. All guest folders are trusted by\nthe worker; users must accept project extension execution before opening them.\nAutomatic trust does not approve direnv, change egress or grant Marshall access.\nThe immutable Open VSX baseline is verified and hash-pinned before extraction;\nthe maintenance updater shares the packaged signature and upstream engine\nvalidators. Failures before lock replacement preserve old bytes; after replacement,\na parent synchronization failure reports durability uncertainty with a complete\nnew lock potentially visible. It cannot claim rollback or crash durability.\nNineteen recorded developer tests cover artifacts, generated paths/pagination,\nclosed outputs and this publication boundary with actual source-bundled\nsignature/engine validation. Native extension execution remains a named gap.\nThe Nix maintenance gate additionally ran these 19 tests against the final\ninstalled validator and passed Ruff/ty; it still does not prove native isolation.\nPer-user controls and separately installed extensions retain their\nexisting authority. Static package defaults produce no invented audit events."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9497
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The capture page stages bounded input without creating a machine. Complete\nordinary sign-in before automatic OSS admission. A same-browser magic-link tab\nresumes the signed-cookie pending launch. Invalid/expired input and unavailable\nsource-capable images refuse; successful creation clears only the matching intent.\nSee [access](#access), [guest workspaces](#guests) and\n[ADR-0156](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0156-browser-launcher-distribution.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9501
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The catalog now includes Arch Linux and AlmaLinux as foreign Linux variants.\nTheir builders reuse the version-4 kernel image contract and the reviewed shared\nforeign-Linux root assembly, but add deterministic `arch` and `almalinux` markers so\ntheir image IDs remain distinct. The [`foreign-linux-guests` evaluation](repo:nix/verification/tests/evaluation/foreign-linux-guests.nix)\nchecks the marker, version-4 command shape and kernel/root artifact bounds. It\ndoes not boot either distribution or establish distribution-native package,\nnetwork, isolation or recovery behavior. Both entries use the normal Linux\nguest agent row and are catalogued without standby metadata in [`fleet/guest-images.nix`](repo:fleet/guest-images.nix).\nTheir offline image assembly now carries the pinned Lyra/terminald binaries,\nPython, Git, OpenSSH, CA bundle and generic systemd service units; evaluation\nchecks verify those closures and service references. The later helper-fixed\nArch/Alma runtime results below are separate from evaluation evidence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9505
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The complete repaired-count Marshall scan at seed 634032 passed 1,682 of 1,694\ntests, failed 12 and excluded four in 1,342.4 seconds. Covenant passed 24/24,\npresentation JavaScript 9/9, Monospace passed and forced Dialyzer passed with\nzero errors/skips. Six failures were the native TooSlow heuristic counting real\ndatabase/distributed/passkey/SMTP runtime. The scoped CI-profile repairs retain\ncase counts, clocks and assertions; actual linked-NIF setting getters prove\nonly the TooSlow suppression bit differs, preserving randomness, seed,\ndatabase, phases and backend. Other repairs retain unique per-case ingress/key\nownership, the requested lowercase alphabet, peer range and existing\n`image_name` output field. Fresh focused and complete execution remain required;\nthe failed scan is not a passing consumer gate. The later pair of actual-host\nbuilds at `d388cf91a` passed in 264 seconds; no later deployment or native runtime\nresult follows from that build record.\nLilly's final repaired-count precommit then passed 610 tests in 66.8 seconds,\nseed 991989, Covenant 15/15 and forced Dialyzer with zero errors/skips in 12.46\nseconds. Both actual-host toplevel builds on frozen registry/foreign-mode source\nand merged `21831915e` passed in 247 seconds. This records corrected count and\nbuild closure evidence, separately from native guest or later deployment claims.\nThe merged first batch `c420650b2` then passed both actual-host toplevel builds\nin 233 seconds and both consumers converged healthy to that exact release.\nIts selected catalog still had six entries; the subsequent exact OmniOS alias\nactivation is a separate source, build, deployment and live-evidence scope.\nBoth actual-host toplevel builds for the seven-entry catalog at `008a02331`\npassed in 291 seconds. That build predates the later caption and crawler-policy\nmerge; deployment convergence and live OmniOS acceptance remain separate.\nBoth actual-host builds after the caption/crawler merge at `b17d6beec` then\npassed in 263 seconds, and both consumers converged healthy to that exact\nrelease. That deployment record does not establish a live OmniOS journey.\nThe combined frozen Windows/Linux/oracle source subsequently passed the\nmulti-target Lyra release build in 318 seconds and actual private Linux\nfilesystem/manifest plus evaluation gates in 167 seconds. These are build and\noffline artifact records; repaired-image runtime and later deployment remain\nseparate.\nThe ten-file focused Marshall rerun passed 59/60 tests in 407.4 seconds, seed\n393478, clearing all twelve original failures. A sibling four-case retry-window\nhistory hit the same TooSlow heuristic; its scoped profile retains every case\nand oracle. That selected test then passed in 46.4 seconds, seed 525114, with\nthree unrelated tests excluded. The complete final-repair Marshall gate then\npassed 1,694 tests with four excluded in 1,540.6 seconds, seed 156566, Covenant\n24/24, terminal JavaScript 36/36, presentation JavaScript 9/9 and Monospace.\nForced Dialyzer passed with zero errors/skips in 59.03 seconds; the command\nexited zero. It started from `21831915e` with the repairs later committed as\n`b84ac6277`, on frontend `02cd8bd`; the later literal caption and handbook\nmerge does not change that test-source scope. These remain separate focused\nand complete records and do not verify the later `f1ad336` frontend pin.\nThe complete `f1ad336` Marshall scan then failed only the native TooSlow\nheuristic in the opposite-source host-lock property: 1,693/1,694 passed, four\nexcluded, 1,526.1 seconds, seed 770435. Covenant 24/24, both JavaScript suites,\nMonospace and forced Dialyzer with zero errors/skips passed. The current scoped\nCI-profile repair preserves both cases, every rotation, three real peers,\nclocks and SQL/allocation oracles; its selected actual `08cd428` run passed in\n26.9 seconds at the same seed. A separate 512 MiB/10-second context probe\npassed three forms at seed 454122. The complete current-pin Marshall gate then\npassed 1,694 tests with four exclusions in 1,382.3 seconds, seed 325708,\nCovenant 24/24, both JavaScript suites, Monospace and forced Dialyzer with zero\nerrors, skips or unnecessary skips in 49.65 seconds. Its frozen runtime\nsources precede the machine-pin integration; final merged-source consumer gates\nremain separate. The matrix review records exact log and source-ledger digests.\nAt the recorded `08cd428` checkpoint, actual Mix resolution normalized all\neleven Hegel locks to that full commit. All eight incoming dependency snapshots were stale, so their hashes\nwere refreshed from actual fetches. Every new stored lock snapshot equals its\nsource and every independently computed recursive NAR hash equals its configured\nvalue. This repairs dependency custody without transferring any prior-pin test\nor release result. Upstream `cdbc76086` merged byte-identical resolved locks and\nhashes, confirmed against the independent proof; both hosts then converged\nhealthy to that exact revision with zero failures at 10:43 UTC. The exact\ncustody and convergence scope is in the matrix review.\nThe later `8fe9786` import again changes eleven pins and eight configured\ndependency hashes. At completed merge `0de23e24d`, all eight ordinary-production\noutputs were realized from existing caches. Their lock snapshots equal the\nimmutable evaluated source and checkout locks, and independently computed\nrecursive NAR hashes equal all eight configured values. Qualified receipt\n`/tmp/overalls-8fe-fod-custody.6EMduL/receipt-qualified.json` has SHA-256\n`9ab22b9dd67c43eb37c66d38c79defb5093495500718b83baaeab688c6bdc09b`.\nThis is cached dependency custody, not fresh refetch/reproducibility, release,\nnative NIF, harness-variant or Cargo acceptance. New consumer checks retain their\nexact source scope; older custody and releases do not qualify the new NIF.\nAt the older pin, the default outer count was 100 even for CI properties;\nAudi's stream-guard menu therefore required at least 60–140 seconds of expiry\nwaits, and its complete 42-mutation role menu scheduled 4,200 fresh OTP starts.\nTheir aggregate timeouts preserve failures rather than establishing a product\nrefusal defect. Fixture-budget corrections must retain the per-case closure\nand refusal assertions, default exploration and fresh-process boundaries.\nThe combined `870eecef5` checkpoint also passed both actual-host toplevel builds\nin 357 seconds and converged healthy on both hosts at 10:10 UTC. Independent\nforced Dungarees rebuilds of the actual Arch/Alma ext4s and wrappers passed\nwith remote hashes matching local outputs. Later Arch root-policy artifact and\npinned-tmpfiles scratch checks passed; neither is a fresh guest lifecycle.\nThe subsequent private Alma resolver bundle passed actual filesystem/unit gates\nin 44 seconds, and the old exact image was rejected for missing resolver files.\nPinned exact-config sysusers execution created a locked nonroot resolver\nidentity, preserved every unrelated account row and was byte-idempotent on\nrepeat. The actual immutable image contains the expected resolver, sysusers and\nnologin ELF bytes. Alma's native systemd 257 parser accepted the exact pinned\n261 service, two sockets and actual identity unit/drop-in without unknown or\ninvalid settings or cycles. The extracted scratch retained eight missing\nexecutable diagnostics; parsing does not establish daemon execution or boot.\nThe matrix review records exact receipts. Fresh repaired-image lifecycle and\nproduction deployment retain separate scopes.\nThe later incoming Ziggy reporter history explicitly selects one generated\ncase, and two Galileo boot-refusal histories select two each with the CI\nprofile. Their remaining inner compatibility `max_runs` options are ignored;\nthese bounded counts reduce exploration and do not inherit the preserved-count\nclaim for the Marshall and protocol repairs above.\nThe later Ziggy context/refusal and Galileo pending/upstream histories also\nselect bounded one- or two-case CI runs. Their reduced exploration retains\nseparate execution and does not inherit preserved-count claims.\nPorsche's invalid-boot property now selects one generated CI history while\nstill visiting every configured refusal mutation and preserving its startup,\nfixture-custody and diagnostic-secrecy assertions. This reduces randomized\nhistory exploration; it does not establish a new passing execution record.\nThe later machine-pin integration adds the Access resource and deletion fence,\nthe protocol `pin`/`unpin` commands and Porsche's closed refusal hints under\n[ADR-0193](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0193-per-user-machine-pins.md).\nIts scenario evidence remains partial. Imported execution\nreports and the earlier checkout's frozen current-Hegel run do not establish\nthe combined source's consumer gates. Final frozen `132bc28b5` Marshall\n`mix precommit` subsequently passed 1,712 tests with four exclusions in\n1,413.8 seconds, seed 576982, Covenant 27/27, terminal/presentation JavaScript\n36/36 and 9/9, Monospace and forced Dialyzer with zero errors, skips or\nunnecessary skips in 51.78 seconds. The frozen runtime ledger verified\nunchanged after exit zero; subsequent fixture-script/CSS corrections retain\nseparate browser evidence. Lilly and Porsche full consumer gates remain\nseparate. The matrix review records exact log and ledger digests.\nThe final `132bc28b5` all-files prek gate passed, including Ruff, Ruff format\nand ty. The same source's two evaluated host catalogs and actual manifest\nvalidation match all seven selected immutable image identities. Prior artifact\ncustody and four completed NixOS, Ubuntu, FreeBSD and OmniOS lifecycle records\nremain applicable only to those exact unchanged images and their recorded\nexecution revisions. This adds no final control-plane runtime or repaired\nArch, AlmaLinux or Windows lifecycle claim; exact receipts are in the matrix\nreview.\nThe later fresh sealed Windows `8ca0a11f` fixture completed exact personal-account\nCLI SSH, stop with a separate current-owner stopped-state checkpoint, restart,\nrepeat SSH and terminal deleted cleanup at healthy `132bc28b5`. Its original\nwarning-only failure remains intact; only the exact stock Windows OpenSSH\nthree-line post-quantum warning is classified as expected, with no trust or\nalgorithm change. The matrix review binds all receipts and the 27-test/pinned\nPython classifier verification. At that checkpoint five families had separately\nscoped lifecycle evidence; the later Arch/Alma outcomes are recorded below.\nNo all-capability or native isolation claim follows.\nThe final protocol precommit passed 303 tests in 420.6 seconds, seed 476754,\nCovenant 14/14, shared contract tests 19/19 and Dialyzer with zero errors,\nskips or unnecessary skips in 2.77 seconds. Its frozen runtime source ledger\nchecked unchanged; Marshall, Lilly and Porsche retain separate final gates.\nFinal Lilly execution subsequently passed610 tests in62.1 seconds,\nseed132572, Covenant15/15 and zero-error forced Dialyzer in11.43 seconds;\nits runtime source ledger remained unchanged. Porsche's final gate passed\n100 tests in76.5 seconds, seed486608, Covenant4/4 and zero-error forced\nDialyzer in4.26 seconds; the same ledger verified after all four consumer\ngates completed. The original full local browser probe passed\nafter four narrow CSS, fixture machine-key and palette accessibility repairs,\nincluding153 screenshots and thread/collaborator revocation checks. These\nsynthetic browser results are separate from guest/package acceptance.\nThe reviewed PAM helper repair also passes actual image, independent rebuild,\nunit parser and isolated root-account nosuid checks for Arch `99fa9876`/Alma\n`473f9589`. Both exact images subsequently passed fresh readiness, strict\npersonal-account SSH, read-only sudo/helper checks, separate current-owner\nstopped checkpoints, restart with repeated SSH/checks and completed deletion\nin run `114223`. Both retained nosuid runtime mounts and exact pinned helper\ncustody. The matrix review binds all receipts and explains the isolated VM's\nsource-ownership overlay. These scoped observations do not establish direct\nhelper setuid elevation or broader guest capability/isolation acceptance.\nFresh exact-image OmniOS run `122228` then passed create/readiness, strict\ninitial personal-account SSH, stop with an independent current-owner stopped\ncheckpoint, same-VM restart, repeated strict SSH and completed deletion. Its\nsource-health holds retained the same UUID and are separate from OS failures.\nThe post-deletion 12:43:41 UTC observation records both hosts healthy at exact\n`d0eaddbc9`, zero updater failures, passing live checks and the exact selected\nseven-image catalog. The matrix review binds all hashes, including the typed\naggregate's 49 stage receipts, four Arch/Alma smoke receipts and 17 separate\nhistorical failures/holds. All seven selected families now satisfy that scoped\nSSH/lifecycle milestone at distinct execution revisions. Preparation timestamps\nand historical revision context are not exact execution times or observed\nactivated-host revisions. The result does not establish a simultaneous run or\nbroader native capability, revocation, isolation, physical recovery, relocation\nor reproducible full-image/kernel assembly. ADR-0190 and ADR-0191 remain\naccepted/partial; ADR-0171 remains proposed/partial.\nThe two earlier `101111` diagnostic cleanup requests remain separate from this\naggregate. Guarded manual retirement confirmed the original idle API process,\nnamespace and open-file holders gone using normal SIGTERM after two fresh\ncomplete idle proofs, with no force, unmount, storage removal or database\nwrites. Alma's original request then reached deleted. New external rootless\nPodman activity created a different namespace before Arch's retry completed;\nArch's original request remains accepted at that checkpoint. No active workload\nwas stopped and no replacement intent was submitted. The matrix review binds\nthe retirement journal. These observations do not establish arbitrary automatic\ncleanup or completion of the separate old Arch request.\nThe [testing guide](repo:docs/development/testing-with-hegel.md) and\n[verification review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-17-elixir-hegel-property-verification.md)\ndescribe the current API, exact execution evidence and remaining gaps."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9509
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The conversation is the main working area. User and agent messages have distinct\nrole labels; tool and turn events use compact, readable status lines. Queued\ntools show their names, and tool results show the recorded outcome when available.\nThe saved objective remains visible before transcript events arrive. When the\nworkspace is ready and the actor can write, the reply composer appears above the\ntranscript. Events show newest first, with the oldest at the bottom. A ready thread\noffers Open terminal to writers,\nwhich navigates to the bound machine's terminal page; the terminal is not embedded\nin the conversation. Open VS Code appears alongside it when a fresh authorized\nmachine projection reports the editor available, and opens the existing machine\neditor route in a new tab. That route rechecks current editor authority.\nManagers receive Archive and Delete in the Actions disclosure and Reopen on\narchived threads. Managers can archive or delete pending, provisioning, ready\nand failed threads; a viewer receives a read-only notice. Semantic markup, text\nstatus labels and streamed thread, activity and transcript collections use the existing monospace themes\nand character grid. List, detail and transcript failures show explicit\nunavailable states instead of appearing empty; missing or unavailable thread\ndetail refuses prompt and lifecycle events. The composer submits to the bound\nmachine-scoped agent thread; it never creates an independent control-plane\nexecution path. Pending provisioning shows preparation progress; failed\nprovisioning offers Retry to current managers. Polling refreshes asynchronous\nprovisioning state. A thread becomes ready\nonly after Compute reports its VM running and the initial objective is accepted.\nThe activity view shows dated rows with a status description and a status filter;\nit summarizes the latest thread state rather than a complete event history. Each\ncommitted event on the current bound agent thread advances the enclosing thread's\ndurable activity clock after the event transaction commits, so agent work moves\nthe row even when no user-thread attribute changed. Archived and deleting threads\nare not touched, and a stale or mismatched agent binding cannot advance a row.\nProject filtering remains separate work. The manager-only Share\ndisclosure lists the protected owner and a page of at most 50 grants, with controls\nto grant, replace or revoke a registered account's viewer/operator/manager role.\nIt loads those details only while open, preserves its request key across failures\nand clears grant controls if the read fails or management authority is lost.\nThread access also applies to its current machine; independent machine grants\nand team access keep their separate lifetime. See [access control](#access)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9513
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The corrected startup-release frozen snapshot subsequently passed both complete\nFirecracker fleet scripts: NixOS in 805 seconds and Ubuntu in 829 seconds total\nbuild plus native execution. Actual Marshall/Audi/Lilly opening, native policy\nand personal controls, broker/worker recovery, saved-data stop/start, current\nphysical readiness and Origin/sign-out refusal passed. This snapshot predates\nlater template changes; periodic sweeps are not validated by these results.\nFinal release 7d2415 complete standalone acceptance subsequently passed in\n342 seconds total build/execution, including lower/core/System refusals, newer\npersonal install/reversion, independent controls, real Rust/Elixir and\nbroker/worker recovery. Its local authorizer and TLS relay are fixture adapters;\nfinal-release Firecracker family runtime remains separate from passing image\nbuilds. Initial standalone/language failures and older scoped successes retain\ntheir original scope. Historical lower/equal scanner state remains unexecuted.\nBoth hosts later activated exact 63a1443c/release 73ad with matching new guest\nmanifests; that activation does not upgrade retained disks or establish a\nproduction browser journey. The current 13:06 existence-only check confirmed\nrecurring fixture files absent; production browser and recurring history remain\nunestablished."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9517
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The corrected startup-release snapshot now passes both complete Firecracker\nfamily scripts, with actual Marshall/Audi/Lilly login and browser tools, native\npolicy/personal controls, broker/worker recovery, saved-data restart and exact\ncurrent physical readiness. NixOS took 805 seconds and Ubuntu 829 seconds total\nbuild plus native execution. The final foreign-Origin/sign-out stages also\npassed. These results apply to their frozen pre-template snapshot and do not\nestablish periodic sweeps or production browser execution."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9521
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The current declared roster has twenty-two provisional seven-day objectives and\nforty-four burn evaluators. Both browser objectives are 99%, with the existing\nminimum-request, warmup, exact-host, fresh-collector and coverage guards. Missing\nfixture credentials, recorder or collector coverage yields unknown, not healthy.\nIndependent Prometheus recordings expose closed fixed-window diagnostics: at least three failures\nwithout a success in fifteen minutes, or no completed journey in ten minutes,\nonly after matching guarded coverage/warmup. They are observations of those windows,\nnot a persistent consecutive-failure counter. Seven-day eligibility, private\ncredential provisioning, production execution and external notification delivery\nremain separate acceptance in [scenario coverage](#coverage).\nNix provisions these two families in a guarded browser-observations table in\nGrafana. Diagnostic panel IDs derive after the full duration/objective roster,\navoiding collisions when browser objectives are added. Declaration/evaluation\ndoes not establish rendering, retained healthy history or recurring execution.\nMarshall's retired SLO reader remains absent. The direct NixOS helper result above\nprecedes integration with independent observability; merged native and Grafana\nbrowser-panel acceptance remain separately scoped."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9529
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The current illumos driver allocates fallible worker/queue resources before\n`DRIVER_OK` and refuses runtime detach. Activated DMA, queue, interrupt and worker\nstorage remains until VM disposal; a late activation failure withholds readiness\nand retains a bounded unavailable attachment. Firecracker's refused device reset\ncannot authorize reclaiming backend-visible storage. This is an independently\nreviewed source correction to the existing lifetime contract. The corrected\n`76b8c1ad` 26-object cross-build and all four host-test gates passed; native replay\nis still required.\nThis does not establish the cause of the earlier ZFS I/O errors.\nHost extraction/fault injection covers 236 preactivation failures, both late\nactivated failures and three rejected lifetime mutants, retaining all 65 DMA/\nchain pairs after activation. The block driver separately reserves worst-case\ndata plus two metadata descriptors per concurrent direct request, with 265,596\ngenerated helper vectors passing sanitizers. These remain host/source evidence;\nnative DMA safety and the original ZFS EIO cause are not established.\nCompletion recycles its bounded request/descriptors before blkdev can\nsynchronously resubmit, preserves the original callback error and never touches\nthe old request after callback entry. Completed external transfers return\nsubmission success after one callback, including polled errors; internal\ntransfers retain direct errors. The five-function host regression passed 161\nsanitized histories and rejected four actual-source mutants; this remains mock\nownership evidence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9533
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The dedicated SSH CA has a recovery-only SOPS document and a separate Overalls\nruntime bundle. Physical host SSH keys decrypt bundles; they are not the SSH CA.\nRecover only into private runtime storage and verify the derived public key\nagainst `secrets/ssh-host-ca.pub`. Changing SOPS recipients must preserve CA bytes.\nAn actual CA replacement requires independently verified explicit client trust\nreconciliation; neither the installer nor proxy silently accepts a new authority.\nReissue cached gateway and guest certificates under the replacement authority.\nRestarting a gateway retains a still-valid cached certificate, so signer\nreplacement and restart alone do not complete rotation.\nThe signer is online in Marshall, unlike TLS's root/intermediate arrangement."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9537
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The default [Compose fixture](repo:antithesis/config/docker-compose.yaml)\ndeclares PostgreSQL 18, migration, two Marshall processes, one controlled\nLilly, enrollment and workload bootstrap. The fixture's TLS, signing,\nencryption, database and host credentials are generated in private runtime\nvolumes. The workload receives only its CA, nonsecret fixture, SELECT-only\ndatabase role and a separate read-only Control credential copy. Missing or\ninconsistent retained credential state refuses startup. The configured hosted\npool has a typed identity but no measured native capacity; it never enables\nplacement admission. See [verification](#reference) for the exact evidence\nlimits and [security](#security) for authority boundaries.\nThe controlled Lilly container selects `cgroup: host` because the tested\nrootless Podman private cgroup namespace omitted `cpuset`. Its collector\nderives the process's actual unified cgroup v2 subtree from `/proc/self/cgroup`\nand reads that subtree and ancestors under `/sys/fs/cgroup`; it refuses a\nmissing, ambiguous, escaped or unreadable path. It does not substitute the\nconfigured native Lilly slice for the container's own limits. The direct\npreflight observed a host-view mount. A later local Compose run committed a\nfresh report from the controlled container with cgroup-derived effective CPU\nand memory fields; raw cgroup files were not independently compared. Its\npool capacity remained nil and execution readiness false.\nThe enrollment process selects Marshall operator mode with an endpoint\nconfigured `server: false`. It keeps Repo, Vault, authorization, PubSub and the\nnon-listening Endpoint needed for real WebAuthn origin/RP identity, while\nbackground jobs and serving listeners stay off. Its private fixture contains\nthree synthetic accounts and passkeys after the recorded local diagnostic.\nOptional Compose overlays select guest/inner-host, origin/SMTP, satellite,\nEnrico and recorder fixtures with their own state and credentials. A default\ncontrol-core setup signal does not validate these additional overlays.\n`compose.inner-private.yaml` selects the separate `private-host-vmm` context\nafter `compose.host-vmm.yaml`; its inner Audi/Ziggy/Galileo roles and guest\nimage use runtime PKI and a boot-bound trust receipt. The source-built fourth\nprivate host image booted directly under TCG, but this Docker variant has not\nstarted and its private roles lack serving credentials."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9541
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The default compactor produces a tool-free summary with progress, decisions,\nconstraints, remaining work and references. The summary has generated-context\nprovenance and cannot replace trusted application policy. Provider-native\ncompaction is enabled only for separately verified provider/model capability.\nThe deployment allowlist is `MARSHALL_AGENT_NATIVE_COMPACTION_CAPABILITIES`, a\nJSON array of exact `{provider, model, evidence_digest}` records. Marshall\ncurrently accepts only the `openai` provider, validates model syntax and a\nlowercase SHA-256 evidence digest, and matches the complete provider/model pair\nat queue and claim time. The legacy global boolean is refused when enabled;\nomitting the allowlist leaves native compaction disabled. A digest records the\noperator's evidence receipt but does not claim live provider acceptance by\nitself, so the decision remains partial until that evidence exists.\nBoth maintenance request forms disable tools and tool choice, so compaction\ncannot execute an ordinary side effect while producing continuation state.\nBoth paths use durable model calls through the existing Ziggy pull and accounting\nroute. A complete validated result is published only if its context generation\nand frontier still match. Failures, cancellation and stale results leave the\nprevious checkpoint intact. Ambiguous claimed provider work is never replayed\nautomatically."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9545
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The default control fixture generates synthetic TLS, database, signing,\nencryption and host credentials only after container startup into private\nruntime volumes. PostgreSQL migration uses the real release; the operator\nuses Marshall's reviewed enrollment and rotation CLI for the fixed Lilly\nidentity. The workload receives a CA, a nonsecret fixture, a read-only Control\ncredential copy and a SELECT-only PostgreSQL role. Its SQL oracle uses verified\nTLS, bound parameters and bounded reads. Missing, malformed or unavailable\noracle replies never count as empty authority state or successful setup."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9549
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The default-enabled `services.overallsOtelFleet.traceSpool` discovers exact enrolled\napplication units and gives each an independent producer-authenticated socket,\nprivate durable archive and ninety-day retention. `quotaBytes` defaults to two GiB\nper producer; `pendingQuotaBytes` limits pending admission to 256 MiB within that\narchive. `hostBudgetBytes` defaults to 32 GiB and refuses an overcommitted evaluated\nproducer inventory. Archives are separate from Tempo's quota; exhaustion refuses\nnew trace diagnostics without stopping a producer. Its read-only `producers`\ninventory drives source health. Native helpers' stdout protocols remain private\ndata interfaces."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9553
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The diagram shows logical boundaries, not independent physical failure domains.\nSeveral services may share one NixOS host. A PostgreSQL replica on the same\nphysical host does not protect against that host's loss."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9557
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The diagram summarizes progress. Retry and failure metadata do not bypass physical\nevidence or release holds."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9561
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The disposable cancellation fixture adds an armed recorder around Lilly's exact\nrecursive public-property ZFS query. It forwards native bytes and exit status,\nrecords no ownership/custom/token query, and retains a private bounded ring.\nA clone-only release seam pairs readiness reads with the exact validated expected\nsnapshot GUID map under the same pool lock; missing or changed context is explicit.\nFailed tests copy those samples before VM teardown to a private builder directory\noutside Nix outputs; `--keep-failed` is required to retain that directory. Truncated\nsamples cannot establish a complete accounting predicate. The eighth native run\nfailed queued activation with `source_unavailable` before physical capture. Six\ncomplete successful native reads were privately collected, all without readiness\ncontext; exact-map readiness replay and complete cancellation acceptance remain\npending. Retained source reports were older than the five-second guard at failure\ncompletion, but the exact activation-time conjunct is not established. The fixture\nnow waits after operator startup for actual new fresh host/pool receipts and settled\nsource operations, then calls the coordinator once. It preserves the accepted\nrequest, production guards and every subsequent refusal; no blind retry is added.\nThe ninth capture timed out during initial host admission, before source creation\nor this activation wait. Both hosts delivered some valid reports, while 31 execution\nexchanges received `invalid_report`; retained accepted observations cannot identify\nthe rejected predicate. No guard or resource requirement has been changed.\nInstrumentation changes timing, so these runs provide no performance acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9565
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The disposable fixture now instruments the actual authenticated report guards.\nIt preserves each predicate, ordering, short circuit, database clock and rollback,\nwhile privately retaining a finite rejected stage and signed sample age. Malformed\nshape has no age. Files survive transaction rollback; they describe a guard\nrejection, never a committed outcome. Each replica retains only its first rejection\nper stage during admission or cloning. This is not a host-attributed timeline.\nDedicated synthetic exchange and sink checks pass; native execution is pending.\nThe diagnostic cannot retrospectively classify the ninth failed capture."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9569
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The earlier corrected Ubuntu native capture passed cross-tab sign-in/required MFA,\nfour exact initial GitHub checkouts and retained stop/start without re-clone.\nTen additional GitHub/GitLab/Codeberg revision cases passed before Codeberg change\nstalled at clone status `cloning`; the run failed after 1,703 seconds. Bitbucket,\npublic downloads and CLI were not reached;\nhistorical ADR-0128 GitHub results retain their old schema/revision. The corrected\nruntime capture predates final frontend privacy and provider/ADR module-comment\ncorrections.\nAn exact dnsmasq 2.93/production-code musl probe isolated the fixture DNS failure:\nA succeeded but AAAA returned REFUSED; authoritative local-domain configuration\nreturned an empty NOERROR AAAA answer and API resolution succeeded with the same\ncaptured Ubuntu CA bundle plus a fresh fixture root. All four fixture domains now\nanswer locally, and fixed guest TLS/schema/fork diagnostics preserve private\nterminal bytes. This focused repair is separate from complete guest acceptance.\nThe repaired NixOS family completed all twelve subtests, exit 0 in 1,352 seconds\n(854.61-second test script): all twenty provider/revision combinations, exact\nguest-owned HEAD/shallow/done assertions, installed-CA provider API probes,\ncross-tab sign-in/required MFA, retained restart, public immutable HTTPS downloads\nand packaged CLI/working directory passed. These are fixed local provider fixtures\non the colocated native fleet. The earlier frozen-source Ubuntu run passed browser\nsign-in/MFA/dedupe in 23.04 seconds, then failed initial guest startup before any\ncheckout assertion, exit 1 after 266.734 seconds. Its exact failed predicate remains\nunproven. The final complete feature `a3d2b69c` Ubuntu capture passed all twelve\nsubtests, exit 0 in 1,713 seconds total build/execution, script 1,422.20 seconds:\ncross-tab resume/MFA/dedupe in 31.21 seconds, initial four actual checkouts in\n22.10 seconds, retained restart in 47.85 seconds and sixteen additional matrix\ncases in 964.11 seconds, twenty total. Installed-CA Codeberg/Bitbucket API/fork\nassertions, public immutable downloads in 0.36 seconds and real packaged CLI/\ncommand/workdir in 24.28 seconds passed. No identity-probe failure marker occurred;\nno readiness or retry behavior changed. ADR-0157 is implemented. NixOS predates\nthe diagnostic-only addition; neither capture reran merged main. Its scoped\ncomponent/schema/contracts/logging checks and Nix HLDD gate passed separately,\nthe latter in seven seconds. Live\nprovider network, production activation, Brady interception and installed-extension\nauthentication/guest composition remain separate validation limits. Normal fragment\ncapture composed through the final frontend, while browser assertions of sentinel\nremoval and capture storage/POST contents remain separately unrecorded.\nThe source2-era NixOS handbook renderer passed all 22\nsections, Ash/topology diagrams, navigation/hook removal, themes and revoked-reader\nrefusal in 388 seconds, before the final documentation and editor SQL corrections.\nEditor DNS publication and its native fixture require the same manifest4 as the shared\ndecoder; exact editor release and current publication authority remain required."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9581
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The exact admitted GET/HEAD of the packaged extension-worker iframe at the grant's\nsource commit permits same-origin framing for the upstream browser extension host.\nOther editor responses deny framing. Foreign commits, unversioned paths and\nsuffix paths cannot inherit that exception."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9585
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The exact handshake-fixed test image `aa669e51` passed ordinary transport,\nAPI pause/full-snapshot/resume reset and two-boot Lilly-policy isolation through\ndirect host-KVM maintained runners. Complete 25-service-check receipts remain\nbound to those boots and image. These manual fixtures are separate from formal\nNixOS nested-KVM gates. Supervised daemon recovery still fails after daemon\nreplacement. Separate complete services, three-boot/two-expansion growth and\nsame-disk process-crash runners passed on these exact bytes, including all 25\nservice and eight crash checks. The [matrix review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-guest-os-matrix.md)\nrecords full identities, test scope and remaining native/physical gaps.\n`native_admission=false` remains unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9589
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The exclusive root-owned lock and postflight precede atomic publication of a\ntyped numeric receipt in volatile `/var/run/overalls/root-growth.json`.\nBootstrap refuses without a current valid receipt; errors expose no utility\noutput. Native commands have120-second/output64-KiB bounds. FreeBSD vendor\nfirstboot growth is disabled so this is the only growth authority. Twelve pure\nchecks and cross-target Clippy passed after native4-KiB alignment correction.\nThree native FreeBSD QEMU boots passed one unchanged root and two2-GiB\nexpansions, increasing UFS capacity5,061,628 →7,098,392 →9,135,160KiB while\npreserving root UUID/start, nonroot GPT entries, retained home bytes and an\nindependent foreign disk. The later exact canonical-roots/SSH FreeBSD image\npassed the full Firecracker service/growth composition in 334 seconds: three\nclean boots, two 2-GiB increases and root bytes 5,183,107,072 →7,268,753,408\n→9,354,403,840, preserving those authorities and data. The final reviewed image\nwith blocking UFS suspension/resume passed the same three-boot growth boundary,\nwith exact receipts in the native review. The source-qualified OmniOS growth\nreplay is recorded separately; interrupted expansion remains open. This does not\nchange Lilly's physical pool/quota admission accounting or enable native images."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9593
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The existing actor reconciles under the same placement,\nepoch and generation; no VMM launch attempt is recorded until disk and bundle\nvalidation succeeds. Missing disks, invalid ownership and malformed bundles remain\nterminal. `vm.disk_readiness_failed` identifies the placement and a bounded stage:\ndataset identity/ownership, snapshot ownership, accounting, mounts, disk metadata\nor boot bundle. Transient contention does not emit that failure event."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9597
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The existing agent loop remains the execution authority for user threads. The\nhigh-level thread stores one closed mode (`low`, `medium`, `high`, or `ultra`);\nthe loop resolves that mode to validated model/reasoning configuration when it\nqueues each model call. PostgreSQL agent turns, model calls and execution\nfences remain authoritative, so a LiveView process or thread URL cannot bypass\nthe machine's current authority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9601
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The existing parser property now varies initial buffers and socket writes,\nchecks exact decoded payload/count and trailer cardinality, and synchronizes\nsender readiness. TCP write boundaries do not imply recv boundaries. The\npressure helper rename and fully-buffered listener race repairs changed fixtures\nonly; failed runs and precise evidence remain in the\n[history review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-02-large-egress-cache.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9605
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The existing terminal viewer rechecks on user/VM hints, input/resize and a\nfive-second backstop. Editor and SSH renewal use current write authority and\nexisting thirty-second leases; expiry remains a backstop when hints are lost.\nWhile Lilly reconnects, renewal applies these revocation checks first and only\nthen answers `unavailable`, so revocation stays conclusive in that window.\nThese are source-reviewed revocation paths, not a recorded native closure result\nfor thread sharing."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9609
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The experimental hosted Antithesis Lilly uses a separate compile-selected\n`:controlled` pool identity only to negotiate Control. Its report carries\n`capacity: nil`, unavailable pool observations and false execution readiness;\nthe controlled executor cannot turn a requested operation marker into disk,\nguest or cleanup proof. The optional guest executor likewise refuses every\nphysical callback until independent process, overlay, boot and recovery\ninventory are implemented. This fixture grants no admission credit and changes\nno ordinary ZFS pool policy. See the [foundation review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-harness-foundation.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9613
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The exported `auto-update-compatibility` check runs the existing fixed assertions\nand generated operator histories against the actual predicate and preflight.\nThe histories use isolated environment/process-discovery seams and verify local\nfixture preservation and finite refusals. They do not execute systemd discovery,\nsecret staging, migrations or activation. Recorded results and historical native\nupdate/credential-rotation scopes are kept in the\n[compatibility review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-updater-compatibility-histories.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9617
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The exported default builds measured a 4,311,171,072-byte NixOS rootfs with a\n7 GiB minimum disk and a 2,818,572,288-byte Ubuntu rootfs with a 5 GiB minimum.\nUbuntu's exact same-derivation rebuild reproduced identical bytes. The separate\nenrolled-host graph with platform roots also built: NixOS measured\n4,311,261,184 bytes and Ubuntu 2,818,572,288 bytes, with the same 7/5 GiB minima.\nThe integrated enrolled-host artifacts retain these sizes and minima; exact\nmanifest validation and a 72-second byte-identical Ubuntu rebuild passed.\nPublished `8930a70f` activated these artifacts healthy on both hosts, with fresh\nmatching image offers. Activation is separate from successful client acceptance.\nProvisioning and fixture sizing must read the\nactual manifest minima rather than assume these historical sizes. The existing\neditor capability already requires at least 2 GiB RAM; idle tool installation\ndoes not create an independent new memory floor."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9621
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The extension is installed in the user's browser. After the user saves a server\norigin, it requests optional access and registers the detector only for that exact\norigin. The detector's document marker is local presentation state: it can hide\nsetup affordances but cannot authorize a request, account, thread or machine.\nMissing, denied or withdrawn permission and detector failure leave setup visible.\nMarshall's existing package closure and HTTPS service serve its archives; there is\nno new listener, host role or signing service. The cookie holds input only and\nPostgreSQL owns admitted VM and source identity. Lilly's host-local boot boundary\nmediates inert source delivery;\nonly the guest reaches providers. A provider failure changes checkout status without\nfailing VM readiness. Cookie expiry/login loss, database admission failure, host\ncustody loss and provider availability are separate failure boundaries. Declared\npackage/service placement is not current production launch or native acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9625
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The extension opens Overalls with a source URL only after an explicit toolbar\nclick on the four supported HTTPS provider hosts. It sends the provider origin\nand path, strips query parameters and fragments, and never collects page\ncontents, credentials, history or telemetry."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9629
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The final `wz4r` kit includes current executor lease cancellation and the\ncorrected `/usr/sbin/sshd` path/OmniOS always-on PAM config. Fresh native\nassembly passed nine modules, 764 payload records, all three SMF validations,\nthe safe native SSH executable guard, stock startup/network removals, read-only\nreceipt extraction/export and clean administration shutdown. The sealed\n8,589,934,592-byte disk SHA256 is\n`e7fc0c04bccb4b443dd4bea2edcdabfa2d1481fee6b683891f4338b399625a41`,\nat `/tmp/overalls-omnios-native-ssh-config-final-artifact/`; exact kit/receipt\nidentities and logs are in the native review. Clone this read-only baseline\nbefore runtime checks. The historical `wz4r` baseline remains outside runtime\nqualification and admission; current source-qualified service, growth and\nprocess-crash receipts are recorded separately. Exact-image lease withdrawal,\ntransport/reset, isolation and image qualification remain pending. Native `sshd -t/-T` separately passed with\nsynthetic volatile host-key prerequisites, preserving PAM and public-key-only\nauthentication; parser acceptance proves no running SSH session or readiness."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9633
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The final focused Marshall source/snapshot/editor set passed 39 checks\n(3 properties, 36 tests). Pinned protocol precommit passed 260 checks; the corrected\nLilly full gate passed 549 checks, seed 582375, after the readiness handoff fix.\nBoth forced Dialyzer runs reported zero errors. Observability precommit passed\n11 checks and its current finite schema check passed without regeneration. Rust\npassed 123 tests and all-target Clippy. These component results retain their\ncaptured scope; final Marshall full validation is separately recorded.\nMarshall full precommit passed 1,454 checks (239 properties, 1,215 tests), seed\n80297, with four excluded; forced Dialyzer reported zero errors in 34.61 seconds.\nIts verification-only capture-test gate addition was directly run and passed.\nThe later diagnostic-only ZFS refinement passed 21 focused checks (7 properties,\n14 tests), seed 523473, then full Lilly 549 checks, seed 643895, and forced Dialyzer\nwith zero errors/skips in 13.12 seconds. Observability passed 11 tests, seed 765622,\nforced Dialyzer with zero errors/skips in 5.74 seconds and its trace-schema check.\nThe new identity-bracket event preserves all storage guards and retry behavior;\nits missing-path regression establishes fixed metadata/privacy and equal refusal,\nnot parent-accounting or native healthy-backing proof. Native event evidence must\nidentify the actual failed predicate before any causal conclusion."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9637
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The final isolated native clone journey also passed the prepared-receive window\nbefore dataset creation, malformed ownership/bundle refusals, same-generation\nrestart after adoption, independent writes and surviving-copy reads after source\ndeletion (854.79 seconds). Complete native and production execution scopes remain\nseparately recorded in the restart recovery review."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9641
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The firewall source registry holds only the Overalls and Dungarees addresses;\noperators reach the browser origin, Porsche and administrative SSH through\nTailscale. The disposable Grafana test certificate lasts ten\nyears to keep cached fixtures usable; production certificate policy is unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9645
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The firmware-booted Windows image ([ADR-0169](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0169-firmware-booted-windows-guests.md),\n[ADR-0170](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0170-lilly-firmware-guest-launch.md)) is a\nlaunchable guest without agents, and that is the boundary to keep explicit.\nIdentity: the image carries a documented bootstrap Administrator password and\nno account roster; nothing maps it onto GuestAccount identities. The placement\nidentity (placement, execution epoch, boot challenge, address, gateway, MAC)\nreaches the guest through Firecracker's MMDS, seeded from a jail-local\nroot-owned file before launch; it is the same identity a Linux guest reads from\nits kernel command line, so nothing new is exposed. Authorization: the protocol\nadmits exactly the eight-key version-5 manifest with the agent set `none` or\n`windows-v1`; every surface reads one authority, the protocol's capability\ntable ([ADR-0171](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0171-windows-guest-agents.md)),\nthrough Marshall's per-capability image facts and Lilly's driver: for `none`\nMarshall refuses terminals, agent turns, the agent executor, web search and SSH\nconnection details, refuses the Inception profile and measures only the\nphysical service level, and Lilly refuses readiness, terminals, workspace\nsources and Inception and reports the VM running but never `guest_ready`; for\n`windows-v1` the same gates open only for the capabilities the table grants\n(never the editor or Inception), and readiness, trust, terminals and workspace\ndelivery still require the guest to answer the boot-bound vsock protocols, so a\nguest without working agents earns nothing. A missing, corrupt or mismatched\nmanifest yields every capability false. Transport: through Lilly the guest runs\nunder the jailer, seccomp observation and the per-tap nftables guard like any\nLinux guest; MMDS frames never leave the VMM; the vsock device exists in the\njail only for an agent set that speaks the protocols, and the guest side is the\nvirtio-win `viosock` driver whose address family the agents discover rather than\nassume. The gate 1 probe runs as SYSTEM, carries no identity or credential\nmaterial and publishes only its own log. The gate 2 daemons run as SYSTEM\ntasks: the readiness daemon answers only host CID 2 after live probes and\ningests nothing from the peer; the trust daemon installs only a host request\nthat names this exact boot and whose roots parse and match their digest, writes\nthe bundle whole before naming it current, and never acknowledges a failed\ninstallation; both re-read MMDS with a fresh session token that is never logged\nor persisted, and the per-boot UUID lives only in a volatile registry key. SSH\non a Windows guest (gate 3) is public-key only: the roster agent pulls the\nwriters' registered keys from Marshall through the private route and writes one\nSYSTEM-owned key file per account that only that account can read, so a user's\nkey opens only that user's account; passwords are random and never recorded;\non a new boot the agent first stops `sshd` and clears prior authorized keys.\nIt starts a keyless listener only after roster reconciliation and a verified\nper-boot host certificate. Readiness requires both current-boot receipts and\na live service on every host poll. Ordinary key delivery still requires the\nrunning execution's completed provision/start operation; bootstrap does not\ngrant key authority. Same-boot retries preserve current keys. Account\nactivation requires the SID-bound registered profile after a bounded native\n`CreateProfile` call. Unlike Linux, acceptance is not bound to the live Audi session\n(Win32-OpenSSH cannot ask per connection); Audi's ingress remains the only path\nto port 22 and still authenticates the outer session. Login receipts come\nfrom sshd's file log, matched by fingerprint to the pulled keys and reported to\nthe same Marshall authority as Linux logins; the bootstrap Administrator is\nretired (random unrecorded password, disabled) after successful key delivery to\na nonempty roster, so the SAC serial login of ADR-0169 stops working on guests\nwith agents. The\nterminal daemon (gate 4) keeps the Linux terminald boundaries: host CID only,\na HELLO bound to this boot's challenge, placement and epoch (read from MMDS),\n128-bit attach tokens and no terminal bytes in logs. Its `root` session is\nLocalSystem, the Windows form of the host-admin PTY; every other session runs\nunder an S4U logon of an enabled roster account, and an unknown or disabled\nname is refused rather than mapped to another identity. Each session's\nprocesses live in a kill-on-close job, so kill and retirement end\ndescendants too. The operator launcher without those\nboundaries remains a spike tool and is not Lilly's path. Execution\nfencing and isolation: the VMM patches widen the vCPU seccomp filter by one\nioctl (`KVM_IOEVENTFD`) and add read-only devices and CPUID leaves; the Nix\nsandbox keeps the installation offline. Storage: the raw GPT disk fills the\nordinary `rootfs.ext4` slot of a per-VM dataset through the verified copy\npath. Recovery: restart adopts from the portable bundle without the image, as\nfor Linux guests; a broken guest is otherwise discarded."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9649
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The first production coding-client canary after `8930a70f` activation timed out\nbefore inference admission. Separate offline validation of the captured public\nchain with pinned WebPKI rejects its legacy extra `*.internal` SAN under the\nconstrained issuer, while the requested `llm.internal` name itself passes.\nADR-0111's same-root exact-name replacement has verified preparation and\nindependent encrypted replacement proofs; commit/push and live acceptance remain\nseparate. These facts do not\nrecover the timed-out guest's missing TLS stderr or explain the older native\ntemplate failures. See the [coding CLI review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-native-guest-coding-clis.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9653
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The fleet certificate lifecycle is an explicit machine:\n- **`ready`:** a validated, proven and acknowledged generation is serving.\n- **`degraded`:** a later failed or slow refresh keeps the proven generation\n  serving existing routes, instead of dropping every route and DNS record.\n- **`unavailable`:** reached only when the generation expires or is no longer\n  installed, or when an explicit HTTP 426 halts the worker (see the Audi\n  control-version refusal under services). A halted worker resets\n  the machine, so it serves no readiness until an operator restart."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9657
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The fleet module selects eight request recorders and exporters on Overalls and\nfive on Dungarees, matching enabled applications. Marshall, Enrico and Porsche\nrun only on Overalls; Audi's three roles, Brady and Galileo run on both hosts.\nThe standalone recorder module still defaults to an empty instance set. Both\nhosts run their recorders; the 2026-10-11 preflight found every recorder\ncheckpoint at version 3. Recorders accept only version 3; any other version\nrefuses startup with a `version` refusal and leaves the checkpoint unchanged\n(ADR-0159).\nThe native crash fixture independently supervises recorders and producers on\none test machine. This is a host-local\naccounting boundary, not a separate physical availability domain."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9661
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The fleet scrape cadence is ten seconds: 8,640 samples per day and 60,480 per\nseven-day window. Objective coverage and history-day diagnostics share that\ncadence definition. The two-minute synthetic long-history fixture deliberately\nuses 5,040 samples; it does not redefine production coverage. Retention, display\nrange and objective windows are independent."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9665
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The focused Linux [installer journey](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/installer-trust/driver.py)\nchecks HTTPS artifact delivery, CA trust and account configuration with the actual\nMarshall, Porsche and Lyra packages. Trust refusal preserves prior client and SSH\nstate. Generated pre-existing local SSH configuration properties check\nbyte-exact preservation of unmanaged content, exactly-one-marker refusal and\ntemp-file-free interruption boundaries. Full NixOS and Ubuntu guest connections\nare covered by the fleet journey. Native macOS installation remains a separate\nacceptance requirement.\nstate. The same journey records an account-key revocation refused after trust\ninstallation — trust setup precedes account proof, so a first-contact account\nrefusal leaves CA trust installed while the executable, managed SSH block, proved\nidentity and CLI configuration stay absent or byte-identical — and owner-scoped\nre-registration restoring the completing install. It also records four-way digest\nagreement across the installed bytes, checksum endpoint, manifest and a fresh\ndownload, an appended-byte artifact upgrade replacing the executable only after\nconfiguration success, served-digest-mismatch refusal before replacement\n(distinguished only by the stderr line, with a shared exit status), and\nPATH-shimmed unsupported-platform, Darwin artifact selection and shasum-fallback\nprobes; native macOS runtime acceptance remains external. The fleet user SSH\njourney records a revoke→probe→re-register cycle closing guest SSH and Porsche\naccount proof on both guest distributions. Full NixOS and Ubuntu guest\nconnections are covered by the fleet journey. Native macOS installation and\narbitrary pre-existing SSH configuration remain separate acceptance requirements."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9669
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The focused [native ZFS fixture](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/zfs-lifecycle/retained-bundle.py)\ntests an unsupported bundle version beside a valid owned disk, repeated fresh\nobservations, no credit/readiness refusal, unchanged marker bytes and continued\nrefusal of corrupt metadata, unsafe kernel permissions, a foreign nonce or\nunexpected files. The focused native test passed in 221.51 seconds, including\nrestoration and fixture cleanup; its exact derivation and scope are recorded in the\n[capacity review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-retained-bundle-capacity.md).\nIt boots no guest and establishes no production rollout, concurrent replacement\nor physical recovery result."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9673
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The follow-up OmniOS agent manifest keeps SMF's contract service model and\nignores only signal failures so the shell supervisor can replace an individually\nkilled daemon without SMF replacing all listeners. Core dumps, hardware errors\nand an empty contract retain the outer service restart boundary. Assembly and\nboot verification require this exact policy and refuse duplicate, broader or\ninstance-overriding settings. Listener close also wakes pending-handshake\nchildren on its port under the socket lock, letting accept refuse without a\ncompleted callback or another interrupt. The host sanitizer regression caught\na missing-wake mutant. The combined payload passed fresh native assembly,\nincluding native SMF validation, nine modules, 764 payload records and clean\nexport; the matrix review binds its sealed `45983bb7` disk and receipt.\nThat exact disk subsequently passed all five maintained direct host-KVM service,\ngrowth, Lilly-policy, same-boot daemon-replacement and process-SIGKILL scenarios,\nplus ordinary transport and one API pause/full-snapshot/resume reset event.\nThe reset receipt establishes pending-socket refusal and current-CID\nrevalidation, without service-restoration or DMA-safety claims. Final services\nand reset replays use explicit owned VMM log files, separate startup stderr and\nserial stdout, newline-complete JSON events and independent authenticated\nterminal-control deadlines. The fixture regression suite passed 87 checks;\nadditional controlled-clock and old-helper/parser mutants retain refusal.\nThese fixture corrections change no guest bytes or classify the retained\nearlier PTY incident. A killed shell supervisor while children survive\ncan leave a nonempty contract without a supervisor; recovery of that case is\nunverified; the later passing daemon-replacement history covers the individual\nchild-death boundary only."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9677
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The following current sources contain acceptance assertions; a declaration or\npassing static check is not recorded execution of the whole boundary."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9685
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The foreign Linux catalog entries for Arch Linux and AlmaLinux reuse the\nversion-4 kernel image assembly. Their deterministic distribution markers make\nthe identities distinct, and the evaluation check covers builder shape before\nstaging. This is structural evidence only; neither distribution has a native\nboot, recovery or independent-host acceptance claim here. Preliminary FreeBSD\nand OmniOS/illumos version-6 image packages and catalog entries exist without\nstandby metadata, but the rolling FreeBSD source endpoint is retired and\nreplaced by a checksum-pinned archive capture. Nix evaluates the pinned native\nreceipts, but their placeholder kernel artifacts make image construction stop\nbefore a stock disk is copied. The OmniOS row uses the explicit `illumosGuest`\nconstructor. Bundle version 3 is implemented and structurally tested, but\nsigned rollout and launch remain unrecorded."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9689
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The fresh standalone startup-package attempt passed both accounts' trust/default\nand explicit direnv stages, then refused a fresh lower EditorConfig installation\nbefore version/language/recovery completion. Lower/equal precedence requires\nbounded historical personal profile state. The narrow newer personal permission\ncorrection passed the final release 7d2415 package and 344 compiled membership\ndecisions, followed by complete native install/reversion and core/System refusal\nacceptance. Actual Workbench full UI/gallery behavior remains separate from the\n36 passing scratch method cases. A scoped writable-settings\nBob probe passed HOME/outside/direnv/disable/override controls on an older package.\nBoth initial actual Rust and Elixir diagnostic attempts failed. A later scratch\nElixir stage passed with a fixture-only immutable Hex/MIX_HOME closure while Rust\nstill failed Cargo arguments. A subsequent strict both-language scratch run\npassed in 36.61 seconds on old startup package 3zz, including actual rustc E0308.\nThe final-package complete standalone then passed both actual language stages,\ntheir personal marker custody and Elixir installer-cache absence. Its fixture\nauthorizer/relay establish no final Firecracker family or production runtime.\nProject-server activation and\nstatic extension presence must not be presented as working language support.\nTheir exact failures and remaining meaningful fixture checks are recorded in the\n[review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-editor.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9697
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The full fleet startup fixture extracts only the current Audi service's immutable\nconfiguration path through its privileged wrapper and rechecks the service PID.\nThe Audi UID validates that bounded canonical path and both real configuration\nand listener entrypoints inside the service's mount namespace. The one-label\nrefusal clone remains private; diagnostics expose only fixed stage names.\nThis fixture custody preserves product authority. Both gateways passed this\npreflight in the complete NixOS run; no broader production claim follows."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9701
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The full merged Marshall component gate passed, including forced typing. Both\nfresh Firecracker guest-family checks at `058f13d8` then failed. Their precise\nexecution records show that both initial cold editor/terminal/custody journeys\npassed before the real-guest policy phase failed. They remain separate from the\nearlier complete passing NixOS journeys below. At that revision the specialization's\ncomplete native acceptance remained open."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9705
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The gallery and Ed25519 signing-key ring are reviewed build arguments in\n[the package](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/vscode-web/default.nix), not mutable browser\npreferences. Changing source, patches, dependencies, gallery settings or keys\nchanges release identity. The guest image manifest must declare that exact tuple;\nan old or mismatched image refuses editor admission. Both guest builders embed\nthe complete package/broker closure offline. No first-boot installer or ambient\nNix channel participates. See [guest images](#guests) and [operations](#operations)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9709
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The generated enrollment/credential histories and the distributed registration\nand retirement schedules behind this procedure are recorded in the\n[host replacement review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-27-host-replacement.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9713
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The generator selects each enrolled `NAME` configuration; an inventory host without\none uses `NAME-base` and is marked accordingly. Enrollment-dependent roles remain\ndisabled in the base profile. The service allowlist lives in\n[the projection](repo:scripts/hldd/topology.nix); review it when adding a service."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9717
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The guest root broker checks the current boot and roster then starts each native\neditor under its personal UID with separate server/extension data and an owner-only\nUnix socket. Systemd owns workers independently from the broker. The upstream\nextension host supplies language tools, Git, search, filesystem and PTYs. Open VSX\nregistry VSIXs require the packaged Ed25519 trust keys before extraction; explicit\nlocal imports remain a user operation. See [interactive access](#interactive),\n[configuration](#configuration) and [recovery](#recovery)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9721
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The helper gives Chromium private writable configuration/cache directories while\npreserving HOME and the native tester's NSS trust database. Its wrapper uses pinned\nFontconfig/DejaVu, including on hosts without desktop fonts. The wrapper/closure\nbuild passed; actual authenticated browser completion is required before recording\na successful measurement, irrespective of that package result."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9725
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The historical Antithesis actor harness (`antithesis/README.md` at overalls revision `9fe1e493`, repository deleted)\nexercised duplicate-generation launch histories through Lilly's real command\nStore, VM actor and journal with a persistent fake executor. Its named-volume\nworkload observed replay across actor process lifetimes; it did not run Marshall\nexchange, a full Lilly runtime, or native KVM, systemd and ZFS execution. Its\n[architecture review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-28-antithesis-local-harness.md)\nretains the recorded local validation and untested hosted/native boundaries.\nThe old `antithesis/` tree is retired before a fresh whole-repository rebuild;\nthe [reset review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-reset.md)\nrecords the removal and retained evidence. No fresh hosted run or mutation\nsweep is established by this removal. The ordinary test fixture remains local\nto Lilly, independently of the retired Compose workload.\nLocal [generated actor properties](repo:lilly/test/vm/antithesis_actor_property_test.exs)\npassed replay/advance histories across actor lifetimes and a simulated\ninterruption after a durable fake launch marker. A further property kills the\nreal actor after the fake executor writes that marker and before it publishes\nrunning. The replacement fences the same generation without another fake\nlaunch. These local checks do not establish full Lilly process restart or native\nlaunch fencing.\nThe [native completed-effect scenario](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-lilly-native-actor-recovery.md)\nadds the real Executor and Firecracker/systemd/ZFS fixture to this recording\nwindow. It requires byte-identical attempt-only journals through actor death,\nunchanged native process identity and launch marker across recovery, and stable\noutcome replay. The combined native check passed in 671 seconds. Actual\nplacement-lock contention returned Executor retry with a persisted attempt,\nstopped unit and absent launch marker before confirmed native lock release.\nThe [worker BEAM extension](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-lilly-native-beam-recovery.md)\nadds abrupt whole-worker loss and independent source-built worker Store/lock\nreopens at the same recording boundary. It checks stable outcomes, native\nprocess/launch-marker identity and guest data access. Three new histories passed\nwithin the 1,040-second native check; the worker histories took 506.95 seconds.\nThe storage fixture disables\nthe production systemd daemon; it cannot establish that daemon's restart path.\nThis does not independently prove actor-deferred result consumption. Marshall\nexchange, authenticated activation and full daemon death at this boundary remain\nseparate.\nGenerated [actor ordering and supersession properties](repo:lilly/test/vm/actor_test.exs)\nalso inspect the persisted attempt before a blocked executor returns, then\ncheck that an older result cannot publish after a newer generation or epoch\nreplaces it. They exercise controlled executor tasks and durable journals,\nnot a native launcher or an actual process kill in that window."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9729
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The host Audiobookshelf binds to."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9730
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The host log is read separately from guest terminal admission. Current Cedar\nread authority, a fresh authenticated host stream and Lilly's exact current\nplacement/execution fences still apply when bootstrap is pending or failed.\nThis read neither requires nor enables a guest terminal capability, and sparse\nserial bytes cannot establish native kernel, agent, readiness or service support.\nEach page is bounded to 16 KiB; guest socket and boot-challenge authority remain\nprivate. See [the diagnostic read boundary](#interactive)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9734
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The identity layer is the generated host identity and enrolled credential;\nthe authorization layer is Marshall's committed host/session state and the\nSELECT-only oracle role. Verified TLS protects Marshall and PostgreSQL reads.\nCurrent v4 Control and fresh report sequence fence the bootstrap claim.\nIsolation stops at the controlled container boundary: synthetic pool identities\nprovide no ZFS ownership or physical capacity, and both controlled and guest\nexecutors refuse unobserved guest and cleanup outcomes. Retained volumes keep\nstate for restart tests but grant no recovery proof. Setup refuses missing\nsecrets, failed migration, unavailable endpoint, stale host report, absent\ncredential or uncertain native effect. The local base control-core setup and\nsix serial Control commands exercised these fixture layers, with 13 true SDK\nassertion records. The stale selected manifest and absence of hosted ingestion\nlimit that result; native effect refusal and recovery still need separate\nevidence in the [foundation review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-harness-foundation.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9738
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The illumos direct block queue now reserves data plus header/status descriptors\nfor every concurrent request and clamps DMA scatter before publication. A ring\ntoo small for one request refuses; negotiated indirect depth remains bounded.\nThe extracted production helper passed 265,596 generated arithmetic vectors with\nsanitizers. This source correction preserves physical pool/quota policy; no\ntrace or native A/B record establishes it as the cause of the initial ZFS EIO,\nand the corrected exact image still requires service/storage replay.\nCompletion also returns the request/descriptors before notifying blkdev, whose\ncallback may immediately submit another DMA window or queued request. It caches\nthe original result and never touches the recycled request after callback entry.\nAn external completion returns submission success after exactly one callback,\nincluding polled errors; internal transfers retain their direct error result.\nThe extracted five-function host regression passed 161 histories under strict\nC11 and sanitizers; four actual-source mutants were rejected. This source/mock\nevidence checks bounded-pool starvation, replacement ownership and duplicate\nerror completion. A paired direct-only QEMU diagnostic reaches console login on\ncorrected disk `a49255bd` under the same no-vsock device settings where disk\n`24318238` panics mounting root. This supports the completion correction for\nthat failure. Growth enters maintenance in the diagnostic configuration;\nFirecracker storage/service replay and original ZFS EIO causality remain open."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9742
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The illumos socket downcall waits for connection RESPONSE submission before\nreturning an accepted child. An immediate application close cannot overtake the\npending handshake; reset, listener closure and interruption refuse. The actual\nextracted downcall passed 256 forced host pthread schedules under ASan/UBSan,\nand a forced old-code mutant failed. This establishes bounded host control-flow\nevidence, with no native interrupt/DMA or admission claim."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9746
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The immutable seed retains the previous managed executable until its candidate\nregisters. A spawn failure or unsuccessful ninety-second registration result\nrestores the prior executable before restarting the executor. Boot reconciliation\nuses the installed bytes and saved applied identity; merely restarting systemd\nmust not leave an unexecutable candidate installed indefinitely. The existing\nregistration/status I/O error propagation remains outside this bounded repair.\nThe supervisor waits for graceful exit between commands before normal activation."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9750
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The installer rechecks identity and storage before formatting. Preserve the phase\njournal and installed receipt. An interrupted install requires inspection and\nexplicit erasure authorization again; it does not silently resume formatting.\nA completed plan cannot be reused to erase the host."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9754
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The integrated Brady precommit passed 80 checks (29 properties, 51 tests), seed\n`219616`, in 117.4 seconds; forced Dialyzer passed with zero errors, skips or\nunnecessary skips in 6.14 seconds. Six new local properties cover:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9758
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The later `075200` six-image run independently passed the complete CLI lifecycle\nand terminal deleted cleanup receipts for NixOS and Ubuntu. FreeBSD passed\nthrough restart and repeated exact-OS SSH; its original delete first remained\n`cleanup_failed` with unconfirmed ZFS namespace absence and a management\ntransport error. After fresh proof of no active unrelated containers, normal\nidle rootless Podman context retirement released the retained namespace.\nThe unchanged helper then completed the original request with a terminal\ndeleted receipt. No force, migration, database repair or replacement intent\nwas used. This fixture recovery is separate from independent physical cleanup\nand from the earlier `072900` cleanup result.\nOriginal Arch/Alma creates failed before init on stripped executable modes;\ntheir corrected artifacts have separate passing offline/cross-host evidence.\nThe later source repair restores root-owned `/tmp` and `/var/tmp` as real\n01777 directories and explicitly selects `lsm=landlock,yama,bpf` rather than\nactivating distro SELinux policy on a root assembled without its labels.\nActual final-image manifest/mode checks and old-input negative controls pass;\nthese offline checks do not establish repaired-image readiness or a live\nlifecycle. Host isolation and current execution authority remain unchanged.\nWindows created and reported ready, but actual CLI SSH failed before a banner;\nread-only inspection found stopped sshd, no TCP/22 listener and SSH-sync\n`pass_failed`. The retained fixture's ready flag is no SSH success evidence.\nA later manual `CreateProfile` diagnostic found the original 4,096-unit\nbuffer failed with Win32 1783; a 260-unit call registered the SID-bound profile,\nafter which the old agent enabled the account, installed a key and started\nsshd. The diagnostic mutated the disposable fixture and does not establish\nunchanged-image SSH or lifecycle acceptance.\nThe repaired exact `8ca0a11f` image subsequently passed actual installation and\nimmutable readback of all three repaired executables. Its sealed recursive NAR\nhas no references. Signed cache publication and exact NAR-fingerprint Ed25519\nverification passed. Independent fresh Dungarees substitution, full NAR contents\nand whole-artifact hashes passed, and source selection pins the reviewed bytes.\nThese publication records alone do not verify a clean CLI lifecycle. The later\nfresh `103842` fixture passed the exact repaired image's strict personal-account\nSSH, stop with an independent current-owner checkpoint, restart, repeated SSH\nand completed deletion. The old mutated fixture does not verify those new bytes.\nThe [matrix review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-guest-os-matrix.md)\nrecords exact run identities, historical failures and later lifecycle stages.\nThe separate exact-promoted OmniOS fixture then completed creation. Its first\nCLI transport succeeded but the OS probe omitted the real `v11` release label\nand failed the oracle. After narrowly normalizing that label while retaining\nthe exact revision, the same 16 GiB VM passed initial CLI identity, orderly stop,\nrestart, repeated strict CLI identity and terminal deleted cleanup. Stop has a\nterminal stopped receipt; no separate pre-restart show-state or physical\ncheckpoint was recorded in that original `091638` journey. Fresh `122228`\nseparately adds an independent current-owner stopped-state checkpoint before\nrestart and completes deletion, without establishing physical absence. The\noriginal failed oracle receipt is retained.\nThe final `101111` custody proof retains Ubuntu's unchanged exact image and\nearlier full lifecycle. The changed NixOS image passed its complete fresh CLI\nlifecycle, including a separate stopped-state checkpoint and deleted cleanup.\nFinal Arch/Alma creates still failed. Their actual-image and independent\nrebuild results establish artifact custody separately from runtime acceptance.\nArch's exact same-boot diagnosis found vendor tmpfiles changed root from\nimmutable 0755 to live 0555, refusing capability-free readiness writes. The\nArch-only higher-priority root policy now retains 0755; actual artifact gates,\nthe old-image negative and pinned tmpfiles scratch execution passed. These\nchecks preceded the later helper-fixed lifecycle recorded below. Alma's exact\nfailed fixture had no `systemd-resolved.service`, leaving dependent\nDNS/readiness/SSH inactive;\nits original create failure remains unchanged. The private Alma repair installs\nthe pinned resolver service, two sockets, D-Bus activation/policy and a scoped\nidentity bootstrap before sysinit/D-Bus/resolver startup. Actual artifact,\nexact-config sysusers and native unit-parser checks pass; the later helper-fixed\nruntime journey supplies separate boot/CLI evidence below.\nThe final historical cleanup census records all 15 known fixtures deleted\nafter strict idle rootless-namespace retirement. Both service and pause scope\nreceived guarded normal SIGTERM with no force, migration, unmount, storage\nremoval or database writes. Current foreign diagnostic fixtures are separate.\nThis manual operational recovery does not establish arbitrary automatic or\nindependent physical cleanup."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9762
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The later `d0eaddbc9` import broadens only Lilly's refused-SSH unit-test\nobservation to include the already typed closed consumer-failure atom. Its\nzero-progress and transfer-lock reacquisition checks remain. The separate\npinned transfer-file run passed five tests in 1.1 seconds; the matrix review\nbinds its log digest. The earlier frozen 610-test gate does not verify this\nchanged test source. No production runtime, native transfer or recovery\nbehavior changes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9766
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The later guest-editor integration through `9e7f6bd3` regenerates the combined\ntrace vocabulary with `audi.http_proxy_failed`, closed proxy phases,\n`response_state` and bounded `response_events`. The generated additions name\ncatalogued fields and finite atoms only; no URL, header value, raw error,\ncredential or terminal payload is admitted. This repairs generated-schema drift\nwithout changing proxy transport or authority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9774
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The machine keeps its identity, name, sharing, private address, image and requested\nshape. A running machine may have bounded downtime while final disk state and\nexecution authority move. `request cancel move-1` is available only before handoff\ncommits. If destination activation or readiness fails after commit,\n`request retry move-1` retries that retained destination. Both controls create\ntheir own durable request receipt; use a distinct `--request-id` for each control."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9778
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The machine owner's pin is also a deletion fence, described in\n[machine lifecycle](#lifecycle): every delete path refuses `machine_pinned`\nwhile the owner holds a live `pinned` row, and the owner's own account deletion\nis the only exception. Account cleanup archives the departing user's pins on\nother people's machines through the resource action, keeping their history.\nPins hold the Pin domain's `coordinate` authority only through\n`Marshall.Access.Pins`; users reach them through the public `pin_machine` and\n`unpin_machine` actions, the browser toggles and the Porsche `pin`/`unpin`\ncommands. Evidence is BEAM-level: the generated\n[pin histories](repo:marshall/test/marshall/access/pins_property_test.exs) and\n[examples](repo:marshall/test/marshall/access/pins_test.exs); see\n[scenario coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9782
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The machine page offers **Open VS Code** only when desired and observed state are\nboth running, its immutable image has the current editor capability, and the\ncurrent write/profile and Customer owner policy allows it. Valid older or\nnoncurrent images show that a new machine using the current image is required;\nmissing or corrupt image/configuration reports temporary unavailability. Host\ndeployment, restarting, resizing or cloning that old machine does not upgrade its\nretained image. Keep the original machine while copying and verifying selected\nfiles in a new one; see [operations](#operations)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9786
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The machine workspace opens personal terminals. Marshall owns authorization and\ncreation receipts; guest terminald owns PTYs and bounded streams. A browser is an\nattachment, not the terminal process. Reconnection must not replay input whose\nreceipt is uncertain."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9790
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The management reference below is generated at compilation from the same catalog\nas the SSH parser. `--help` and the command grammar remain authoritative for the\ninvocation being used. Commands operate under current authorization; examples\nnever supply credentials as arguments."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9794
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The marker's positive path and denied/withdrawn permission path require Chrome and\nFirefox browser execution evidence. Unit and package checks establish exact\nregistration and asset shape only; they do not prove browser permission prompts,\nFirefox runtime injection or deployed TLS. See the\n[presence review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-browser-extension-presence.md)\nand [coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9798
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The matching provision/start physical outcome carries `guest_ready: true` only\nfollowing successful current-boot guest readiness. Marshall's immutable service\nlevel measurements read that exact VM/placement/host/epoch/generation receipt at\nits database arrival time. Old retained outcomes without the flag remain unknown\nfor readiness; historical remote transition events cannot fill the gap. Missing\nreadiness by the original deadline remains a finished measurement failure."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9802
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The multi-GiB acceptance extension adds native fixed/chunked sizes through 4 GiB\nplus one byte, offline ranges at signed/unsigned 32-bit boundaries and four\n4 GiB concurrent readers with existing RSS bounds. Wide accounting properties\nand bounded live checkpoint jobs retain exact capacity and current-owner proof.\nNo cache configuration or provider exception is added. The final native check\npassed through 4 GiB + 1 byte in 1768 seconds. Baseline live NixOS/Ubuntu\nacceptance reused a 7,487,562,572-byte public checkpoint with exact independent\nSHA-256 and current-owner/generation hit records, documented in the [extension review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-multi-gib-egress-cache.md); smaller historical passes do not establish this scope."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9806
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The multi-OS guest contract keeps native FreeBSD and OmniOS/illumos image\nidentities explicit while their capability rows fail closed. Their current\nartifacts are agentless firmware images: Lilly omits vsock and native manifests\ncannot declare a workspace source until a reviewed guest daemon and machine\nevidence exist. Arch and AlmaLinux use the generic Linux runtime closure, with\noffline evaluation assertions for the packaged Lyra, terminal, trust and SSH\nservices; those checks do not establish a native boot."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9810
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The native Anthropic POST routes `/v1/messages` and `/v1/messages/count_tokens`\naccept either no query or the literal `beta=true`, including their matching\n`/anthropic` prefixes. Ziggy retains a closed query type and appends only the\nvalidated beta query to the upstream URL. Native body bytes and permitted\nAnthropic headers are preserved; accounting still uses query-free `messages`\nand `count_tokens`. Other query values, duplicates, extra parameters, encoded\naliases, provider-prefix mismatches and queries on OpenAI routes refuse before\nprovider effects. The [route property](repo:ziggy/test/route_test.exs) checks this\nbounded dispatch, rather than establish native transport by itself."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9814
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The native OSS clone fixture derives a CPU shape exceeding half the uniformly\nobserved host core count. A real source restart hold then makes a same-host copy\ninadmissible; fresh measured headroom must admit the shape on both hosts before\ncreation. This preserves the cross-host assertion without fabricated capacity.\nThe corrected expanded cloning check remains pending."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9818
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The native isolation fixture uses the packaged Lilly router and TAP source guard\nwith validated synthetic identities. Public, sibling, private, protected-uplink,\ngateway and unregistered-service TCP sinks must all be reachable before rule\ninstallation. Under policy, public traffic and private SSH authority must remain\nusable, forbidden targets must refuse, and a foreign-source attempt must\nincrement the actual TAP guard's drop counter before the assigned source\nrecovers. The composed NixOS driver, Linux rule installation/counter smoke,\nRuff/ty and three parser boundary tests passed. The exact canonical-roots/SSH\nFreeBSD candidate then passed two clean boots with independent policy receipts,\nreal sink controls, guard drop counters and assigned-source recovery. This\nestablishes the stated native IPv4/router/TAP scope; OmniOS, jail, physical-host\nand multi-host isolation remain unproven.\nThe sibling target is a Linux TCP sink behind the test peer uplink; direct\nnative-VM peer isolation is also unexecuted."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9822
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The native recovery check observes three row-lock waiters before client-machine\nloss and preserves the 20-second holder/35-second total reclamation assertions.\nThe combined correction passed a captured candidate, with the adopted recovery\nexecutable inputs reviewed against that capture. Complete integrated NixOS and\nUbuntu terminal journeys separately passed machine-loss recovery, retained\neffects and revocation. Physical/managed-provider acceptance remains separate. See\n[ADR-0126](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0126-bounded-database-client-recovery.md)\nand the [verification reference](#reference)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9826
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The native root helper accepts no target argument and runs only as root before\nMMDS identity/network initialization. It derives the actual mounted root and\nchecks physical capacity, both GPT headers/arrays and CRCs, exact pinned disk\nand partition GUIDs, partition starts and nonoverlap before mutation. FreeBSD\nrecovers the backup GPT, enlarges partition5 and runs UFS growfs; it preserves\nthe root UUID/start and every nonroot entry. OmniOS additionally requires the\nassembly receipt's dataset/pool GUID and exactly one whole-disk ZFS vdev, then\nuses `zpool online -e`, preserving loader and reserved-tail identities.\nFor a whole-disk OmniOS vdev, the validated `s1` partition remains the authority\nidentity, `p0` is the raw GPT preflight device, and the expansion operand is the\nunsliced disk. The pinned libzfs lookup strips `s1` for whole-disk vdevs.\nA host-direct Firecracker diagnostic on a disposable `76b8` clone reproduced\n`s1` refusal and unsliced success. The typed operand regression and fresh\ncorrected-kit native assembly passed; disk `bea32fe6` and its assembly receipt\nare bound in the native review. Exact-image replay is still required before\nclaiming automatic growth or service acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9830
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The native test's trusted browser uses the real package/broker but a synthetic\ngateway and roster; its result cannot replace the full authenticated NixOS/Ubuntu\nFirecracker journey. Coordinate approved package configuration and image manifests\nacross Marshall and Audi, then install/register exact image artifacts before\nadmission. Do not relabel old manifests or infer capability from installed host\nsoftware. Existing writable disks remain on their original image capability."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9834
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The nesting describes checks along a sensitive path, not a claim that all services\nshare one linear call chain. Recovery must reconstruct every relevant layer.\nAdministrative host root remains a trusted privileged boundary; the guest controls\nneither that authority nor the control-plane database."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9838
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The new Arch/Alma image packaging uses these same layers. Their earlier\neditor-free manifests remain refused, even though CLI SSH succeeds. Final-image\nclosure checks and a newly displayed editor control cannot substitute for a\nrecorded personal browser journey. The\n[foreign Linux review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-foreign-linux-browser-editor.md)\nrecords all twelve passing Alma native browser subtests and both new production\nCLI lifecycles separately. The native fixture checks recovery, current-boot\nrestart, foreign-Origin refusal and sign-out; its private roots and policy files\ngive it a different immutable image ID. Arch's same-image diagnostic replay\npassed all twelve stages, while its earlier broker-restart 502 remains unexplained.\nThe later trunk merge, production\nbrowser sign-in and physical isolation remain separate evidence boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9842
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The new growth service runs after local filesystems and before native bootstrap\non every boot, with a mandatory volatile mounted-root receipt. Its production\nbinary expands the target set to ten per OS; the exact twenty-executable native\npackage cross-build passed. Three native FreeBSD QEMU boots passed unchanged\nroot plus two expansions and retained-data/GPT/foreign-disk checks. Final\nFirecracker composition and the source-qualified OmniOS service/growth replay are recorded separately; interrupted growth and native isolation remain pending. The native isolation\nmachine driver builds and uses actual packaged\nLilly router/TAP rules; a Linux nft smoke passed, while the native two-boot\nscenario remains unexecuted. These are disposable verification programs, not new\ndeployed hosts or application domains."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9846
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The newer FreeBSD transport replay passed in139seconds against the UART-patched\nproduction VMM, retaining exact image/firmware/VMM hashes and admissionfalse.\nThis establishes token/CID/ABI/refusal/credit-window scope; full services and\nOmniOS transport remain separate acceptance work."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9850
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The ninth native capture failed initial host admission before recording or clone\nactivation. Its private offline database copy retained three accepted reports,\ncorrect host/storage identities and no VMs or clone requests. Rejected report\nbodies and their validation clock were not retained; accepted sample timing does\nnot establish their failed predicate or overload causality. The original image\nremains unchanged; admission and complete cancellation acceptance remain pending."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9854
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The no-op predicate does not independently inspect OpenSSL subject-hash links\nor hook outputs. Native rotation verifies CApath after actual rebuilds; isolated\ndrift of those uninspected outputs remains outside that predicate's proven scope."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9858
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The observability cutover migration revoked live monitor credentials. Under\nADR-0159 Marshall no longer models the retired monitor hosts, credentials or\nper-VM certificate issuance archive; their rows were exported (digest in the\n[removal review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-11-legacy-compatibility-removal.md))\nbefore the tables were dropped, and the administrative operations page shows\nneither panel. The cutover migration cancelled only `available`, `scheduled`, `executing` and `retryable` jobs belonging\nto the retired `Marshall.Fleet.Workers.ExpireHistory` and\n`Marshall.Telemetry.Workers.ExpireSummaries` workers. Completed history and jobs\nfor lifecycle, business or security work remain intact. ADR-0159 pruned the\napplied migration file; no rollback restores credential or host authority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9862
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The opt-in [HTTPS egress acceptance](repo:docs/acceptance/egress-acceptance.md) uses three\nreceipt-bound disposable guests on Overalls and records private command, request\nand updater evidence. Its pinned public destination has a positive reachability\ncontrol before Customer refusal. Seven runner safety tests cover ownership,\nreplay, failed responses, approval redaction and local SSH/proxy timeout cleanup;\nthey cannot establish native isolation. A local timeout does not cancel admitted\nVM work: reconcile the original request.\nCustomer refusal requires connection failure or timeout against the independently\nreachable pinned public address; TLS handshake or receive errors fail the probe.\nThe `brady-network` and\n`brady-native-storage` machine checks keep shared-service outages and pressure\nfaults isolated, including HTTPS full/range recovery with the origin stopped."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9866
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The opt-in [Porsche matrix runner](repo:scripts/porsche_acceptance.py) collects\nseparate live evidence using explicit immutable image IDs and exact expected\ncatalog names. Its default selection requires all seven families; missing or\nunadmitted OmniOS fails preflight before any creation. Explicit `--os` subsets\nretain their narrower scope. It binds each fixture to durable create receipts,\nrechecks current UUID/name/image/profile/owner/SSH endpoint before new mutations\nor guest access, and reconciles uncertain lifecycle work with its original\nbounded request ID. Accepted UUIDs persist before create polling, and failed\nterminal receipts remain available for owned cleanup without another creation.\nGuest probes use the installed Overalls CLI, strict pinned\nSSH trust and the authenticated personal account. Exact Linux distribution,\nFreeBSD release, OmniOS release or Windows edition/build output is required;\nraw probe output is not written to evidence. Stop/start/delete receipts and a\nsecond SSH stage retain separate outcomes. Configuration and resume procedures\nare in the [acceptance guide](repo:docs/acceptance/porsche-acceptance.md).\nWindows alone may emit the exact stock three-line OpenSSH post-quantum\nkey-exchange warning with LF or CRLF endings. Attempts record the closed stderr\nclassification and digest; the runner changes no trust or algorithm settings.\nExtra, modified or repeated stderr, another guest family, nonzero exit,\ntimeout or a wrong edition/build still fails. This classification does not\nestablish post-quantum transport or promote a prior failed attempt.\nImplementation and host-side tests do not establish a live seven-OS pass,\nnative attestation, physical isolation, storage growth or multi-host recovery.\nThe 2026-10-18 live subset created NixOS, Ubuntu and exact promoted FreeBSD\nfixtures, passed their initial exact OS oracle through CLI SSH and orderly-stopped\nall three at generation 2, then passed restart and repeat exact-OS CLI SSH.\nFinal cleanup returned deleted receipts for all three owned fixtures. Earlier quarantined create fixtures were reconciled and cleaned\nwith their original bound identities; those failed creates prove no guest boot."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9870
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The optional Antithesis inner host measures a fixed virtual ZFS member and\npublishes its pool identity for reviewed enrollment before Lilly can become\nready. Its composed path remains unexecuted. A separate\n[local storage-router TCG smoke](repo:antithesis/verification/storage-boot.md)\npassed first creation of a real Linux ZFS mirror, origin-off HTTP/HTTPS cache\nreplay, QMP reset, retained pool/parent GUIDs and exact 41-byte marker, and\nreapplication of Brady's 64 MiB quota/refquota. This direct emulator result\ndoes not establish physical power-loss durability or production guest\nattribution.\nThe [integrated-host boot record](repo:antithesis/verification/integrated-host-boot.json)\nadds a local third-image TCG host that sampled a real ZFS dataset/pool GUID,\nfree/used/available bytes and kernel cgroup CPU/memory values. Two earlier\nboots reported unavailable health. The passing sample has `connected: false`\nand `execution_ready: false`, so it cannot grant Marshall capacity admission.\nThe later fourth private-service image repeated real ZFS/cgroup sampling and\ncompleted runtime PKI preparation, but stayed disconnected and unready. The\ndefault controlled container still reports unavailable pools and nil capacity.\nA later local Compose diagnostic committed its cgroup-derived effective fields\n(32 cores and 134558031872 memory bytes) in a fresh v4 host report while\nexecution readiness stayed false. Raw cgroup file values were not independently\ncompared; this report grants no physical storage or guest admission credit."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9874
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The optional FreeBSD/OmniOS recovery fixture observes one real flush-daemon\nfailure within the same boot. Managed SSH verifies the root-owned PID marker,\nsaved executable mode/hash and live process. The fixture removes execute\npermission, kills and confirms the daemon reaped, then requires three refusals\nover the still-live readiness channel. A finally block restores the saved mode;\nthe supervisor must create a different PID with identical bytes/mode and boot\nidentity before real flush/refusal and readiness checks resume."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9878
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The optional TCG guest TAP runs directly in its inner Lilly host's network\nnamespace. In the authored private-service variant, Audi can see an actual\npre-NAT guest peer and Lilly's exact current-boot proof. The base Compose\nfixture has not admitted a nested guest; its connected controlled host has no\nreport. This harness does not exercise production namespace/nft anti-spoof\npolicy or adversarial guest packet isolation. A cooperative terminal or\nprivate-service exchange would retain that limitation even if it later passes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9882
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The optional same-boot daemon-recovery fixture must observe the real flush\nprocess killed/reaped and readiness withheld while restart is disabled, then\nrestore exact mode/bytes under a new supervised PID before current-boot flush\nand readiness resume. Four host boundary tests passed. The vendor-base and final\nreviewed FreeBSD images both passed the complete two-boot failure/refusal/\nrestoration boundary with exact per-boot receipts. The distinct abrupt-crash fixture must\nobserve VMM SIGKILL, new identity, retained bytes, native filesystem checking and\nold-authority refusal; its seven host checks pass. Actual crash execution exposed\nscheduled-only UFS sync. The corrected native suspension/resume flush passed the\nfinal FreeBSD observed-SIGKILL/same-disk restart boundary with exact retained bytes,\nnative UFS snapshot fsck and complete service oracles before/after the crash.\nThe final exact OmniOS image separately passed supervised daemon kill/reap,\nthree withheld-readiness probes and same-boot restoration, as well as SIGKILL\nand same-disk recovery. Those receipts remain distinct from the earlier failed\ncandidate. Neither fixture establishes physical stable-media/host power-loss\ndurability or fleet recovery."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9886
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The page shows each key's label, type, fingerprint and creation time. Remove a key\nto revoke its access. You may remove your last SSH key and continue using email\nlogin. Adding the same public key again creates a new registration; previous\nsessions remain revoked."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9890
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The patched Firecracker build is required for native firmware guests. Its\nvirtio PCI transport keeps the queue BAR and the MSI-X table/PBA BAR independent,\ncommits 64-bit relocation only after the high-half write and moves queue\nioevents only after successful registration. Hot-unplug removes both BARs and\nall ioevents. During illumos initialization, valid queue size/address writes are\naccepted while status is zero; malformed queue state and writes after a\nforbidden transition remain refused. Firmware may notify a queue through\nPCI configuration space before BAR MMIO registration; the reviewed handler now\nsignals the same queue eventfd as ordinary MMIO, with bounds and write-width\nchecks. Firecracker's selected patch tests and common vsock property checks\npassed independently of native guest acceptance. Exploratory OmniOS boots with\nthe modern virtio modules reach userspace in QEMU; the Firecracker scratch boot\nand native CID-bound transport remain unresolved. These disposable console\nexperiments do not establish reproducible image construction, target service\nstartup, reset safety or recovery."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9894
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The personal browser editor is built from pinned official Microsoft Code OSS\nsource with a maintained patch stack. Marshall grants current account write\nauthority; Audi serves the account origin, Lilly fences a dedicated connector,\nand the guest broker runs the native editor under that account's UID. Both image\nbuilders embed its immutable release capability. The complete authenticated\nNixOS Firecracker journey passed; Ubuntu provisioned and reached the correct\neditor origin but failed initial workbench visibility. These records precede the\nmerged independent-observability revision; current acceptance stays separately\nscoped. See [interactive access](#interactive),\n[guest images](#guests) and [scenario coverage](#coverage) for rollout and native gaps."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9898
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The pinned xterm renderer carries a reviewed local accessibility layout correction.\nRow text and dimensions update together, natural widths are measured without\nintervening writes, then each row is scaled to its logical columns. Screen reader\ntext, selection mapping, resize and announcement scheduling remain enabled. The\nactual vendor-method regressions pass for mixed-width/blank text, partial refresh\nand resizing. Both complete native guest journeys passed fifty shells, 10,000\nimages (310,000 bytes), accessible completion within 120 seconds, reconnect,\nretained shell and mixed-width accessible selection before graphics and after\nreplay. Whole-Marshall recovery retained the shell effect and met the existing\ndatabase and restored-live bounds. Sharing, foreign refusal, operator view-only\ninput refusal and grant/login revocation passed. Earlier recovery failures retain\ntheir captured scope in the review. These tests exercise the accessible DOM and\nxterm selection; separate assistive-technology integration remains unverified.\nSee the [scoped execution record](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-terminal-workspace-verification.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9902
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The previously sealed `wz4r` disk was assembled from a stale cached kit: its\nreceipt still carries the old `/workspace/*` Git ownership configuration and\npre-fencing workspace receiver. The current source-derived kit is\n`/nix/store/w5mg2jw3c2sispdkfg1r9ys5vxm41a97-overalls-omnios-native-image-candidate-kit`\nand has not yet been assembled into a replacement disk. Consequently the\nhistorical `wz4r` hashes below do not qualify current workspace, lease, service,\ngrowth or recovery behavior; OmniOS admission remains disabled."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9906
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The prewarm process schedules another sweep ten minutes after each completed\nsweep, retrying transient lease or pool-lock contention without a Lilly restart.\nThe same GenServer serializes these attempts; initial prewarming can delay a\nqueued sweep. The catalogued `lilly.template_sweep_finished` event reports only\nthe pool UUID, closed outcome and first failing stage (`bind`, `record`, `owner`,\n`remount`, `retire`, `storage_busy` or `template_unconfirmed`; success uses `none`).\nPartial retirement still attempts the other incarnations. Periodic contention\nretry has no new recorded native or property execution at this checkpoint."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9910
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The protocol library also provides the shared primitives for liveness and\nauthority under ADR-0140:\n- closed transition-table state machines that fail compilation when malformed and\n  refuse undeclared transitions;\n- a single-flight cadence. Periodic ticks that arrive during a run are dropped,\n  so periodic work always rests between runs and cannot hold shared locks back\n  to back. An explicit request during a run starts exactly one follow-up;\n- a single-writer, versioned in-memory view that readers consult without locks."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9914
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The protocol's native FreeBSD and OmniOS rows (`freebsd-v1` and `illumos-v1`)\nare explicit, unqualified all-false capability contracts. Native image\npackages and catalog entries exist, and the rolling FreeBSD source endpoint is\nretired and replaced by a checksum-pinned archive capture; Nix evaluates the\nreceipt inputs, but placeholder kernels stop image construction before any\nstock disk can be admitted. Complete image qualification separately enables\nreadiness, trust, terminal, workspace, flush, SSH and agents only for the reviewed\nsealed FreeBSD and OmniOS manifests or their exact portable descriptors. Editor\nand Inception remain false. A matching kernel, agent name or retained row cannot\nqualify an image; altered, missing, foreign or unapproved native identities\nrefuse new admission. Lilly selects transport from validated\nmetadata, rejects cross-OS agent sets and preserves the jailer, seccomp,\nper-tap guard and ZFS boundaries. OmniOS remains the installable downstream\nimage while `illumos` is the protocol family."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9918
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The provisioner protocol validates closed schemas and authenticated host sessions.\nGenerated authenticated takeover histories require each fresh host session to\nadvance its durable epoch once. A current nonce retries idempotently; every\nsuperseded nonce keeps returning 409 on open and exchange, with the host row\nunchanged. The [local ratchet property](repo:marshall/test/marshall_web/provisioner_api_test.exs)\nchecks two to five takeovers and the exact consumed-nonce map. It does not\nexercise competing Marshall processes, lost replies or cloned host identity.\nEvery v4 action, including clone, uses the bounded protocol decoder. The clone\neffect exchange is version 5: an unconfirmed effect result MUST name exactly one\nreason from the closed host set (storage busy, execution unavailable, source\nauthority, snapshot, copy, stream, lease, receive, key, SSH, permit, revocation,\nimport cleanup, mount, disk or effect unconfirmed); a bare or unknown reason is\ninvalid and peer strings never create atoms. Malformed\nJSON returns the permanent `invalid_message` 400 class before a control\ntransaction; an unexpected internal transaction error retains the fixed 503\nclass. A generated action matrix verifies the classification and unchanged host\nstate locally. It does not measure the Lilly retry loop under fleet failure.\nGenerated wrong-version session and exchange requests also return 426 without\nchanging host or operation rows; the next valid session advances one epoch.\nThe local property does not exercise a skewed Lilly release over TLS."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9922
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The public Start action retains Marshall authorization and logical generation\nfencing. Lilly must own the placement and prove native VMM confinement before\nadvancing an existing boot journal to the exact current running intent. Placement\nand execution epoch must match, generation cannot regress, and the boot challenge\nis preserved. Missing or corrupt evidence cannot mint a boot proof. Current\ngeneration trust/readiness and roster checks remain mandatory. Isolated journal\nproperties and native repeated-start process continuity passed. The latter does\nnot establish native foreign/corrupt ownership refusal. Native clone cancellation\nwith occupied transfer custody and an unpublished destination passed accounting,\nrestoration and cleanup. The full clone matrix later refused ordinary relocation\nwith source_unavailable; the observational source preflight grants no authority.\nThe final complete native clone matrix passed in 1539.61 seconds after the\natomic snapshot and RPC-margin fixture corrections. Earlier refusals remain\nhistorical evidence; native foreign/corrupt boot refusal remains open."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9926
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The public `/docs/browser-extension` guide is linked as **Browser extension** in\nboth the public header and signed-in reference navigation. Overview's **Open\nrepositories from your browser** card links to the same guide. The public\ndocumentation catalog exposes its HTML and Markdown pages and includes it in\n`/llms.txt`; the guide explains installation, explicit server configuration and\nthe supported GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg and Bitbucket pages."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9930
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The pump reserves each retention-limit increase before changing the ring. Real\nLinux PTY histories grow initial 1 MiB rings to 2/4/8 MiB maxima and fill the\n64 MiB aggregate reservation. Two complete output phases establish growth and\nfull final retention despite intermediate read-boundary truncation. Independent\nbacking snapshots, survivor IDs and exact output counts verify memory refusal,\nretirement and replacement growth with peers still connected. The\n[growth review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-05-terminald-growth-backing.md)\nrecords 51 passing native tests and a control exposing 128 MiB backing against\n64 MiB reserved. This standalone history serializes output across sessions;\nthe following scenario adds mixed stalled/healthy growth and admission. RSS,\nauthenticated guest authority and VM/browser/hosted composition remain open."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9934
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The rc.d/SMF service definitions describe the complete daemon closure. Linked\nexecutables and service definitions do not establish an integrated native image.\nThe supervisor starts local listeners before bounded SSH bootstrap retries so\ntrust installation can unblock roster/certificate HTTPS. No sshd PID marker\nexists before bootstrap succeeds, withholding readiness.\nNative account utilities follow each OS: FreeBSD uses `pw` and pinned Bash/sudo;\nillumos uses `useradd`/`usermod`, `/bin/bash`, `passwd -N` and `/export/home`.\nHelpers validate fresh MMDS on every invocation. Host keys, locks, sshd PID and\nthe fresh SSH-ready marker live under `/var/run/overalls`. Managed sshd uses\n`/etc/overalls/sshd_config`, refuses root/password login and forces the current\nauthorization session helper; stock sshd stays disabled. A two-second roster and\ncertificate refresh removes readiness on failure. Nine live supervisor PID\nmarkers and the fresh SSH-ready marker are required. A disposable OmniOS QEMU\nprobe using the production account adapter passed create/retire/reactivate with\nexact UID/GID/home/Bash, unusable password authentication, clear active expiry,\nretired local lock/noninteractive shell and retained home bytes. OmniOS explicitly\nprepares the safe `/export/home` parent, prevents implicit user ZFS datasets,\nuses local atomic lock/shell retirement and restores the files repository's\nnon-password state on activation. This does not execute the complete MMDS-bound\nhelper or authenticated SSH journey. Actual authentication and expiry journeys\nremain separate runtime requirements.\nTerminald retains one session engine and boot-bound HELLO protocol; native\nadapters supply the MMDS identity, socket ABI and Unix PTY system calls. illumos\nPTY creation refuses an infinite or larger-than-1048576 hard descriptor limit\nand checks STREAMS terminal setup before exec. A stock OmniOS native probe passed\ncontrolling-terminal identity, shell execution, resize to 101x41 and inherited\ndescriptor closure after opening the STREAMS slave following `setsid`. This is\nisolated PTY adapter evidence; the complete boot-bound terminal protocol remains\npending. Unavailable image-facing kernel\nreceipts stop image assembly before copying a stock disk, and all native capabilities\nremain false under [ADR-0190](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0190-native-kernel-provenance-and-admission.md).\nThe intended services must accept only host CID 2 for guest CID 3 and refuse\nforeign, malformed or stale boot bindings. Protocol, Marshall and daemon units\nare source evidence; cross-link checks prove userland linkage. Native startup,\nauthenticated terminal journeys, CID enforcement, restart, storage and\nnetwork isolation require the pending nested-KVM matrix."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9938
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The readiness-only ZFS identity event accepts a validated placement UUID and fixed\nbefore/after/rejection atoms. Paths, dataset names, native output and commands are\nwithheld; ordinary identity/capacity/staging probes stay quiet. A\n`parent_accounting` reason follows parent policy-shape success and strict policy\nrejection, before later mountpoint/root/pool/backing guards. It supplies no healthy\nbacking proof, retry classification, ownership, readiness or capacity credit.\nOriginal storage success/refusal checks and attempt bounds remain unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9942
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The real default Proof callback now has local OpenSSH property evidence beyond\nthe startup banner. Three shuffled four-case histories use a disposable real\nEd25519 CA, host certificate and user key. Strict certificate-only verification\naccepts the trusted CA with the matching hostname; signed user proof submits\none session with the canonical user key and returns a scripted pending approval.\nA foreign CA, hostname outside the signed principal, or absent trust exits 255\nbefore any backend request. Client fallback to a plain host key is disabled."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9946
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The recorded native descriptor-history extension varies one/two/four retained\nempty namespaces, duplicate descriptors, original closure before cleanup and\nhidden proc. Each generated disk is started, stopped normally to remove jail\nbindings, then copied into retained namespaces. Every retained descriptor must show the exact root before deletion\nand none afterward; complete descriptor identities and the unrelated guest must\nremain unchanged. See the\n[history review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-02-namespace-descriptor-histories.md).\nThe first generated case failed its exact-root precondition before deletion;\nits mount list was not captured. A focused native diagnostic reproduced the\nrunning guest’s canonical root plus jail bindings and confirmed only the root\nremains after normal stop. The corrected full native run passed all six histories, original cases and\nfinal busy-file refusal/retry in 1,986.83 seconds. This records bounded stopped-disk\nnamespace cleanup and neighboring process/data preservation.\nDuring-sweep descriptor races, denied inventory\nand changing aliases/descendants remain separate gaps."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9950
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The recorder's native child inherits the pool lock descriptor and original process\ngroup. A parent-death fence with a parent identity check, bounded signal/pipe-error\nreaping and nonblocking persistence preserve custody. Partial arming also cleans\nboth hosts. A clone-only build seam privately captures the exact validated snapshot\nGUID map passed to readiness, bounded to eight bindings and 4096 bytes. Its native\nowner PID selects the context file; each sample distinguishes present, missing,\ninvalid and changed context. The unchanged observer runs once with the original\narguments and result, and `after` removes context. Replaying accounting requires\nthat exact map and frozen pool identity. The recorded instrumented run collected\nsix complete native reads before a queued activation refusal; every sample lacked\nreadiness context. It establishes bounded collection, not readiness-map pairing,\naccounting replay or cancellation acceptance. After verified process shutdown,\noffline analysis used private copies of the retained disposable database and left\nthe original image unchanged. Only finite failure and predicate projections leave\nthat boundary. The fixture's new activation wait observes real fresh receipts and\nsettled operations; it grants no authority and does not relax production guards."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9954
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The repaired legacy fixture uses real 16 GiB mirrored disks and matching enrollment\ndescriptors for conservative startup headroom. Each 4 GiB root carries another\n1 GiB portable WorkloadBundle ownership reservation. Admission uses measured\nphysical headroom and pending startup holds; settled virtual promises do not cap\nadmission. No component carries a logical budget: Lilly refuses one in its\nconfiguration, session pools and ownership record, the Nix module and host tool\nomit it, and Marshall enrollment refuses a pool that supplies one; its column is\ndropped (ADR-0159).\nThe existing race starts with one settled guest and two intended successful\npending starts, plus 512 MiB pool overhead. Its physical gate derives each startup\ncost from the actual image manifest and portable bundle. The requirement depends\non measured physical use and pending holds, not three settled virtual promises."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9958
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The retained-start daemon-loss fixture also caught one deliberately weakened\nlaunch-marker comparison: a same-authority relaunch contradicted the required\nfailed settlement after the before-submission fault. This is bounded native\nsensitivity, not a hosted mutation sweep. The integrated Marshall gate passed\n1452checks including238properties; forced Dialyzer reported zero errors.\nInitial provision, guest data usability and power loss remain separate gaps.\nSee the [submission review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-05-lilly-submission-recovery.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9962
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The reviewed `editor.authority` event follows the outer ledger commit. A nested\nsuccessful action whose caller rolls back emits no committed authority outcome.\n`editor.assets_ready` records a new immutable asset receipt, without repeated\nrefresh events. These diagnostics do not authorize connections or prove browser\navailability. Missing live asset readiness withholds DNS, while a DNS record alone\ncannot prove current user authority. The [scoped review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-editor.md)\nseparates passing local transitions from native and full guest acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9966
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The reviewed official-source package passed its complete build and installed\nChromium workbench startup inside the Nix sandbox, as recorded in the\n[editor review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-editor.md).\nThe separate standalone NixOS check passed verified TLS, personal worker/terminal\nUID and broker/worker recovery with the real package and synthetic roster/bridge.\nThe pre-observability NixOS fleet passed the actual authenticated Firecracker\njourney in 510 seconds; Ubuntu failed initial workbench visibility after\nprovisioning and expected-origin handoff in 361 seconds. After integration,\nobsolete literal prompt matching stopped both first reruns before editor opening.\nThe corrected merged NixOS snapshot then passed the complete journey in\n742 seconds total build plus native execution, including personal UID/webview,\nbroker and worker recovery, saved stop/start, current physical readiness and\nforeign-Origin/sign-out closure. Its source was `849d8022` plus the eligibility,\nSSH projection and root-UID fixture changes later committed as `af01b4b9`.\nCorrected Ubuntu passed executed root-UID proof, provisioning and authenticated\nhandoff, then reproduced initial workbench visibility failure in 610 seconds\ntotal build plus execution. Its runtime cause, complete journey and production\neditor acceptance remain unproved.\nLater diagnostics observed initial bad-gateway with no workbench bootstrap,\nMonaco or WebSocket startup; discarded optional worker-health output cannot\nestablish those fields. Source inspection identified missing store-directory\ncreation and build-user ownership in Ubuntu closure assembly. The corrected\nassembler preserves complete hashed paths and root-custodied configuration with\nexplicit assertions. The subsequent exact `058f13d8` NixOS and Ubuntu runs passed\ntheir initial cold editor/terminal/custody subtests, then failed real-guest policy.\nThe later corrected startup-release full NixOS and Ubuntu fleet scripts passed\ntheir policy/control, broker/worker recovery, saved-data restart/current physical\nreadiness and Origin/sign-out assertions. Standalone version/language, broader\nfault histories and production acceptance remain separate.\nKeep package, standalone and each distribution's checks separate when approving\na new package/image tuple.\nNative command discovery now refreshes the palette's action snapshot within the\noriginal thirty-second deadline and submits a contributed row once. The control\nfixture uses origin-keyed private browser profiles and the workbench reload\naction; its CORS fixture accepts only the two exact local origins. Composed\nRuff/ty helper checks passed in six seconds, while complete new native execution\nis still pending. These fixture changes do not establish historical failure\ncauses or production/browser-backend persistence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9970
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The revision must be reachable from published `main`. Inspect the selected disks, target identity, ports, and erasure digest.\n`install --erase PLAN_SHA256` destroys the selected disks:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9974
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The root flake exports optional `antithesis-NAME-context` packages from pinned\nsource-built closures. Its ordinary application packages use\n`antithesisSdk = false` and `antithesisGuest = false`. A selected harness build\nsets `ANTITHESIS_HARNESS=1` during both fixed-output dependency fetch and\nrelease compilation; the separate Lilly guest selector also sets\n`ANTITHESIS_GUEST=1` and requires the SDK selector. The optional inner-host\nrelease additionally selects `ANTITHESIS_INTEGRATED_HOST=1` at compile time to\nuse measured ZFS and TCG-specific host ownership; it is not the default\ncontrolled container release. Each of the eight Mix\nprojects includes the fallback SDK only for the harness selector. Changing a\nruntime environment variable on an ordinary release does not install its\nharness modules or switch Lilly's compiled executor. These selectors are build\ninputs for disposable test contexts, not deployed NixOS service options."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9978
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The root flake is the public build interface. Applications use the pinned build\npackage input; hosts use the separately pinned stable NixOS input. `x86_64-linux`\nis the current platform. Do not substitute ambient channels."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9986
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The same loopback bridge retains the reviewed BEAM and terminal runtime gauges,\nwith per-unit identity and a separately derived runtime observation timestamp.\nGrafana's BEAM memory and run-queue panels show only recent runtime observations.\nThese diagnostic gauges remain separate from canonical service-level recordings.\nQueue refusal, export failure and logging/spool drop alerts use a one-minute\npending period within their five-minute event windows so isolated failures can\nsurface before they leave the window; persistent health-state alerts wait five\nminutes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9990
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The sealed reviewed FreeBSD disk `5022d714` passed all five native service,\ngrowth, IPv4/router/TAP policy, daemon-recovery and process-SIGKILL recovery\nvariants with complete service oracles, including workspace, PTY, roster-bound\nSSH and guest execution. Its separate immutable promotion and exact registry\nentry grant only that image's reviewed subset. Rebuilding changed current\nuserland does not inherit the sealed image's qualification."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9994
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The separate Audi DNS role counts only ordinary, well-formed questions for its\ndelegated zone. Authoritative NOERROR and NXDOMAIN replies are successful\ncompletions; stale-publication SERVFAIL and socket send failures are failures.\nMalformed packets, out-of-zone refusals, unsupported transfers and truncated\nUDP replies stay outside the candidate denominator. Its mode-0600 numeric\nsnapshot feeds a Rust exporter through an Audi-DNS-only collector socket.\nAtomic in-memory counters are published to the snapshot every five seconds;\npublic query volume does not cause per-request file writes.\nNeither DNS answers nor metrics grant VM or gateway access. Restart can lose\nunexported counts; the candidate ratio has no target or burn alert."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9998
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The separate [FreeBSD candidate](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/freebsd/candidate.nix)\npassed two offline QEMU boots with all installed file hashes, native Git/trust,\nkernel digest and executing sysctl token checks. The\n[OmniOS kit](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/omnios/README.md) packages exact base\nGPT, interpreter, kernel objects, complete service/bootstrap and Git roots. Its\nroot-only helper verifies a separately identified unimported pool, preserves the\nbase BE, installs into a clone and exports before fresh-boot verification. An\nexport failure retains the mounted workspace for recovery. Nine helper safety\ntests passed for the recorded kit. Eleven updated helper regressions and pinned\nRuff/ty checks pass with the offline vendor-service profile. Native execution on the matching image imported the\nseparate pool, linked/installed all nine modules and 758 payload entries into a\nclone, rebuilt its boot archive, selected its bootfs and cleanly exported. A\nseparate QEMU boot reached that clone; fresh-boot verification and loader/cache\npersistence remain pending. Both outputs retain native admission false."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10002
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The separate [abrupt-crash fixture](repo:nix/verification/tests/support/native_crash_recovery.py)\nflushes and reads back retained home bytes, observes actual VMM SIGKILL, then\nboots the same disk under new MMDS identity. It requires a new boot ID, exact\nretained bytes, old flush/workspace/terminal authority refusal, a native UFS\nsnapshot check or completed zero-error ZFS scrub, and final clean shutdown.\nSeven host boundary checks passed. The first actual FreeBSD crash run exposed\nscheduled-only UFS sync: after an acknowledged flush and SIGKILL, fsck discarded\nmanaged-home and marker bytes. The correction uses `/dev/ufssuspend` to wait for\nUFS suspension and soft-dependency drains, then immediately resumes before\nacknowledgement. The final reviewed FreeBSD image passed all eight crash checks:\ncomplete service oracles before/after observed SIGKILL, same-disk restart with\nfresh MMDS/boot identity, exact retained marker/home bytes, stale-authority\nrefusal, native UFS snapshot background fsck and final clean exit. The review\nbinds exact image/kernel/firmware/VMM hashes and immutable crash/service receipts.\nPinned OmniOS ZFS sync already blocks on `spa_sync_allpools`. The source-qualified\nOmniOS crash variant passed with the same-disk restart receipt at\n`/tmp/overalls-omnios-native-probe/exact-crash-recovery-new/crash-recovery-receipt.json`.\nIt observed a first-boot Firecracker SIGKILL, booted with fresh MMDS authority\nand boot ID, refused the previous flush/workspace/terminal authority, retained\nthe exact home and marker bytes, completed a zero-error ZFS scrub and observed\na clean final shutdown. The image, kernel, firmware and patched VMM hashes are\nbound in that receipt; `native_admission` remains false. This does not establish\nphysical stable-media/host power-loss durability, interrupted expansion,\nisolation, relocation or fleet recovery."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10006
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The separately installed `codex` and `claude` commands are user-run guest\nprocesses with native client sessions and guest-local files. Their Ziggy settings\ndo not attach their history, approvals or tool execution to this Marshall-owned\nloop. Guest permissions and private inference admission continue governing them;\nsee [guest defaults](#guests) and [service boundaries](#services)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10010
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The session list labels each terminal with a generated `adjective-noun` name\nderived from a hash of the session id (`MarshallWeb.TerminalNames`), so every\nbrowser shows the same label without storing anything. **New** opens a\nterminal immediately under that name; renaming it replaces the label in that\nbrowser only, and the guest's own title and user appear in the detail line\nbeneath the label."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10014
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared C core validates the complete 44-byte little-endian frame, exact\nused length, stream type, host/guest CID, nonzero ports, flags and bounded\npayload before publication. Credit counters reject acknowledgement of unsent\nbytes and data beyond the receive window. Invalid transitions do not mutate\ncore state; the wrapper must refuse/reset. Generated malformed-frame/state\nchecks and sanitizers passed for this core on Linux. They do not cover DMA,\nsocket lifetimes, host hardware isolation or either native kernel wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10018
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared OpenTelemetry processor uses a fixed 256-entry volatile queue and\nbounded batches. Every thirty seconds each application's reviewed Events catalog\npublishes four numeric producer-health fields through logs: availability,\npending count, queue drops and export failures. These events bypass trace\nadmission, so trace saturation remains visible without recursive spans. Producer\nrestart resets volatile counters; independent spool health records durable\ncustody and delivery. Grafana presents both boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10022
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared browser helper creates private writable Chromium configuration/cache\ndirectories and its wrapper supplies pinned Fontconfig/DejaVu for headless hosts.\nIt preserves HOME and inherited data paths so the native tester's actual NSS\ntrust database remains authoritative. Provision the required TLS roots through\nthe reviewed browser trust setup; a package-only startup fixture's temporary\ntrust bypass cannot establish authenticated native or recurring availability.\nThe wrapper/closure build passed, while actual recurring browser execution remains\nseparate acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10026
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared fleet declaration names seven families: NixOS, Ubuntu, Arch Linux,\nAlmaLinux, FreeBSD, OmniOS and Windows. Its native admission filter currently\nyields seven source-selected catalog rows, including the exact OmniOS promotion. The\n[current OS matrix](repo:docs/guests/os-matrix.md) records package selection,\nboot/agent contracts and evidence limits for each family; a catalog row or an OS\ndisplay label does not prove a successful guest boot or lifecycle."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10030
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared fleet fixture enables boot-trust receivers, private DNS and current\nSSH roster prerequisites for every feature variant. The selected feature\nscenario remains independent of those prerequisites. Both Ubuntu's primary\nimage and its alternate NixOS image receive the current host's public roots.\nThe bounded [native trust journey](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/fleet-guest-trust/driver.py)\nchecks current durable receipts, actual service order, wrong-boot refusal with\npositive delivery controls, and a new receipt with retained data after restart.\nThe wrong-boot request carries a different valid root set and must finish with\nreceiver EOF and no response bytes. Timeout is a failed probe. Installed guest\ntrust and retained data are checked before a final valid delivery can repair them.\nPython's installed SSL implementation must load the actual guest CA file and\ncontain each expected root as DER, alongside exact bundle and unprivileged-read\nchecks. This establishes installed trust contents; the local HTTP control does\nnot establish a live TLS handshake. Failures report only a fixed probe stage.\nBundle reconstruction uses separate bounded commands before the complete byte\ncomparison; every shell input including result markers is limited to 3,072 bytes\nto stay below the Linux canonical PTY line limit. The rejected oversized command\ndid not establish a guest bundle mismatch; bounded reconstruction subsequently\npassed on both actual guest distributions.\nThe SSH roster timer removes its ready marker before refreshing and recreates it\nafter success. The existing 60-second service observation therefore requires the\nreal marker, successful sync result and active required units together. Persistent\nabsence still fails; the fixture never creates the marker or restarts a service.\nThe [execution review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-25-fleet-guest-trust-verification.md)\ndistinguishes test presence from passing execution. It does not rerun the seven\nlegacy feature matrices or establish independent physical failure domains.\nThe follow-up [evidence review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-guest-trust-evidence.md)\nadds a bounded trust-failure exhaustion stage to the same probe: after the\nwrong-boot refusal, seven mutations each violating exactly one receiver check\n(legacy protocol version, foreign placement, stale execution epoch, malformed\nnonce, forged digest claim, duplicated root, private-key-bearing certificate)\nmust each end in the same completed refusal before the receipt-unchanged check;\nthe exact guest bundle, service state and a final accepted same-boot delivery\nbound the sweep. Sender-side terminal trust-retry exhaustion and independent\nphysical hosts remain separate acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10034
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared foreign root now recreates pinned PAM's compiled account helper at\n`/run/wrappers/bin/unix_chkpwd` on every boot. The root oneshot\n`overalls-pam-helpers.service` installs only the immutable pinned 0555 source\nas a regular root-owned 04755 runtime copy; SSH requires its successful\ncompletion. The installer accepts no arguments and refuses symlink, foreign\nownership or unsafe parent paths. Existing nosuid runtime mounts remain in\nplace: pinned PAM's root account caller sets UID 0 before invoking the helper.\nThe isolated VM gate records missing-helper PAM9, repaired account success0,\nand expired13/password-change12/unknown10 refusals, exact bytes, repeat\ninstallation and twelve unsafe-input refusals. It uses a byte-identical\nroot-owned source overlay inside its disposable chroot because vmTools projects\nstore ownership to nobody; actual images separately verify root-owned 0555\nsource custody. This does not establish unprivileged setuid elevation or a\nforeign guest SSH/sudo session. Exact rebuilt Arch `99fa9876` and Alma\n`473f9589` artifacts passed offline and independent rebuild checks. Their fresh\n`114223` journeys then separately passed readiness, strict personal-account SSH,\nread-only sudo/helper checks, stop with independent current-owner stopped\ncheckpoints, restart with repeated SSH/checks and completed deletion. Both\nobserved exact helper/source custody and nosuid runtime mounts. These checks\nestablish the recorded root PAM/sudo paths; they do not prove helper setuid\nelevation, terminal/executor/workspace, revocation, isolation or physical\nrecovery. The matrix review preserves the historical failed images and receipts."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10038
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared personal-account reconciler requires executable\n`/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash` on NixOS or `/bin/bash` on Ubuntu before changing\naccounts or its state. Creation and every successful active-account refresh apply\nthat shell, so a manual shell change on a managed account is reverted. Missing\nBash fails closed without choosing another shell. Allocated identity, home\nownership, retained homes and sudo policy continue to use the existing roster\nrules. Browser and SSH shells obtain the same account defaults. Existing writable\nguest disks are not migrated by rebuilding an image."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10042
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The shared protocol also defines native FreeBSD and OmniOS contracts. Both use\nversion-6 firmware/raw-GPT manifests; FreeBSD uses `os = freebsd` and OmniOS is\nrepresented as `os = illumos`. The FreeBSD disk retains its complete GPT layout\nwith EFI and UFS partitions. Bundle version 3 carries the OS, matching agent set,\nfirmware, bounded artifact sizes and kernel provenance: positive bytes, digest,\nsource revision, patch/configuration hashes and compiler. Lilly requires a\nboot-bound probe response with the expected native OS and kernel digest before\ncredentials or readiness; the file digest alone does not prove which kernel\nbooted. Linux kernel bundles emit `initrd_path` only\nwhen an initrd is declared; native FreeBSD and illumos bundles are firmware-only.\nLilly retains the jail-local vsock transport shape for the native wire sets and\napplies the Hyper-V CPU template only to Windows firmware. Unqualified native\ncapability rows remain all-false. A separate complete-image qualification enables\nonly the reviewed FreeBSD image's readiness, trust, terminal, workspace, flush,\nSSH and agent control; editor and Inception stay false. Unapproved native images\ncannot enter new Marshall admission or Lilly launch. FreeBSD and OmniOS\nbuilder and conditional catalog declarations exist; only the separately\npromoted FreeBSD package passes the fleet admission filter. The OmniOS\ndeclaration consumes the explicit `illumosGuest` constructor while retaining\nits downstream display name, but contributes no evaluated catalog row. OmniOS\nand retained stock FreeBSD identities retain\nfalse capabilities; the host-only transport alone grants no guest operation. The\nrolling FreeBSD 14.3 source endpoint is retired and replaced by a checksum-pinned\narchive capture; Nix evaluates the pinned receipts, but the placeholder kernel\nartifacts make image construction stop before a stock disk is copied. The separate FreeBSD candidate now cross-builds a complete GENERIC kernel;\nthe recorded native Lyra package linked eighteen production target executables using the pinned\nFreeBSD libraries and official OmniOS SDK. The illumos sources include a modern\nvirtio backport, public socket ABI and bounded transport/socket wrappers.\nBoth native terminal daemons now cross-link as separate outputs. Experimental\noutputs do not replace the sealed promoted FreeBSD image or grant admission.\nFreeBSD's separate two-boot QEMU candidate assembly and scoped nested-KVM\ntransport gate have passed.\nOmniOS full-kit pool import/clone/install/export and diagnostic Firecracker\nfile/token/host-CID transport checks have passed. Earlier source-qualified OmniOS\nreceipts record complete services over two clean boots, three-boot/two-expansion\ngrowth and separate process-SIGKILL/same-disk recovery. Reproducible full-image\nassembly, formal policy gates, supervised daemon recovery, reset/DMA, broader isolation and\nfleet acceptance remain open; admission stays false."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10050
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The signed-in sidebar in Workspace and Control room places **Source code**\ndirectly below **HLDD**. It opens the\n[Overalls repository](https://github.com/ghuntley/overalls) on GitHub."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10054
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The skill catalog combines repository `.agents/skills`, creator `.agents/skills`\nand `.overalls/skills`, machine defaults and managed installations. Bounded\ntraversal detects cycles and retains distinct canonical identities for duplicate\nnames. Linked instruction paths are rejected; linked skill directories are\nskipped with an explicit warning. `SKILL.md` frontmatter supplies name,\ndescription and supported invocation metadata, including `agents/overalls.yaml`.\nThe catalog admits at most 100 entries and 64 KiB of metadata. Local skill\nfrontmatter is bounded at 32 KiB; managed installation frontmatter has a 4 KiB\nbound to fit the current operation envelope. `/skills` opens the catalog,\nand `$` completion selects an exact identity without submitting a prompt.\nExplicit selections load before inference; implicit invocation follows the\ndescription and metadata. Catalog metadata is bounded, while selected bodies\nload completely through paginated reads. Supporting files resolve relative to\nthe skill directory. Required content that does not fit returns a refusal\ninstead of silently shortening the skill. Guidance cannot grant tools or\ninstall unsupported connector dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10058
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The source endpoint uses a dedicated SSH listener, pinned Ed25519 identity, and\npermit-derived keys. Transfer credentials do not grant an interactive host shell.\nBoth stream quiescence and key fencing matter when releasing transfer slots.\nRelocation reserves one slot on each endpoint host. Concurrent opposite-direction\nrequests therefore serialize or refuse busy admission; they do not promise two\nsimultaneous transfers. A retained slot on either member of a two-host fleet can\nblock both movement directions. A generated local PostgreSQL property checks\nclone-owned and relocation-owned claims on either endpoint, repeated contention,\npaired relocation rollback, one active owner per host, and release followed by\nacquisition by the other owner. It does not exercise independent Marshall\nprocesses or prove that physical streams have ceased before release.\nCancellation before handoff restores the source under fresh authority; after\nhandoff, follow the recorded destination recovery state. Do not start both copies."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10062
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The source exporter retains the direct sender PID with NOWAIT until private-group\nsignaling, then waits for adopted children across sessions. Uncertain reaping\nreturns EXIT_IO rather than confirming cleanup. Fresh integrated package checks\npassed111seconds with two library properties and ten integration checks, including\nescaped completion and seven failure paths; rustfmt/Clippy passed. See the\n[source-export review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-05-source-export-adopted-children.md).\nExplicit escaped-child death remains a fixture step; this does not establish\nbounded native cgroup recovery, actual ZFS/SSH transfer or physical slot release."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10066
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The source registry contains only the two fleet host addresses. On enrolled\nfleet hosts each entry admits TCP and UDP traffic to all listener ports; people\nuse the Tailscale overlay, and SSH and application authentication remain separate."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10070
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The source-built Lilly `zfs-io` NixOS check passed on 2026-10-13 with two\nmirrored-pool observations, outside-dataset read/write traffic and malformed,\ntimeout and real export/import faults. Collector capacity remained usable while\nindependent `ZFSIO` rates became unavailable, then recovered; the control\ncapacity report carried no I/O field. The earlier probe failed on a decoded\nprotocol struct equality assertion before these cases ran. The\n[prerequisite review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-antithesis-prerequisites.md)\nrecords exact derivations and source identities. This disposable KVM result\ndoes not measure physical IOPS or production pool behavior."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10074
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The source-owned client retains clean/running/gap markers. Ambiguous admission,\nsaturation, unclean startup and storage failure report a coverage gap before fresh\ncoverage resumes. A gap-crossing admission advances coverage conservatively at\nsettlement. Clean shutdown requires source quiescence and no pending admission or\ngap. Source lifetime, durable coverage clock and current sample clock are distinct.\nA cached snapshot and producer heartbeat cannot prove complete admissions."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10082
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The staging intent records host, pool, image, nonce and incarnation before native\neffects. Verification hashes the staged bytes, records nanosecond file identities\nand the dataset GUID, and flushes before sealing read-only. Shared native reader\nleases cover materialization; exclusive builders and retirement exclude readers.\nBulk copying and hashing run outside the pool mutation lock. Warm use checks\nownership, GUID, seal and exact file identities again. A quarantined incarnation\nnever becomes ready again: safe replacement requires confirmed retirement and a\nfresh fully verified incarnation."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10086
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The static researched catalog contains 104 primary properties. The current\nliteral scanner finds 164 SDK callsites, 30 matching primary messages and 74\nmissing primaries, with no duplicate callsite or kind mismatch; none\nof the 104 properties is complete as a workload plus independent oracle and\nhosted result. The default `control-core` selection now stages 13 commands in\nseven templates, six of them one-command terminal singletons: credential\nrevalidation, dispatch, legacy request, SQL reply loss, host-session successor\nand host rotation. Its cross-service dispatch primary binds only a Lilly socket\nand counts foreign effect tables; its legacy-document primary observes\nMarshall's durable host session, not Lilly's Store or Journal; its 4001\nrefusal primary drives the shared client against a loopback peer for a bounded\nquiet window. None has a hosted run. Local SDK writer probes and unit tests show bounded JSONL\nbehavior, including short-write refusal across helper restart, but they do not\nprove hosted ingestion. BEAM fallback assertions provide no BEAM branch\ncoverage. The harness-only Marshall hint relay `Reachable` marks an observed\nnotifier recovery from `:isolated`; it is a search anchor, not evidence that a\nlost hint was reconciled or a committed host command progressed. Python catalog scanning provides no source coverage; the C helper is\nassertion transport, not SUT instrumentation. The workload image presents the\nselection-scoped Python catalog as one `/opt/antithesis/catalog` link to a\nsingle `workload` module tree, so the hosted instrumentor catalogs one root and\nevery workload process registers every selected assertion; a selected\nassertion no command reaches then appears as a zero-example property. A run\nwhose \"Paths found for Antithesis instrumentation\" lists more than one Python\npath has split the catalog per package: the Python SDK registers one root's\ncatalog per process, and unreached assertions in the other packages are\nabsent from the report rather than passing (admission-stub run\n`8e582f650dd7092cda983705eb7b0e05-65-0`). Native Rust coverage remains\npending. A local control-core setup completed on diagnostic4 image bytes, but\nthe selected manifest's four stale dependency hashes prevent a frozen local\nor remote baseline claim for that image."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10090
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The stock native constructors still consume unavailable artifact receipts and\nrefuse before copying a stock kernel or root disk. Separate candidate builders\ncarry the complete FreeBSD kernel or the nine linked illumos modules. The\ncurrent native userland build links twenty Lyra production helpers, ten per OS\nincluding root-only MMDS/network bootstrap and mounted-root growth, plus both\nnative terminal daemons. Separate outputs contain the two transport test probes.\nThe earlier eighteen-helper Linux/Windows/native build passed in 297 seconds;\nthat historical result does not validate a later payload. Native manifests\nvalidate bounded ELF64 AMD64 executables with the target guest's interpreter,\nreviewed shared libraries and a non-executable stack; each records exact output\ndigests and pinned Rust/compiler/linker/SDK inputs. The illumos SDK is a\nuserland linker sysroot, not a kernel-module linker or runtime attestation.\nReadiness additionally requires live supervisor PID markers for every daemon,\nincluding terminald, sshd and the roster/certificate refresh worker, plus a fresh\nvolatile SSH-ready marker; marker presence alone cannot grant readiness. Each\nsupervisor publishes markers through same-directory atomic rename and clears its\nknown daemon markers, including `sshd-native`, after an interrupted predecessor;\nthe FreeBSD service-manager pidfile is preserved."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10094
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The storage parent must be an enrolled ZFS dataset with stable GUIDs. Do not create\nforeign datasets beneath it, edit ownership properties, or reset its baseline to\nbypass readiness. Native LZ4 compression and sparse allocation do not imply a\nfilesystem reservation or a guarantee that every advertised virtual byte can be\nmaterialized simultaneously."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10098
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The subsequent `e5234f67f` import changes only Brady process cleanup,\nPorsche/Ziggy Hegel fixture bindings and receipt metadata. Its changed Porsche\nstrict host-certificate property passed a separate one-property execution with\nall four trust cases; the matrix review records its exact log digest. The\nprior frozen 100-test Porsche gate does not verify this new test source, and\nno new Brady or Ziggy passing execution is claimed. Production/image behavior,\nprotocol contracts and deployment placement are unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10102
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The subsequent upstream `d18a6f11e` integration bounds eleven declarations in\nthree Enrico property files to one generated CI case. Assertions and fixed\nrestart/saturation/refresh loops remain, but inner compatibility `max_runs`\nvalues do not preserve prior random counts. This reduced exploration has no\nnew passing Enrico execution record and lies outside the unchanged four-consumer\nruntime ledger. Production behavior, service placement and authority are\nunchanged; ADR-0191 remains partial."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10106
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The supervisors own real runtime browser credentials and an independent producer\nidentity. Exporters read only their fixed numeric recorder publication and reach\ntheir own collector sockets; they cannot alter outcomes or access browser sessions.\nProbe, recorder and exporter are distinct services on Overalls. Neither Dungarees\nnor guest telemetry duplicates these outer-fleet journey totals. A lost credential,\nrecorder or collector interrupts coverage and leaves the source unknown. Declared\nplacement does not claim that a production fixture or completed journey exists.\nThe 12:01 inspection at revision 50021c04 found all four private fixture/session\nfiles absent. A separately authorized existence-only inspection at 13:06:33 on\ncurrent 63a1443c again found all four files absent, with browser supervisors\nrestarting. Current recurring browser coverage and seven-day history remain\nunestablished despite active request exporters."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10110
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The thirty-second backstop requires an armed watcher on the exact verified VMM\nthat supplied the published proof. Unarmed or unavailable target bindings retain\nthe two-second bound, including after delayed arming messages. A verified boot\ntarget replacing a live target withdraws old live authority before publishing\nroster-only proof. A same-target boot observation does not renew running proof.\nThe ordered [actor/port regressions](repo:lilly/test/vm/actor_watch_test.exs)\nexercise these boundaries; native exit delivery remains separately recorded in\nthe [cutover review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-09-observability-decoupling.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10114
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The thread has an explicit working directory, initially a verified source\ncheckout or otherwise the guest workspace. `/context` reports the directory,\nsource identities, warnings and usage and offers Reload. Changing working\ndirectory refreshes applicable guidance at a safe boundary. The creator's\ndurable guest account supplies personal configuration, including Windows\nprofile lookup; a collaborator does not replace that owner."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10118
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The tunnel also admits collector metrics on 9464 and independent collection-health\nmetrics on 9467 in both directions, and Tempo\ntrace intake on 4317 at Overalls. The collector's guest OTLP/HTTP intake binds\nonly local loopback; its application intake uses the restricted Unix socket.\nThe fleet resource exporter binds loopback 9466 and enters the host collector's\nmetrics pipeline; it has no fleet-facing listener of its own.\nGrafana and Prometheus query APIs also bind loopback. Grafana's provisioned\nVictoriaLogs datasource sends server-side queries over verified fleet-entry TLS\nwith a reader-only runtime credential. Marshall has no diagnostic query credential\nor reader dependency.\nOveralls exposes only\nGrafana through a dedicated authenticated HTTPS proxy frontend on\n`136.243.69.55:3443`, with a matching destination-scoped perimeter grant.\nThe proxy reuses its existing public certificate; Grafana login remains the\noperator authority boundary. Prometheus and Tempo have no public frontend. The\nWireGuard peer key and firewall identify the physical sender, while the Rust\nreceiver checks the Audi UID and replaces guest metadata. Prometheus scraping\ndoes not buffer missed metrics; trace export uses a bounded on-disk queue.\nEach host's Rust Underclass probe reads only the unauthenticated local monitor\nsocket and emits one fixed readiness bit through an Underclass-only collector\nsocket. The probe receives neither proxy nor administrator credentials and\ncannot authorize a provider request. Collector identity is assigned by Nix.\nThe candidate rule requires fresh complete probe series from both enrolled\nhosts; a failed local monitor returns zero, while lost probe or collector\nobservations make the candidate unknown. Tailnet publication and provider\ncompletion remain outside this local readiness boundary.\nBrady's request exporter runs under its independent static reader account in the\nhost network namespace, reads the durable recorder's publication and reaches\nthe collector's group-owned Unix application socket. The collector grants socket\naccess only to the exporter service and keeps its trace queue owner-only.\nActual request-to-collector acceptance and a measured baseline remain separate\nfrom declared placement.\nEach trusted application exporter also sends a fixed source-start-time gauge\nfrom its validated snapshot. Nix recording rules withhold each five-minute\ncandidate ratio until each matching host/replica lifetime and coverage clock\nspan the whole window and the sampling clock is fresh. Durable admissions and\nsettlements preserve acknowledged counts through recorder and producer loss.\nClean application restart preserves coverage; an uncertain recording interval\nadvances coverage and withholds affected windows. These clocks do not establish\nphysical availability or power-loss acceptance.\nGrafana candidate queries repeat the same guard against retained recordings\nafter Prometheus restarts; Grafana has no independent outcome authority.\nThe single Overalls Marshall release writes a mode-0640 browser outcome\nsnapshot in its dynamic-user-owned, mode-0750 directory. A static Rust exporter\nreads the file through the source group but cannot replace it; only the exporter receives the Marshall\ncollector socket group. Nix supplies the fixed `overalls` replica identity. Exporter or\ncollector failure cannot stop the browser endpoint, while a Marshall process\nrestart can lose counts that were not exported.\nEach public Audi gateway writes a fixed SSH establishment snapshot after a\nvalidated grant and connector result. A separate Rust exporter reads it through\nthe source group without directory write access and alone receives the Audi\nSSH collector socket group. Invalid keys and denied grants cannot improve the\ncandidate ratio; source-process restarts can lose unexported counts. The\ncollector assigns application identity and the candidate rule requires a fresh\ncounter from both enrolled Audi hosts.\nGalileo's Rust exporter reads its fixed cumulative snapshot through the\nGalileo-owned Unix frontend. A separate collector socket and group stamp\n`service.name=galileo` from configuration. The ordinary Galileo service lacks\nthat collector group; collection failure does not interrupt integration requests.\nIts packaged and full Firecracker source-to-collector journeys passed;\nproduction collection and the target baseline remain open.\nEnrico's Rust exporter reads its fixed cumulative snapshot through the\nowner-only Unix health frontend. Its separate collector socket assigns\n`service.name=enrico`; the ordinary Enrico service cannot connect to that\nsocket. The packaged source-to-collector journey passed, while cross-host\nMarshall scheduling, manager-crash accounting and production collection remain\nopen.\nPorsche's Rust exporter reads only its mode-0600 fixed numeric snapshot and\nconnects through a Porsche-only collector socket. Nix assigns\n`service.name=porsche`; ordinary Porsche processes lack the collector group.\nThe candidate series measures SSH dispatch results, while physical VM\ncompletion, production collection and a target baseline remain open. The\npackaged service fixture passed zero-valued source-to-collector export and\nordinary UID socket refusal, but no authorized Marshall-backed command.\nZiggy's Rust exporter reads only a fixed mode-0600 snapshot produced after\ndurable spool transitions. Its separate collector socket assigns\n`service.name=ziggy`; the ordinary Ziggy service cannot connect to that socket.\nProvider calls and accounting remain under Ziggy and Marshall authority;\ncollection loss cannot replay inference. Live collection and targets remain open.\nUnderclass's proxy exporter runs under a separate static UID on both hosts.\nIt reads only the mode-0640 numeric snapshot in the source-owned mode-0750\n`/run/underclass/slo` directory and connects to the proxy-only collector intake.\nIts source group grants no provider state or credential access; the producer\nlacks the collector group. This service remains separate from the tokenless\nlocal monitor probe. Isolated native custody passed; production activation remains\nunder verification.\nEach host's `underclass-egress` node is a second, unprivileged, userspace\ntailnet identity with its own state and socket. It has no TUN device or routes,\nand the host's own Tailscale node is unchanged. When an exit node is configured,\nUnderclass's cgroup can reach only loopback. Provider requests then leave\nthrough the egress proxy and the operator's Australian exit node, and fail\nrather than fall back when that node is unavailable (ADR-0185). The exit node\nis outside the fleet failure boundary and is a single dependency for both\nhosts' provider traffic.\nAudi private's Rust exporter reads only its mode-0600 numeric snapshot and\nconnects through a separate group-restricted collector socket. Nix assigns\n`service.name=audi_private`. Bound transport completions stay distinct from\nguest OTLP diagnostic uploads; this metric does not authorize guest access.\nAudi DNS owns a separate mode-0600 numeric snapshot. A Rust process running\nas its DNS user reads it and exports through a DNS-only collector socket;\nNix assigns `service.name=audi_dns`. This candidate counts qualified\nauthoritative responses after socket send, without query-name labels or\ngateway authority. Its metrics disappear on collection loss and unexported\ncounts may disappear on process restart."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10122
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The two mechanisms cover different crawlers. A crawler that honours `robots.txt`\nnever requests a page, so it never sees the `noindex` directive; a URL it already\nknows can stay listed from external links. Removal of already-indexed URLs is a\nseparate action in each search engine's webmaster removal tool. The header and\nmeta tag cover crawlers that fetch pages regardless of `robots.txt`. Neither\nmechanism is an access control: authentication, Cedar authorization and the\npublic-route allowlist decide what a request may read, and public installer and\ndocumentation routes stay reachable to anyone who knows their URL."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10126
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The update failure and recovery boundaries are exercised natively. A build, preflight,\nmigration or activation failure is recorded with its phase, failure counter and retry\ndeadline while the serving revision remains last-healthy. An out-of-band drift of the live\nsystem closure — for example an operator switching the running generation back by hand —\nreconverges exactly once on the next attempt with a single healthy record; a further poll\nis a no-op fast path and leaves neither staging nor handoff residue. A failed activation\non a Lilly host with live guests preserves every guest VMM process identity, restores the\nprior consumer roles, keeps last-healthy pinned to the serving revision and records the\ndrifted live system honestly, and a corrective revision reconverges without replacing\nguests. Host credential rotation archives the superseded credential immediately, so a\nretained pre-rotation envelope is refused with 401 even inside its nominal TTL. Recorded\nexecutions and per-leg durations live in the\n[update failure matrix review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-update-failure-matrix.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10130
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The updater selects compatible stable releases, including paginated results,\nthen checks manifest, platform, dependencies, archive bounds, packaged Ed25519\nsignature and upstream engine compatibility. It atomically replaces the lock\nonly after every selected artifact passes and refuses observed concurrent\ndestination changes. Validation, resolution and synchronization failures before\nrename preserve the old lock. The complete temporary file is synchronized before\nrename, then the parent directory afterward. A postrename parent synchronization\nfailure returns exit2 with `outcome: durability_unknown` and\n`reason: publication_durability_unknown`; the complete new lock may already be\nvisible. Review the visible lock and rerun validation before building new images.\nNo partial lock bytes are published. The updater emits closed JSON outcomes and\nfinite selected IDs/versions without paths or registry response contents. It does\nnot rotate keys, run servers, alter Code OSS pins or deploy.\nThe final specialized package passed its complete build and installed Chromium\nworkbench/PTY checks in 234 seconds, with exact release/output/log in the\n[review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-editor.md). This synthetic\nlocal package fixture does not establish the new defaults or language tools in a\nnative guest. Fresh standalone and both Firecracker family checks remain required.\nThe installed-validator maintenance check passed all 19 tests and Ruff/ty in nine\nseconds; the packaged wrapper's four-pin check returned current JSON with all\nchange flags false. The default live `--check` also selected latest compatible\nstable versions through bounded pagination, validated complete signed artifacts\nwith the actual packaged verifier, and returned current with all four unchanged,\nexit0. These maintenance results do not prove native language activation.\nThe final standalone native attempt passed first-terminal proof but failed the\nnew Native Policy stage. Replay observed its policy marker and EditorConfig\nresult with no visible AI UI; the exact failed browser assertion remains unknown.\nThe corrected per-action run later passed the original cold browser journey and\nAlice's outside-HOME policy stage, then failed before Bob's HOME invocation when\nits command-palette row was absent for 30 seconds. Resolve that discovery failure\nand record complete two-account controls/language execution before claiming fresh\nextension acceptance; the earlier failure remains separately unexplained.\nBuilt-in AI policy, extension lock and adapters change release identity; retained\nmachines keep their disks and require a new matching machine for editor access.\nStartup/walkthrough defaults also enter the package configuration and release\nidentity. New matching images carry those defaults; personal settings can\noverride them. The new actual package and installed-validator maintenance check\npassed in 217 and 28 seconds, and a separate fresh-profile manual Chromium smoke\nobserved no startup Welcome/Get Started/Walkthrough UI after actual workbench\nrestoration. These scoped results do not establish saved-editor removal,\nextension-install walkthrough behavior or user override execution. The later\ncorrected frozen snapshot passed complete NixOS and Ubuntu Firecracker family\nscripts in 805 and 829 seconds total build plus native execution. Standalone\nversion and real-language failures retain their original scopes. Later strict\nscratch both-language success on old startup package 3zz preceded complete final\nstandalone acceptance in 342 seconds: personal install/reversion, core/System\nrefusals, actual Rust/Elixir, controls and broker/worker recovery. The final personal-update\npackage at release 7d2415 passed in 236 seconds, installed-validator maintenance in\nseven seconds and both matching guest image builds in 99 seconds. These build\nresults do not establish native fleet or production runtime. Earlier\npackage/native/activation records retain their own scope, and these fleet passes\npredate the incoming template changes rather than validating periodic sweeps."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10134
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The worker box applies separately to each host. It is not a shared guest disk or\nan independently replicated control plane. Domains within Marshall are shown in\n[Ash business domains](#domains)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10138
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The workload owner alone emits setup. A missing fixture, SQL/TLS failure,\nunready endpoint, stale host report, timeout or early stop leaves setup\nincomplete. The hosted Lilly publishes no physical pool credit and withholds\nexecution readiness, so this fixture cannot run an ordinary guest admission.\nThe guest and storage-router contexts are separate TCG emulator source. The\n[local guest boot record](repo:antithesis/verification/guest-boot.md) captures\nthree ready NixOS boots, including restart on the same qcow2 overlay and offline\nretained machine-id verification. It also records the first diagnostic boot's\nNIC-name readiness failure and the `net.ifnames=0` repair. The guest runner is\nnot connected to the default hosted Lilly's fail-closed controlled executor.\nThe optional integrated host and guest bridge are authored but have no complete\ncomposed execution. A separate\n[router TCG smoke](repo:antithesis/verification/storage-boot.md) passed direct\nboot, Brady HTTP/HTTPS cache replay, QMP reset and retained ZFS marker/quota\nchecks. Its container entrypoint and hosted run remain unexercised. Retain KVM\nmachine checks for native guest and ZFS claims."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10142
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The writer is a line-start state machine: carriage return and line feed both\nend a line, empty lines stay bare and output does not depend on pipe chunking.\nThe 1 MiB cap applies to the stamped stream; bytes past it are drained and\ndiscarded, so a noisy guest can neither block the VMM nor grow the file."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10146
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "These archives are unsigned and are served by the Overalls deployment. Download\nthe archive for your browser, then follow its developer-install flow:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10154
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "These are user-run processes with guest-local state, separate from\n[Marshall-owned agents](#agents). Customer still refuses private inference.\nProduction Ziggy enables only OpenAI; Claude is configured and its beta URL is\nsupported, but returns `provider_unavailable` until Anthropic is separately\nenabled with Ziggy-owned credentials. New image identities affect future\nprovisioning and do not migrate retained writable disks. Actual client execution,\nTLS/provider routing and GC retention remain scoped evidence in\n[coverage](#coverage) and the\n[review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-13-native-guest-coding-clis.md).\nBoth native image probes executed client version/help, configuration and root/plain\nuser trust before and after GC. Both complete integrated coding-client fleets\npassed: native personal-account terminal inference, separate curl beta counting,\nlimited trust refusal, root GC, registered verified SSH inference from both\nclients after GC, and Customer no-effects refusal. These fleets use editor-free\nfixture images with the shared client packages/settings and isolated verified-TLS\nproviders; their identities differ from the production artifacts above. The\ncurrent plugin-update assertion passed in both native client/GC probes. The\ncomplete packaged NixOS lifecycle and current Marshall/static/Nix handbook gates\npassed at the reviewed `c5747868` checkpoint. Ubuntu's broad lifecycle failed on\na quarantined template when starting a fresh neighbour after its client, source,\ninitial confinement and host-loss checks passed; an unchanged full rerun failed\nthere again. Two diagnostic slices did not reproduce the failure. Separate\nretained-state inspection after another import demonstrated strict device-identity\nrefusal without proving the historical cause. A limited test-only post-import\ncache action now covers exact reuse or device-only refusal, fenced deletion and a\nfully verified distinct incarnation. It changes no production guard or launch\nauthority. The complete corrected Ubuntu suite passed in 2,203.23 seconds of\ndriver execution, including exact reuse in the new action and final clone/receive\ncleanup. Device-only replacement remains unexecuted, and the original failure\ncause remains unknown. Focused current NixOS exact reuse and original neighbour\ncleanup passed in the current packaged prefix; later omitted cases retain only\nthe earlier complete NixOS scope. The corrected/refrozen packaged handbook browser\ncheck passed at its captured source. Both hosts activated published `8930a70f`,\nbut the first production NixOS Codex canary timed out before any inference\nadmission; guest deletion, reservation release and dataset absence were confirmed,\nand Ubuntu creation was not issued. Separate pinned public-chain validation\nrejects the legacy leaf. Same-root preparation and both independent encrypted\nhost replacements passed; commit/push, live replacement and a successful\nproduction client journey remain separate. The GC helper\nruns as root; the subsequent SSH journey separately establishes personal-account\ninference after GC."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10158
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "These four tokens are the only colours. The appearance select offers\nSystem, Light and Dark; the browser stores an explicit choice in local storage\n(`phx:theme`), applies `data-theme` on the document and falls back to the\nsystem preference when storage is unavailable. Public documentation and the\nsign-in pages honour the same preference without a session. State is written\nas text (`[running]`, `[failed]`), weight or inverse video, never colour alone.\nThe terminal renderer, Mermaid diagrams and the loading bar read the same\ntokens, so switching appearance redraws them; terminal bytes, geometry and\nclipboard consent follow [interactive access](#interactive) unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10166
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "These pages are public and contain no account or fleet data. Each page is available\nas HTML by removing `.md` from its URL. Language-model clients can start at\n[/llms.txt](/llms.txt) and follow the Markdown links."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10170
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This activates the previously tested sealed image. Rebuilding current native\nuserland after local MAC normalization yields a different FreeBSD candidate,\nwhich remains unadmitted; it does not replace the sealed closure. The\n[activation review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-17-native-image-admission.md)\nseparates artifact verification, cache publication, SQL/host refusal tests and\nnative scenario evidence. Reproducible native assembly, direct native peer-VM\nisolation, physical recovery, relocation and broader Marshall-driven capability\nacceptance remain gaps. The current source-qualified OmniOS service, growth and\ncrash receipts are recorded separately. The final OmniOS disk `45983bb7` now has all\nfive exact-image scenario receipts, a verified\n[promotion constructor](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/native-promotion/omnios.nix)\nand compiled registry identity\n`sha256:98b23b7931bc52d55b3c5b38cc20c97c1c967be96cf301940f88b01c715530d8`.\nIts complete immutable manifest/descriptor enables the same reviewed native\nsubset; unqualified rows, editor and Inception remain false. Candidate runtime\nreceipts remain false and do not transfer to other images. The candidate NAR\npassed exact local comparison, signed-cache verification and independent\nDungarees substitution; the promoted closed three-file package passed its\nartifact/evidence verifier. `omniosGuest` and `illumosGuest` now select that\nexact immutable promotion, producing seven source-selected catalog rows while\nthe candidate kits retain false admission flags. The separately recorded\nselected-release deployment and exact-image live CLI journeys do not transfer\nto unrelated candidate builds.\nCatalog evaluation, the promotion gate and the actual promoted package build\npassed after alias selection; exact `98b23b79` identity/flags and alias equality\nare checked while candidate-kit admission/runtime flags stay false.\nThe [matrix review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-guest-os-matrix.md)\nrecords exact disk/manifest/NAR/receipt pins. Declared minimums are 2 GiB memory\nand 16 GiB disk; the scoped growth history is 8-to-10-to-12 GiB, while the\nseparate ordinary OmniOS fleet lifecycle uses the selected 16 GiB minimum."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10178
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This checks real local certificate negotiation and user signature proof, not\nMarshall account ownership, browser approval, HTTPS transport or authorized\nmanagement. The pending response remains scripted. Packaged/native journeys,\nproduction trust rotation and hosted faults remain separate. Evidence:\n[negotiation property](repo:porsche/test/host_certificate_negotiation_property_test.exs)\nand [execution review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-29-antithesis-property-audit.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10182
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This fragment requires the default module, host networking, secret preparation,\ndatabase trust, and the declared fleet connection budget. `replicaCount` describes\nthe fleet, not the number of entries on this host. An explicit single-instance\nconfiguration is supported but has no control-plane redundancy."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10186
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This is the isolated test graph, not physical fleet placement. PostgreSQL owns\ncommitted fixture state; the operator uses Marshall actions and private volumes\nto issue credentials, while the workload alone decides whether to emit setup.\nThe earlier local diagnostic started migration, Marshall A/B, enrollment and\nLilly health, and the operator created three passkey-backed accounts without\nopening a listener. The controlled host's connected session had no report.\nA later fresh base project published a current v4 report and emitted setup;\nsix serial Control commands passed locally with 13 SDK assertion records. Its\nselected manifest has four stale runtime-dependency hashes, so hosted acceptance\nremains open. The optional fourth-image inner host\nmeasured ZFS and cgroups but remained disconnected and execution-unready; its\nguest witness and runtime PKI preparation confer no admission authority. Its\nrecovery inventory admits only the fixed `lilly.service` and\n`lilly-guest-emulator.service` cgroups plus exact tracked VM units when\nsystemd creates them; unknown or malformed native inventory fences recovery,\nand emulator inventory independently checks tracked placements.\nFor the integrated branch, physical ZFS/cgroup and guest-vmm preflight must\nprecede a current delivered host report. Reviewed enablement and credential\nrotation then allow inner Ziggy/Audi to become ready; a new private guest\nlaunch/restart refuses before its runner effect until those roles are ready.\nThe inspected source moves this private check to GuestExecutor to avoid a\ncredential/report bootstrap cycle. The current private host still has report\nsequence zero, so this ordering is source-reviewed rather than composed\nexecution evidence.\nThe separate container `guest-lifecycle` profile instead places a guest-vmm\nQEMU TCG container beside a STUB-storage Lilly release: Lilly alone mounts the\nmutating guest control socket, enrollment alone mounts the read-only witness\nsocket and the image catalog share, and the workload reaches both only through\nthe operator's closed request channel. Its synthetic pool is not ZFS; losing\nguest-vmm health, the registered image or the stub catalog withholds setup.\nLoss of the fixture database, private credentials, current report or inner-host\nmeasurement withholds the corresponding setup or guest claim."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10190
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This is the repository's declared fleet, not live health. The placement diagrams\nare generated from an allowlisted evaluation of NixOS configurations. They include\nthe named Overalls services, PostgreSQL, fleet addresses and administrative SSH\nports; they do not enumerate every systemd unit or firewall rule."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10194
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This option is also available to all submodules. Submodules do not\ninherit args from their parent module, nor do they provide args to\ntheir parent module or sibling submodules. The sole exception to\nthis is the argument `name` which is provided by\nparent modules to a submodule and contains the attribute name\nthe submodule is bound to, or a unique generated name if it is\nnot bound to an attribute."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10198
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This optional path is disabled in the deployed fleet. The central host owns\nevaluation, route configuration and credential custody; the external receiver\nowns notification handling and receipt. An unavailable receiver causes retries.\nLoss of the central host interrupts both evaluation and sending; this diagram\ndoes not establish independent notification availability."
msgstr ""

