#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10818
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Windows fleet staging/registration selects `windowsGuestPublished`, a\nrecursive-hash fixed-output copy of the reviewed 64 GiB Desktop Experience\n`windows-v1` installation. The separate source builder remains available for\ndevelopment. Exactly three regular files (`disk.raw`, `firmware`,\n`manifest.json`) with no references bind both hosts to the same bytes through\nordinary signed-cache substitution. Missing publication fails with operator\ninstructions; it cannot rebuild a different installation or select host-local\nbytes. The [Windows guide](repo:docs/guests/windows-guests.md) records the exact\nNAR/artifact pins and update workflow. Package/file-custody and refusal checks\nand both-host verification of the public cache signature passed. These checks\ndo not establish a new Windows lifecycle or capability result."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10826
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "WireGuard fleet IPv4 the entry binds; never a public or wildcard address."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10830
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "With exec, place the same command after `ssh YOUR_PORSCHE_HOST` and quote it for\nyour local shell when needed. Porsche does not evaluate shell expressions,\npipelines or substitutions. Single and double quotes group command arguments."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10834
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Withheld viewer credit pauses ordinary live PTY delivery. An authorized explicit\nkill cancels that delivery obligation: terminald aborts registered viewer queues,\nshuts down their sockets and kills the owned process group before retirement can\nreap its leader. A bounded PTY readiness wait observes cancellation even when an\nindependent owner holds a quiet slave. Session/ring and viewer permits return\nwithout peer disconnection. Natural shell EOF still drains queued bytes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10838
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Workers retain the published commit during a failed refresh. Each terminal result\nin Enrico must match its current local task generation. Completion retires the\nmonitor before reuse; repository removal clears its queued receipt keys.\nMissing or unknown completion generations cannot manufacture an outcome.\nActive and retained attempts replay without another fetch, while removal,\ncapped receipt eviction and restart end local replay retention. Generated\nsupervised-worker histories passed across the 1,024-receipt cap using controlled\nGit replies; real remote/cache pressure and aggregate active admission remain\nopen in the [generation review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-03-enrico-attempt-generation-histories.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10842
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Workspace labels `/usage` **AI usage**; its page shows **Your AI usage**.\nControl room labels `/admin/usage` **Fleet AI usage**. These names describe\ninference request and token accounting; they do not change reader authority,\nowner attribution or the treatment of incomplete reports as unknown."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10846
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Write admission/publication diagnostics use the reviewed Brady Events catalog.\nCompleted request events distinguish published, abandoned and unattempted writes,\nwith existing authoritative owner/VM/generation bindings. No per-chunk logging or\nURLs, headers, payloads or credentials are added. Deployments and VM lifecycle\nmutations retain their existing management audit path. Native/property execution\nand live isolated-store nixpkgs acceptance are recorded separately in the\n[large-cache review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-02-large-egress-cache.md).\nIts live phases passed real independent-store pinned nixpkgs fetches and exact\n318,532,502-byte LLVM reuse across NixOS and Ubuntu. Nix uses `/tar.gz/`, while\nthe legacy curl archive uses `/legacy.tar.gz/`; their references remain separate.\nBounded private guest jobs retain completion receipts across interrupted test\ntransports. Earlier SSH and unresolved-attribution failures remain documented\navailability gaps; they are not presumed fixed by cache or runner changes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10850
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ZFS create, set, mount, unmount, rename, destroy, snapshot and receive-abort use a\n120-second default native utility bound; explicit bounds take precedence and\nother utilities retain three seconds. The new interrupted-create and\nunconfigured-sweep machine subtests are present. Their source additions do not\nextend the older recorded native execution below."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10854
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Ziggy also serves the private guest CLI update manifest and binary on fixed routes.\nIts durable spool counts newly completed inference admissions with fixed\ncumulative outcomes and duration buckets. Caller-cancelled streams stay out of\nthe candidate denominator; a recovered interrupted admission remains a failure\nwith unknown duration. A private mode-0600 numeric file feeds a Rust exporter\nthrough a Ziggy-only collector intake. Its provisional seven-day target and\nburn states are described in [monitoring](#monitoring); collection cannot\nauthorize or replay inference. Durable startup persists an empty source lifetime\nbefore readiness. Recovery folds retained terminal slots before serving callers;\nan uncertain checkpoint fences admission, acknowledgement and export. Checkpoint\ncreation is unconditional; there is no reader-only mode (ADR-0159).\nEach delivery requires Marshall's decision for the current VM execution to match the\npackaged digest and size exactly. An authority refusal is the only guest-distinguishable\ndenial (403); a decision/packaging mismatch, absent configured release or an unavailable\ncontrol plane all answer 503, and a transient guest-side failure of that kind marks only\nthe check failed — it never creates, extends or clears the candidate rejection cooldown.\nThe refusal matrix is pinned by generated histories on both sides per the\n[update failure matrix review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-update-failure-matrix.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10858
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Ziggy is the private inference proxy on Lilly hosts. Audi supplies the current guest\nbinding. Marshall authorizes admission and renewal; Ziggy calls configured providers\nand durably delivers usage receipts. Provider secrets stay in Ziggy's private\nruntime custody. A guest-provided identity header confers no authority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10862
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Ziggy v2 sends only outcome, token counts, completeness and optional priced\naccounting provenance to Marshall. Duration, byte counts, HTTP status and provider\nrequest identity stay in its private local completion. A replayed completion\nsucceeds only when it exactly equals the stored one; PostgreSQL refuses a stored\ncompletion outside that closed key set, and the legacy-shaped rows were purged\nafter export (ADR-0159). The final SSH authorization recheck and its security audit remain.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10866
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Ziggy's generated local boot histories cover 87 refusals across 29 invalid\nfile/schema/origin/path/provider/credential cases. They require exact startup\nrejection and its catalogued event before the named supervisor or reviewed\nserving/provider workers start. Fixture hashes/modes/link counts stay unchanged,\nand original/mutated credentials and fixture paths do not enter diagnostics.\nUnset configuration starts an empty supervisor without a serving call. Twenty\ngenerated immutable spool paths are checked through the read-only loader without\nworker startup outside fixture custody. The revised focused suite passed three\nchecks, including two properties. Packaged startup,\nconfigured serving, file-replacement races, native isolation and hosted faults\nremain open."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10870
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0135](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0135-streaming-public-artifact-cache.md)\ncarries forward interception, attribution and pressure policy and adds automatic\nanonymous full-commit GitHub archive reuse. Verified HTTPS codeload paths under\nany canonical owner/repository qualify without configuration. Credential/cookie,\nquery, branch-name, private/no-store and ambiguous variant cases still refuse.\nMissing archive freshness is bounded to one hour; compatible shorter explicit\nfreshness is honored. Authorization Vary can be removed only when Authorization\nis absent; Accept-Encoding remains part of variant identity."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10874
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0150](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0150-default-guest-starship.md) records\nthe additive prompt decision. Both managed SSH/browser journeys passed after\nintegrating independent-observability revision `a2d9e6e` with feature `63214e60`:\nstock startup, nested Bash, exact noninteractive stdout, two successful bounded\nreal roster refreshes, relocation and reboot. Integrated Lyra, image evaluations\nand all-files static hooks passed separately. Root/manual-account prompts and\nchecked account/home cleanup passed both complete root suites before integration\nat `63214e60`/`1cd8be40`; no merged root-suite rerun is claimed. Historical managed\njourneys, Marshall precommit and the evaluated Nix handbook gate retain their\nrecorded revisions. The guest image and account-helper source is unchanged across\nintegration. The\n[review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-guest-starship.md) preserves exact\nartifacts, earlier failures and property/custom-configuration gaps;\n[scenario coverage](#coverage) remains partial."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10878
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0160](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0160-privileged-administrator-step-up.md)\naccepts a fixed fifteen-minute administrator elevation and inline retry of the\nexact pending action for enrolled administrators. No-key administrators complete\nsetup in the sign-in section of the account page (`/settings#security`,\n[ADR-0196](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0196-single-account-settings-page.md))\nand explicitly retry. This section states that design;\nthe historical baseline used five-minute elevation and redirected all verification.\nScoped final packaged browser acceptance is recorded in [scenario coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10882
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0169](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0169-firmware-booted-windows-guests.md)\nadds a third, experimental guest family. A Windows Server 2025 image is\nbooted by firmware rather than by a Linux kernel: Firecracker loads edk2's\nCloudHv UEFI (`CLOUDHV.fd`, a PVH ELF) through `boot-source.kernel_image_path`\nwith no initrd and no kernel command line, and exposes its devices over the\nopt-in virtio-pci transport (`--enable-pci`) so the signed virtio-win\n`viostor` and `NetKVM` drivers bind. The firmware is built from the pinned edk2\nwith one patch that ends its 32-bit PCI aperture at Firecracker's MMCONFIG\nwindow ([firmware package](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/windows/firmware.nix))."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10886
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0170](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0170-lilly-firmware-guest-launch.md) makes the\nimage a launchable guest. The protocol's\n[image manifest validation](repo:protocol/lib/overalls_protocol/image_manifest.ex)\nadmits exactly the eight-key version-5 shape beside version 4 and derives a\n`lilly-image-v5` identity that can never collide with a kernel image; the\n[workload bundle](repo:protocol/lib/overalls_protocol/workload_bundle.ex) gains a\nseven-key version 2 for firmware bundles while version 1 encodes byte for byte\nas before. Both results carry `boot_kind`, `root_format` and `guest_agents`, and\nthe protocol alone names the artifact files (`kernel`, `initrd`, `rootfs.ext4`, or\n`firmware`, `disk.raw`)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10890
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0171](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0171-windows-guest-agents.md) adds a\nthird agent set. [`OverallsProtocol.GuestAgents`](repo:protocol/lib/overalls_protocol/guest_agents.ex)\nnames the closed set (`linux`, `none`, `windows-v1`, `freebsd-v1` and\n`illumos-v1`) and a pure capability table. Linux has everything, `none` has\nnothing, Windows has its reviewed firmware subset, and unqualified FreeBSD and\nillumos rows remain all-false transport declarations. Qualified `capabilities/2`\nrequires an exact approved FreeBSD or OmniOS identity for the reviewed native subset;\neditor and Inception stay false. Lilly's driver, executor and\njail and Marshall's surfaces branch on these capabilities, never on the set's\nname. Version-5 Windows and version-6 native manifests may declare\n`workspace_source_version` 2 as metadata, but that field alone grants no source\ndelivery. A readiness budget for a native row does not establish readiness;\neach approved native launch still requires current-boot kernel attestation."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10894
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0175](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0175-thread-sharing-and-bound-machine-authority.md)\nadds canonical sharing on the durable thread. A viewer reads the task and retained\ntranscript and receives existing machine-read actions. An operator may prompt a\nready task and use existing machine-write actions, including prompt-triggered\nagent wake; ordinary machine lifecycle still requires management authority.\nA manager may manage thread sharing/lifecycle and use existing machine-management\nactions. No web-only thread role exists, and the creator's ownership and billing\nremain protected. Managers resolve current registered recipients by complete\ncase-insensitive email only after authorization."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10898
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0175](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0175-thread-sharing-and-bound-machine-authority.md)\nadds durable task grants that contribute to the currently bound machine's ACL.\nSharing changes must preserve every layer below. No native execution of this new\nsharing journey has yet been recorded; focused and historical results retain the\nscope in the [sharing review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-thread-sharing.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10902
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0178](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0178-durable-agent-context-and-planning.md)\naccepts the following context and collaboration behavior. Its implementation\nand execution evidence are being completed in the\n[context review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-agent-context-and-planning.md);\nthis section does not establish deployed availability."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10906
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0178](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0178-durable-agent-context-and-planning.md)\nand [ADR-0179](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0179-guest-instructions-and-managed-skills.md)\nadd context checkpoints, collaboration controls and guest guidance. The table\nstates accepted requirements; final source and executed evidence remain tracked\nin the [context review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-16-agent-context-and-planning.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10910
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0179](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0179-guest-instructions-and-managed-skills.md)\naccepts guest instruction collection and managed skills. Native Linux and\nWindows behavior, provider continuation and restart acceptance remain separate\ndelivery evidence in the context review."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10914
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0193](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0193-per-user-machine-pins.md) adds a\npersonal pin that orders lists and, for the owner, fences deletion. The pin is\nnot an authority: it cannot grant, extend or retain access, and only the owner's\npin changes what any actor may do. Evidence is BEAM-level (generated histories,\nLiveView and SSH command tests); no native journey has run."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10918
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0195](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0195-user-time-zones.md) gives each\naccount a display time zone. Storage, ledger comparisons, the usage CSV export\nand SSH JSON stay unchanged; `ls`/`show` omit the additional machine-owner\nfields, and `share show`/`team show` omit saved zones from owner, grant and\nmember records. Conversion happens only when a page renders. The viewer's zone resolves as the saved zone, else a valid browser zone\nsent in the LiveView connect params (`timezone`, from the Intl API), else UTC.\nThe dead render trusts only the saved zone. `MarshallWeb.LiveUserAuth` assigns\n`time_zone`, `time_zone_source` (`saved`, `browser`, `utc`) and `now`, and a\none-minute tick refreshes `now` only after fresh-login validation on required\nand security views. Optional and public views retain their existing behavior."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10922
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0195](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0195-user-time-zones.md) stores one\ndisplay preference per account. It is not an authority: it never enters Cedar,\npolicies, ledger comparisons or any wire contract, and only its owner can write\nit. Evidence is BEAM-level (Hegel properties, LiveView and Covenant tests); no\nnative journey has run."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10942
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[Fleet-network tests](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/fleet-network/default.nix) exercise\ntunnel reachability and forbidden paths in NixOS machines. The\n[host replacement](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/fleet/scenarios/host-replacement.py)\njourney retires one test provisioner and enrolls its replacement (fresh machine\nidentity, name and storage GUIDs) through the protected SOPS/SSH path on the same\nfixture topology. The\n[fleet scenarios](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/fleet/default.nix) use a separate test\ntopology, including three Marshall instances; that fixture is not this fleet's\nreplica count. Prefer native NixOS allowed/refused traffic tests and generated\nauthority histories for changes to these boundaries. Production failure-domain,\npublic DNS/gateway failover and host-loss acceptance require separately recorded\nevidence. See [scenario coverage](#coverage) and [recovery](#recovery) for the\nremaining security and recovery gaps.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10946
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[Generated cache histories](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-cache-history-verification.md)\ncombine those file/process lifetimes with expiry and current privacy checks using\nindependent expected public bytes. Their pressure inputs are component fixtures.\nHistorical native network/ZFS and guest attribution evidence retains its original\nrevision and separately establishes interception, trust and isolation boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10950
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[Generated operator histories](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-secret-recipient-histories.md)\ntrack actual SOPS/age decryption, host/recovery key overlap and removal, explicit\nrecovery-only custody, all-candidate refusal and bounded publication retry. Their\nlocal CLI evidence is separate from native delivery and coordinated credential\nrotation; off-host recovery availability and physical disaster drills remain\noperator acceptance. Removing the last host from a document without explicit\n`recoveryOnly` custody refuses during inventory validation, before any host can\nwedge on it; removal while other hosts remain is valid custody but wedges this\nhost's updater at staging, as described above."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10954
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[Lilly.Lifecycle](repo:lilly/lib/lilly/lifecycle.ex) uses host-local journals and\nnative locks. Delivery, observation and accepted control-plane outcome remain\nseparate states. Loss of a host report does not prove that its guest stopped.\nSee [lifecycle](#lifecycle), [workflows](#workflows) and [security boundaries](#security)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10958
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[Native kernel inputs](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/native-kernels/README.md)\npin the exact release sources. FreeBSD uses vendor family 45 with BSD length and\nfamily bytes; illumos uses private family 40 with a 16-bit family. Both publish\n`sys/vsock.h`, a 16-byte `sockaddr_vm`, port/CID at offsets 4/8 and the standard\nCID/port constants. Only passive host-initiated streams are implemented.\nBinding/creation requires root authority (global-zone system configuration on\nillumos), guest CID 3 or ANY, and accepted peer CID 2. Invalid CIDs, reserved\nbytes, port zero, frame lengths, flags, transition/credit state and resource\nexhaustion refuse. Each wrapper caps sockets at 128 and receive windows at\n64 KiB; queue pools are fixed and control traffic retains transmit capacity.\nFreeBSD also refuses buffer/low-watermark setters so generic socket options\ncannot enlarge that budget. Test-only native assertions check four option\nrefusals, exact buffer size, socket-129 refusal and full capacity reuse; their\nfinal-candidate machine execution remains separate from test presence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10962
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[Overalls services](repo:fleet/hosts/overalls/services.nix) declare one\n`marshall-overalls` instance. The public proxy forwards to loopback port 4443\nwith verified TLS. PostgreSQL is loopback-bound with TLS/SCRAM. Porsche uses\nmanagement SSH port 22; administrative OpenSSH uses 2222. Audi and DNS bind\nseparate public addresses on each host. Enrico is a private quota-bound cache.\n[Dungarees assertions](repo:fleet/hosts/dungarees/configuration.nix) enforce its\nworker-only control-plane configuration."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10966
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[The HLDD Nix check](repo:flake.nix) compares evaluated service placement with the\ncommitted public projection and rendered view. [Properties and units](repo:marshall/test/architecture/hldd_views_test.exs)\nreject host/role drift and missing explanations. The [native browser](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/hldd/browser.py)\nchecks that the deployed release fixture renders both visual sections individually.\nThese checks do not contact or establish the health of either physical host."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10970
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[The browser editor review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-10-browser-editor.md)\nrecords the official/OpenVSCode source comparison and retained historical failures\nunder [ADR-0153](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0153-official-source-browser-editor.md).\nThe corrected startup-release snapshot passed complete NixOS and Ubuntu\nFirecracker family scripts in 805 and 829 seconds total build/execution, including\ncurrent physical readiness, policy/controls, recovery and Origin/sign-out refusal.\nThe later final package `7fdh`/release `7d2415` passed complete standalone native\nacceptance in 342 seconds, driver 323.30 seconds, including personal install/\nreversion, core/System refusal, actual Rust/Elixir and broker/worker recovery.\nIts authorizer and TLS relay are fixture adapters. Family results retain earlier\nrelease `73ad` and their pre-template snapshot; final-release family and this\nbrowser-launcher merge's native integration are separately unrecorded. The\n`63a1443c`/release `73ad` host activation establishes installed identity rather\nthan a production browser journey. Periodic template sweeps, production editor\nacceptance and recurring browser history remain separate.\nSynthetic package bridge/roster evidence cannot be promoted to full service\nauthentication or guest isolation. Native veth packet-mark acceptance retains its\nLinux network scope. Recurring probe declaration does not prove production\ncredentials, completed browser journeys or seven-day history."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10974
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[The fleet network](repo:fleet/fleet-network.nix) uses pinned WireGuard peers and\ndistinct host credentials. Overalls admits fleet TLS APIs on 5443, Enrico on\n5445 and the diagnostic log store entry on 8443 (verified TLS, path-scoped\ninsert/reader credentials; ADR-0127). The tunnel does not replace service TLS\nor application authorization. Guest prefixes cannot become host-management\npaths. Public ingress, guest private services, fleet APIs and administrator\naccess are separate boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10978
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[The host profile](repo:nix/platform/hosts/profile.nix) derives Lilly identities, pool GUIDs,\nbudgets and credential paths from enrollment. Each host owns its ZFS guest data,\nLilly journals, Underclass pool and service caches. A second worker does not make\nthose disks shared or make PostgreSQL highly available. On authority loss, existing\nphysical execution and expiring service leases follow their own fences; recovery\nmust reconcile observed execution before granting new authority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10982
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[View properties and units](repo:marshall/test/architecture/hldd_views_test.exs)\ncheck source drift, domain/resource ownership and missing explanations. The\n[NixOS browser scenario](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/hldd/browser.py) checks the release's\nauthenticated section delivery and rendered diagrams. Neither establishes that a\nresource policy is correct."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10986
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`/hldd` lists sections. Each `/hldd/SECTION` page loads one chapter; previous and\nnext links follow handbook order. The contents page sends no chapter bodies, and\nsection changes remove previous diagrams. Direct section links require the same\ncurrent login as the contents page."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10990
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`/run/overalls-workspace/status` shows `waiting`, `cloning`, `done` or `failed` to\nlogin users. The unit runs in both NixOS and Ubuntu images and is not a readiness\ndependency. Clones of a machine copy the checkout and receive no new source."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10994
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`Audi.HTTP.EditorAssetsPublisher` is an OTP child inside public Audi, not another\nfleet role. Marshall and Audi modules default to the same approved source-built\npackage. Current immutable assets, host/service readiness and image capability\ngate editor discovery; per-request Editor grants remain separate. The broker and\nworkers live inside each NixOS/Ubuntu guest, not on the outer host. Lilly alone\nadmits the dedicated marked connector, while the broker checks boot/source/account\nand personal systemd ownership. Browser communication expires on lost authority;\nworker processes and home data have their separate native lifetime."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10998
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`Audi.HTTP.EditorAssetsPublisher` is an OTP child of the existing public Audi\nservice, not a separately deployed role. It validates the approved immutable\nbootstrap files and proves live serving before each report through a fresh verified\nTLS HEAD on its configured local IPv4 listener. The proof has a 1.5-second deadline,\nbounded headers and exact hash SNI/Host/current-release matching; missing immutable\nheaders, wrong trust or suspended acceptors withhold readiness. Healthy publication\nruns every two seconds; Marshall receipts expire after five seconds. Unsupported control versions stop publication. Audi serves\nonly the reviewed index, fake and service-worker bootstrap files from that release\non neutral webview hash hosts. The asset path needs no guest connector or user\ncredential. Missing/mismatched assets withhold editor discovery.\nThe actual healthy/wrong-release/wrong-SNI/suspended-listener regression passed\nwithin eight local Audi front checks. Native multi-gateway discovery remains a\nseparate acceptance boundary."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11002
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`CODEX_CA_CERTIFICATE`, `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`, `SSL_CERT_FILE`,\n`REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` and `NIX_SSL_CERT_FILE` point to\n`/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt`. NixOS supplies session and PAM variables. Ubuntu exports\nthem through `/etc/profile.d/overalls-coding-clis.sh`, global Bash startup and\nPAM's `/etc/environment`, so native terminal and SSH accounts retain verified\nTLS. Changes apply to rebuilt images for future provisioning. Native user\nsettings retain ordinary precedence and may override defaults where supported;\nguest network and Marshall authority checks remain independent."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11006
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`Marshall.Threads` is a user-facing orchestration boundary above\n`Marshall.Compute` and `Marshall.Agents`. A pending thread is durable before its\nOban provisioning worker runs; the thread mutation and job insert share a\ntransaction. The worker uses a machine admission key, records the admitted VM,\nwaits for an observed running state, binds the automatically registered agent\nroot and low-level thread, and submits the initial objective before reporting\nready. The machine key fences writes from stale provisioning attempts.\nThread reads and mutations recheck creator/team membership and current canonical\nthread grants. The current binding contributes those roles to existing machine\nCedar authority without copying VM grants; machine-only grants do not expose the\ntask. Sharing locks the thread before its current VM, while independent VM/team\nauthority remains additive. Prompts and terminals retain their existing current\nmachine and personal-session checks. Archiving records\ndeleting and delegates teardown to Compute; retained physical archival history\npermits the final archived state. Authorized archived transcript reads validate\nthe paired root/thread binding. Reopen retains the create request identity,\nclears the retired binding and queues a fresh machine admission in one\ntransaction. PaperTrail and durable generation rows retain retired bindings;\nauthorized readers can browse previous transcript generations after reopen\nthrough the read-only generation selector."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11010
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`Marshall.Threads` records a pending thread, its typed machine specification and\nthe Oban provisioning job in one transaction. After commit, the worker calls\nCompute admission with a durable machine key and records the admitted VM\nidentity before waiting for an observed running state. It binds the registered\nagent root/thread, applies the selected mode and submits the initial objective\nwith a stable request key before reporting ready. Duplicate jobs reuse the same\nadmission and initial turn; every worker write checks the current machine key\nso a retired attempt cannot overwrite a reopened thread."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11014
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`elixir/request_accounting/` is the shared typed request-accounting library.\n`Overalls.RequestAccounting.Producer` performs private quality-marker and local\nUnix RPC I/O for durable admission custody. `Aggregate` provides the immutable\ncounter/histogram model and validates snapshot construction; each application\nretains eligibility, classification, concurrency, persistence and publication.\nThe library remains independent of best-effort diagnostics. Protocol measurement\ntypes and codecs remain pure; ADR-0142 confines outbound control WebSocket I/O\nto `Control.Client` and `WebSocket.Connection`, with application-owned credentials\nand trust. Protocol stores no durable credentials or domain authority.\nAn unsupported control version is terminal for that client instance: pending\nrequests settle as unproven, further requests remain disconnected, and recovery\nrequires a replacement client after the incompatible version is corrected.\nThe original client's delayed reconnect was reproduced after60.3seconds. The\nstopped-phase repair passed the five-check client file in167.3seconds before\nthe queued-connect and HTTP426 extension. The expanded six-check client\nfile subsequently passed in288.0seconds. Its generated loopback history\nretains a61000ms observation and does not prove arbitrary future absence or\nnative fleet rollout. The refined queued-connect wire oracle subsequently\npassed the six-check file in318.9seconds and caught the targeted retry mutant.\nThe heartbeat ownership review reproduced early replacement-socket traffic in\n10.4seconds. The timer-reference repair passed its focused reconnect property\nin47.8seconds before integration. Retired-reference redelivery passed the\nintegrated property in45.9seconds and caught a ref-matching mutant in6.2seconds.\nProtocol249, Lilly503, Marshall1466 and Galileo31-check gates passed with\nzero-error forced Dialyzer; the Protocol gate predates the extended test and\nis reused only for unchanged production source. Incoming-main integration,\nverified TLS reconnect histories and native fleet acceptance remain open.\nThe loopback test's100ms transit allowance is not a production latency guarantee.\nMarshall, Audi, Brady, Porsche, Galileo and Enrico package the explicit\nlocal library inputs. Run its pinned `mix precommit` and each changed consumer's\ngate. The native `otel-request-recorder` fixture separately exercises the actual\nBEAM producer and Rust recorder; unit/type checks do not establish application\ntransport acceptance or production history."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11018
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`freebsd-kernel-candidate` is a separate complete cross-built kernel, while\n`illumos-kernel-object-check` compiles the modern framework, all consumers and\nthe custom socket/transport code. The latter emits ELF object and input/hash\nevidence; it does not emit natively linked kernel modules or a bootable image.\nThe separate native link/install helper verified and linked all nine illumos\nmodules in the matching scratch guest, installed them into an alternate ZFS boot\nenvironment and rebuilt its boot archive. The original was preserved and the\nguest shut down cleanly. That helper refuses the running root and cannot grant\nnative admission. The corrected clone passes QEMU boot and a native TCP bind;\nFirecracker virtio-vsock execution remains open.\nThe recorded native Lyra package linked eighteen production executables for both libc families,\nwith separate test-only probes.\nIts exact interpreters, non-executable stacks and absence of Nix/Linux runtime\nreferences are checked. The official OmniOS SDK carries explicit content hashes\nand archived-source TLS acquisition limits. None of these artifacts replaces\nthe unavailable receipts selected by the image builders."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11022
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`lilly-boot-report` starts after `lilly-readiness`, is required by nothing and\nuses `Type=exec`, so the boot transaction can finish during its 30 s completion\npolling window. An in-flight completion probe can add its 20 s tool timeout;\nlater report queries each have that timeout too. This does not meet ADR-0154's\naccepted at-most-30-second completion wait; correcting that bound remains open.\nA guest that never finishes booting (for\nexample an isolated fixture whose roster sync has no authority) still reports\nthe manager instants and timeline with `Bootup is not yet finished`. Every\nreport line carries the `lilly-boot-report: ` marker and up to 200 printable\npayload characters, and each section is line-bounded; the report holds unit names\nand times only, never credentials or payloads."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11026
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`lilly-hostd` is the privileged executor of every utility Lilly runs under a lease\n([ADR-0176](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0176-host-concurrency-single-writers-and-effects.md)\ndecision 4). Its boundary:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11030
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`native-fixture-unit` executes the host parser/refusal/cleanup/Git/FAT boundary\nsuite with pinned Python, Git, OpenSSH, OpenSSL, FAT and block tools. The recorded\nNix gate passed 59 tests in a four-second build. This is host verification\nevidence; no native boot, isolation, growth or recovery follows from it. The\nshared service machine accepts an explicit FreeBSD/illumos test image, preserving\nthe same service/growth/isolation/recovery/crash scenario boundaries without\nconstructing an admitted production manifest."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11034
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`native-reset-probes` cross-builds isolated C probes for FreeBSD and illumos.\n`native-freebsd-reset` passed its nested-KVM gate in 232 seconds: a peerless\nidentity socket fenced and pending accept/read/write operations woke with errors\nafter a real pause/full-snapshot/resume sequence, with fresh sockets refused until\na new VM. Exact test image/kernel/probe/firmware/VMM receipts are in the native\nreview. The private Unix fixture API and disposable snapshot/memory do not grant\nproduction authority; this does not establish DMA/race stress, OmniOS, physical,\nservice or recovery acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11038
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`new` selects OSS when `--profile` is omitted, as does the browser creation form.\nUse `new --profile customer` to choose Customer explicitly. Customer machines\npermit only owner SSH and serial-terminal access. All outbound network\nconnections, workspace sharing, and web/port exposure are disabled.\nSSH reply traffic is allowed only on admitted owner connections. Choose an\nappropriate non-Customer profile when networking or collaboration is required."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11042
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`overalls open URL [-- COMMAND]`, for a supported GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg or Bitbucket link,\nvalidates the URL locally with the shared closed grammar, then runs Porsche\n`new --source URL --profile oss --request-id overalls-open-HEX --timeout 600 --json`\nwith the configured origin host and strict CA-pinned host keys. When the result is\n`succeeded` with outcome `running`, it connects to the reported `ssh_host` through\nthe managed Audi ProxyCommand. A fixed remote script waits for\n`/run/overalls-workspace/status` to become `done`, then changes into\n`/workspace/REPO` and starts a login shell or the given command. An invalid URL\nmakes no SSH call; a failed or timed-out clone exits nonzero without opening a\nshell in a partial tree. See [repository launch workspaces](#guests)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11046
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`overalls-logging-health.service` reads filesystem metadata and allowed localhost\nFluent Bit counters independently of the logging service. It exposes numeric\nqueue bytes, oldest chunk age, disk headroom, output budgets/counter validity and\nshard checkpoint presence on port 9467. Failed counter reads expose invalidity\nand omit unknown counters; they do not invent healthy zeroes. Central Prometheus\nscrapes both authenticated host addresses directly. Health remains reachable when\nFluent Bit or its destination is stopped, subject to host/network availability."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11050
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`pin MACHINE` and `unpin MACHINE` are non-physical mutations: they resolve the\nmachine like `rm`, call the Access pin coordinator as the SSH actor, answer\n`{id, name, pinned}` synchronously and replay by request id. `rm` on a machine\nits owner has pinned answers the closed `machine_pinned` reason; Porsche refines\n`machine_pinned` and `pin_limit` into named conflict exits and prints a fixed\nhint (\"unpin it first: unpin MACHINE\"). `ls` gains no pinned column. The\n[SSH command tests](repo:marshall/test/marshall/ssh/commands_test.exs) cover\npin, unpin, the refused `rm` and the admitted `rm` after unpin; no native Porsche\njourney has run them."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11054
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`pin MACHINE` keeps a machine at the top of your lists in the browser; `unpin\nMACHINE` removes your pin. Pins are personal, and you may hold up to twenty. While\nthe machine's owner has it pinned, nobody can delete it: `rm` answers\n`machine_pinned` until the owner runs `unpin MACHINE` or unpins it in the browser."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11058
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`relocate MACHINE` moves the same machine and disk to another eligible physical\nhost. Porsche never accepts a destination: Marshall selects one from current\ncapacity, image availability and transfer readiness. The machine keeps its name,\nidentity, sharing and private address."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11066
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`services.marshall.marvinPackage` selects the immutable unsigned Chrome/Firefox\nlauncher package and defaults to the repository's `browser` output. Set it to\n`null` to disable extension downloads. After loading other environment files, the\nwrapper removes any inherited `OVERALLS_MARVIN_RELEASE` and supplies the selected\nNix-store output, or an explicit empty value for null. Environment files cannot\noverride this selection. Public `/bin/chrome.zip`, `/bin/firefox.xpi`,\n`/bin/marvin/manifest.json` and `/bin/marvin/sha256/ARTIFACT` serve only the fixed\npackage artifacts and their metadata. The CLI's release manifest stays separate.\nThe extension server origin is configured by its user, not this server option. The\nextension requests optional site access only for that exact saved origin when its\npresence detector is configured; this local marker never changes server authority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11070
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`services.overallsBrowserProbes.instances.nixos` and `.ubuntu` declare separate\nfive-minute browser journeys. Each requires private absolute runtime `fixtureFile`\nand `storageStateFile` paths and fleet OpenTelemetry. Overalls declares both;\ncredentials are separately provisioned from an actual completed operator browser\nsign-in. They are never Nix values or automatic authentication bypasses. The\nclosed fixture pins the machine, expected personal origin, guest OS, username/UID\nand pre-existing Markdown probe file. Missing credentials leave the source without\nusable coverage. Do not infer health from an enabled unit; see [monitoring](#monitoring)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11074
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`services.overallsLogging.outputs` requires explicit sources and output budgets.\nGalileo and Underclass are ordinary application sources. Stable journal shards\nretain separate cursors; enabling a new source never authorizes restricted audit\nor Firecracker data. `healthAddress` defaults loopback and may use only the\nauthenticated host fleet address; `healthPort` defaults to 9467 and is separate\nfrom Fluent Bit's localhost counters. Source snapshots expose numbers, not archives."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11078
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`services.overallsOtelFleet.enable` selects a `central` Overalls or `worker`\nDungarees collector over the authenticated fleet network. Metrics and trace\nretention default independently to ninety days; objectives keep seven days.\n`services.overallsFleetExporter.enable` supplies bounded loopback resource metrics\nwithout a Marshall credential or diagnostic upload."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11082
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`services.overallsOtelFleet.metricsRetentionDays` and `tracesRetentionDays` default\nto 90, with a supported range of 7–365. They change store retention independently\nof seven-day targets and burns. Inspect oldest available samples, Grafana's\nsource-status/evidence tables and capacity alerts after activation. The metrics\nstore has no automatic size-triggered deletion; ten GiB is a warning budget.\nTempo's configured quota still bounds trace storage. Increasing retention never\nrestores already deleted observations. Resolve capacity pressure by reviewing\nvolume and storage allocation without silently shortening the declared horizon."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11086
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "cgroup v2 paths whose root processes may open a lilly-hostd session.\nLilly's own unit by default; an empty list admits any root peer\n(machine tests whose probe BEAMs run outside lilly.service)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:11090
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "pg_dump/pg_restore tools compatible with Marshall's database server."
msgstr ""

