#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3741
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A running VM establishes execution, not published route or guest SSH readiness.\nPositive integration verification waits for the current verified HTTPS gateway\nkey and actual SSH success. The native withdrawal fixture shares one ninety-second\nbudget across route publication and SSH, with forty-five seconds per SSH attempt.\nAuthority refusal probes remain single attempts and require their exact forbidden\nevidence; transport failure or an unpublished route does not establish refusal."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3835
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Account authorization controls preference updates. Validated identity is inert execution metadata bound to the machine’s admitted snapshot and current boot. Guest services atomically publish protected Git configuration; malformed metadata refuses. Identity values must not enter diagnostics. Guest sudo and Git local configuration retain their existing authority. Native cross-OS and production rollout acceptance remain separate from BEAM property evidence.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3847
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Accounts rename and remove their own passkeys in the **Sign-in and passkeys**\nsection of the account page; both\nrequire a passkey verified within the last fifteen minutes and a live stored login.\nA missing, blank, or over-80-character name is refused closed without touching\nstored state. Removing a credential that is not proving the current elevation\nkeeps MFA, every session and that elevation. Removing the proving credential ends\nthe elevation immediately; the login survives and a remaining passkey can\nre-verify. An ordinary account may remove its last passkey, which disables MFA\nand revokes every browser credential; an administrator can never remove their\nlast passkey. Accounts enroll at most ten passkeys: registration refuses at the\nlimit without consuming the challenge, which stays usable after the inventory\ndrops back below ten, and failed ceremonies consume their challenge without\nenabling MFA. The **SSH keys** section is owner-scoped; accounts hold at most one\nhundred keys and removal is permanent. A key's description can be renamed in place\n(`rename_for_user`): ownership is rechecked under the key's row lock, only the\nlabel changes, another account's key refuses as not found, and the change emits\n`ssh.key_renamed`. The\n[credential self-service review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-26-credential-self-service.md)\nrecords generated inventory histories, the label-crash repair and the packaged\nbrowser journey; execution status is recorded in [scenario coverage](#coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3897
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Admission reserves checkpoint headroom before capture. Physical allocation credit\nrequires confirmed owned checkpoint datasets and is capped by their reservation;\nunknown artifacts consume pool space without granting credit. A durable restore\nconsumption record precedes possible execution. An uncertain consumed checkpoint\ncannot be replayed against disks that may already have changed. Storage retention,\ncompute absence and successful reclamation are independent facts."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3913
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "After a restart during snapshot load, Lilly may continue the exact claimed live\nVMM. A paused process needs the same launch identity, PID/start time and full\nconfinement proof before a Resume API call; it never reloads the checkpoint.\nMissing or changed authority leaves restoration fenced."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3933
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "After both gateways report their fresh host key, wildcard installation and\neditor assets, verify a VM can pause with CPU/RAM released and resume its existing\nprocess state. Then exhaust its original host's capacity, keep an authorized SSH,\nHTTPS or editor connection open to that original gateway, admit a compatible\nsecond host, and verify that same incoming request completes after paused-copy\nrelocation. Refused credentials and revoked stored logins must end the wait;\ncapacity alone has no short overall timeout. A wake receipt grants no guest\nsocket, and discovery or a successful TCP handshake is not memory-retention\nevidence. The [fleet scenario](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/fleet/scenarios/suspension.py)\ncontains these acceptance operations; test presence does not establish a passing\nnative run or a production rollout."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3937
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "After relocation, the original gateway uses one authenticated fleet relay hop to\nthe destination gateway over WireGuard. Its single-use capability is bound to\ncurrent placement and the specific admitted service/account. The destination\nconsumes authority through Marshall and uses its local Lilly connector. Peer\ninput cannot select arbitrary upstream hosts or ports. Revoked, expired, foreign\nor replayed authority refuses before guest connection. TCP acceptance and waiting\nare not evidence of guest readiness."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:3945
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "After the acknowledged snapshot response, Lilly records `copied` under the\ncurrent placement/capture authority without reacquiring the pool lock. This\nprivate journal update changes neither dataset ownership nor allocation credit.\nSealing and ready publication still serialize through the pool. A merely\n`capturing` journal never infers success from files or repeats the snapshot."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:4020
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "An ambiguous or failed memory restore retains its execution fence and storage\nholds. It cannot replay the consumed checkpoint or use the former cold destination\nboot retry. Historical retry receipts remain readable; new retry admission refuses.\nExplicit Stop retires memory before an intentional later cold Start."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:4166
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Audi's fleet-private relay listener joins the existing WireGuard peer topology.\nWhen a sleeping workload moves, its original discovery gateway keeps waiting\nconnections and forwards one hop using a single-use destination-bound capability.\nThe receiving Audi connects only to its local Lilly under renewed Marshall\nauthority. This is neither public relay exposure nor guest-subnet routing.\nFull compatible-fleet exhaustion leaves durable demand pending; PostgreSQL,\ntransport and destination capacity remain separate failure boundaries. Declared\nplacement does not prove multi-host availability; the suspension fleet test is\nthe corresponding native acceptance boundary."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:4195
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "BEAM properties establish admission, coalescing and refusals. Native RAM\ncontinuity, filesystem coherence and multi-node availability need separately\nrecorded machine evidence in the\n[suspension review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-memory-suspension.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:4325
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Boundary: ADR-0200 accepts a source-built native Code OSS candidate and SYSTEM broker on dedicated gateway-only TCP 4444. The separate windows-editor-v1 row requires manifest v7 with a closed immutable editor descriptor; portable bundle v4 retains that tuple. Existing windows-v1/v5 remains editor false and no production catalog selects the candidate. The candidate binds current MMDS execution, the closed preface, locked roster reconciliation, canonical local SAM SID and S4U token, personal profile, exact Job Object/process/pipe custody and a private recovery receipt."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:4329
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Boundary: Account-owned preferences become immutable VM configuration; existing execution and guest custody boundaries remain."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:4405
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Boundary: Current Cedar/key/publication/editor authority admits coalesced durable wake only; compute and private checkpoint capacity, sealed same-boot continuation, single-use restore and destination relay consumption independently fence execution."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:4677
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Boundary: One LiveView at /settings mounts through the AuthenticationPrivacy tag and LiveUserAuth's :security mode, so the profile, passkey, SSH key and Git sections all recheck the persisted login on mount, every event, every message and every parameter change; mutations use the socket actor through User.security, Marshall.SSH, User.set_time_zone and User.git_settings; fragments (#profile, #security, #ssh-keys, #git-settings) carry no authority and return_to keeps its allowlist."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5027
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Cancellation is accepted only before the handoff commit, from any phase up to\n`handoff`, and restores the original running, paused or stopped source under fresh\nauthority. Deletion supersedes any unfinished phase through `deleting_copies`."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5097
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Closing an observer tolerates its concurrent normal port exit; an already\nclosed port must not crash the VM actor. Lock-free execution reads independently\ncheck that both the actor and runtime publishers are alive, rather than waiting\nfor the shared view to process their monitor messages. Stale table rows cannot\nextend boot, ingress or live execution authority after either publisher dies.\nThe [publisher-liveness property](repo:lilly/test/lilly/execution_test.exs)\nholds monitor cleanup pending while checking those refusals; this is BEAM\nauthority evidence, not physical VMM-death evidence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5458
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Deploy the coordinated protocol, Marshall migration, Lilly/hostd and Audi builds\nbefore admitting pause/resume operations. Lilly must retain immutable checkpoint\nmemory backing until the restored VMM exits. A failed or incompatible restore\nmust refuse; replacing it with a cold boot loses the promised process state.\nThe [fleet declarations](repo:fleet/fleet-network.nix) enable the Audi relay on\nOveralls and Dungarees through their existing keyed `fleet0` peers. TCP 9445 binds\nonly `10.90.0.1`/`10.90.0.2`; its firewall permits declared WireGuard peers. It is\nnot a public listener and does not route guest subnets."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5561
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Elixir properties currently use the test-only Hegel frontend ref `b0dec2a`\n(`b0dec2abf868c7036950c00256a2ebf5af2929e7`) under\n[ADR-0191](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0191-elixir-hegel-property-verification.md).\nProtocol properties run once per declared case count with fixed seeds and no\nexample database or printed replay blobs. A local draw boundary records literal\nlabels and redaction markers; generated terminal bytes and payloads do not enter\nthe native replay printer. Bounded generators retain exact string/collection\nconstraints, malformed peer shapes and unsigned 64-bit GUIDs through the engine's\nsigned integer interface. Transport cases release their own listeners, supervised\nchildren and fixture messages before another case or replay."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5569
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Enablement also requires explicit Marshall gateway enrollment. On the trusted\nMarshall operator entry point, use new private output paths and each host's\ncurrent enrolled Lilly UUID:"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5629
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Every machine page (Terminal, Access, Settings, Agents, Resize and the Boot log view)\nshares one header. A bordered heading box carries the machine name, then an\noperating-system label that is the registered display name of the machine's\nimmutable image (`[NixOS]`, `[Ubuntu 26.04 LTS]`, `[Windows Server 2025]`), then\nits state label (`[Running]`, `[Stopping]`, …); a machine whose image row is\nmissing shows no operating-system label rather than a placeholder. Every status\nlabel is one bracketed word on the character grid whose text names the state;\nseverity is weight, style or inverse video, never colour: `[Pinned]` and the\noperating system are secondary-colour metadata, `[Stopped]` is plain text,\n`[Running]` is bold, transitions (`[Starting]`, `[Stopping]`, `[Deleting]`,\n`[Pending]`, `[Unknown]`) are italic, and `[Failed]` is a bold inverse-video\nblock one row tall with a one-cell margin inside the block. The label carries\nthe observed state in `data-state` and the severity in `data-tone` (`danger`,\n`success`, `pending`, `muted`, or none for neutral states) on the Overview,\nthe Machines list and the machine header alike; the tone is decided in the\nsame order as the text, so a failed machine with delete intent reads\n`[Deleting]` as a transition rather than `[Failed]`. Beneath them\nsit the resource facts (vCPU, memory, disk, profile) and the machine ID with a\none-line **Copy** control; the authorized power actions and **Open VS Code**\nstay on the right and wrap under the summary on phone widths. The \"Connect from\nyour computer\" box follows, collapsed to its heading while it holds commands\n(an unavailable explanation starts open, and the viewer's open or closed\nchoice survives live updates). Expanded, it shows one explanation and two steps, \"Install and\nconnect\" lists the Linux / macOS and Windows installer commands side by side,\neach with a platform label and **Copy** button, and \"Already installed\" shows a\nsingle `ssh HOST` command for every platform, replacing the former Linux/macOS\n`ssh HOST` and Windows `overalls ssh HOST` pair (see \"Windows workstations\").\nCopying any\ncommand or the machine ID writes only the displayed text to the user's own\nclipboard and reports \"Machine ID copied.\" or \"Command copied.\" in the status\nline; a refused clipboard write tells the user to select the text manually. The\nsame image name appears as an **Operating system** row in Settings → Resources,\nbeneath the machine name in the Machines list (the table gains no column, so it\nstill fits the main column) and before the state label in the Overview preview. The view projection exposes only the image's display name:\nmanifests, placements and reservations stay private, and the label is shown\nonly to principals who can already read the machine."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5714
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Exact local fleet /32; never a guest or public listener address."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5766
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Explicit keyed fleet /32 peers allowed to present single-use relay capabilities."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:5974
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Generated cases own their SQL sandbox, supervised children, sockets and temporary\nfiles. Cleanup runs before the next case, shrinking attempt or replay. ExUnit\nsetup runs once per test and cannot substitute for case isolation. Supervised\ncleanup checks the original PID before stopping a child, so a reused child ID\ndoes not grant cleanup authority over its replacement. Fresh-VM boot histories\nretain the complete mutation matrix and generated exploration. Measured aggregate\nbudgets can reach three hours; each individual boot retains a 30-second deadline\nand a five-second forced-termination allowance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6007
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "GitHub Actions runs on privileged, cached Namespace runners so Nix builds keep\ntheir sandbox. Every job installs multi-user Nix, starts `nix-daemon` so builds\nrun as `nixbld` build users as on the fleet, installs the devenv revision pinned\nby `devenv.lock`, and publishes what it builds to the `overalls` Cachix cache.\n`publish-cache.yml` runs on trunk pushes and pull requests: repository gates,\n`mix precommit` for each Elixir project, and every enabled non-machine flake\ncheck. `nixos-machine-tests.yml` runs daily or on dispatch and fans out one job\nper NixOS machine test selected from `meta.ci` (Inception is excluded); its\ndiscovery evaluates only that metadata, and each matrix job builds its test.\n`nix-upgrades.yml` updates `devenv.lock` weekly and pins the flake's\n`buildNixpkgs` to the same nixpkgs. Two further weekly jobs apply security\nupdates only. `mix-security-upgrades.yml` runs `mix hex.audit` (Hex 2.5.3 or\nnewer) in every project with a `mix.lock` and updates packages with a security\nadvisory or security retirement; `cargo-security-upgrades.yml` runs\n`cargo audit` against every tracked `Cargo.lock` and updates vulnerable crates\nwith `cargo update --package NAME@VERSION`. Both resolve affected builds through\nthe flake's `lib.dependencyPins` registry, which lists each entry's lock files,\nthe derivation that must build and its fixed-output fetches. Changed entries get\ntheir `dependency-hashes.json` keys faked, rebuilt and filled from the reported\nmismatch by drvPath, then must build before the commit is pushed to trunk. Mix\nlibraries without an entry compile with warnings as errors; a tracked\n`Cargo.lock` without an entry stops the Cargo job. A finding with no allowed\nfixed version fails the run after fixable updates are pushed. Bot-refreshed\nhashes are authenticated by that build, not by human source review, and the\nbuilds use the build nixpkgs rather than every consumer's package set. A run is\nexecution evidence only for the commit it ran; a selected matrix entry is not a\npassing result, and nested-KVM availability on the hosted runners is unverified."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6187
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Incompatible executors return an explicit upgrade-required state. Roll out\ncompatible consumers and guest executors before enabling operations; use the\nexisting Linux updater and rebuilt Windows images. No additional listener,\nservice, private Git credential flow or plugin marketplace is introduced."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6232
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Installation observability: the image build keeps an `evidence/` directory\nbeside the artifacts. The QEMU driver records a timestamped timeline of\nlifecycle events from the QMP socket (each `RESET` is a Windows Setup stage\nboundary), the guest's serial checkpoints from every unattend pass (Windows PE,\nspecialize, SetupComplete), change-triggered PNG screenshots and disk usage\nsamples; `SetupComplete.cmd` publishes the Windows Setup, specialize and\ndriver-binding logs plus a device inventory on the EFI system partition, which\nthe build reads back from the raw disk with mtools. A stalled or failed\ninstallation can therefore be placed between two known checkpoints and examined\nwithout rerunning it. The launcher keeps Firecracker's own log and metrics next\nto the disk copy for the same reason. See\n[docs/guests/windows-guests.md](repo:docs/guests/windows-guests.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6252
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Interrupted sealing skips repair only when both memory and device-state files\nare regular, singly linked, root/service-owned and mode `0444`. Incomplete files\ncan be repaired only after independently confirming a writable owned dataset;\nan incomplete read-only checkpoint refuses. Each ownership request names one\nfixed output file through hostd's existing native file grammar."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6516
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Marshall's common runtime guards reject invalid inference retention, ingress\nports, relocation readiness timeouts and default/Inception image identities\nbefore application startup. The fresh Mix property checks 34 cases in three\nshuffled histories with test-build beams.\nIt requires a specific fixed refusal, no reviewed application/Repo/endpoint/\nBandit startup or surviving Marshall state, private input/path-safe output and\npositively calibrated tracing. Valid\ndefault/boundary controls load runtime configuration without starting Marshall.\nThese local checks establish neither production-release startup nor configured\nserving. Production secret/TLS/mail/budget faults, optional integrations,\npackaged startup, file replacement and hosted faults remain open. Evidence:\n[boot property](repo:marshall/test/marshall/operations/invalid_runtime_boot_property_test.exs)\nand [execution review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-09-29-antithesis-property-audit.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6557
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Memory/device snapshots occupy private sibling checkpoint datasets, outside the\nordinary VM disk, clone and backup streams. Two slots are bound into each VMM\njail before launch; the mapped restore slot remains immutable while a later\npause captures into the other slot. Files are sealed only after the VMM is gone.\nThe patched VMM aborts snapshot creation if its block drain/flush barrier fails;\na successful API response is required before sealing a checkpoint. Host sync alone\ncannot establish asynchronous device-state ordering. A coherent disk snapshot and\nmemory checkpoint travel as separate authenticated\ncomponents during a paused relocation. Adoption validates both before handoff."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6581
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Multi-disk profiles move as one placement dataset stream. Every expected drive\nmust exist on adoption. A partial drive set cannot become a running destination.\nRelocation and clone transfers use one owned source snapshot, `lilly-b`, taken\nof a quiesced disk and sent as one full stream; native policy admits only\n`lilly-b` and `lilly-backup` snapshot names on guest datasets. Relocation holds\nno temporary disk snapshot headroom because the quiesced source cannot diverge.\nDisk transfer journals reject the retired pre-copy schema: a journal in the removed\npre-copy shape (`baseline_guid`, incremental stream or a `lilly-a` slot) fails\nclosed. ADR-0159 removed the one-way startup migration after hosts were purged\nof that state.\nBackup consistency and relocation consistency are separate guarantees."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6658
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "New `/threads`/Orb provisioning enables a fixed four-hour idle policy. The clock\nrecords tool admission and completion, not model output, heartbeats or the last\nhuman message. Admitted tools and queued/running turns defer capture under the\nVM lock. A prompt on a suspended machine requests wake before tool execution.\nSuccessful wake grants a fresh four-hour window. A running prompt or repeated\nnetwork request does not extend it. Owner pins prevent automatic suspension;\npersonal pins held by other users confer no such control."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6666
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "New identity-bearing admissions require an image manifest advertising `git_identity_version: 1`. Old firmware agents reject unknown MMDS fields, so artifacts and consumers must be upgraded before enabling these admissions. An older image without this capability refuses new machine creation rather than receiving incompatible boot metadata. Existing machines without an identity snapshot continue to omit the metadata. Native reviewed-image qualification remains a separate requirement.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6670
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "New machines capture the creating account’s Git username and email from Settings. The defaults are Overalls and noreply@overalls.dev. Boot-bound metadata supplies a protected system Git include even without a repository checkout. Restart and relocation retain the snapshot; settings changes do not update existing machines. Ordinary Git configuration precedence and guest sudo permissions remain applicable."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6678
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "New thread/Orb machines opt into suspension four hours after the last admitted\nor completed tool call. Provisioning and successful wake initialize that window.\nActive admitted tools/turns defer suspension. Existing machines remain opted out.\nPrompts and authorized network access wake sleeping machines; requests to an\nalready running machine do not continually reset the tool clock. Only the owner's\npin prevents automatic suspension, and pinning a suspended machine requests wake."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6690
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "NixOS and Ubuntu embed the source-built `vscode-web` package and its Rust broker.\nThe Arch/Alma shared builder now carries the same optional pinned package pair,\noffline registered closure, GC roots, account broker and exact descriptor. Their\nearlier selected images omitted that payload and correctly display an unsupported\neditor explanation. Final evaluation and actual-filesystem closure/configuration/\nservice checks pass for editor-bearing Arch `0b3da3e8` and Alma `65e7700b`.\nThe actual Alma nine-node browser suite passed all twelve provisioning, editor,\npolicy, recovery, restart and current-authority subtests. Arch's equivalent\nnative suite passed all twelve stages on the captured same-image diagnostic\nreplay; its earlier broker-restart 502 remains unexplained. Neither native result\nqualifies the later trunk merge or a production browser session.\nBoth production replacement images passed fresh strict personal CLI SSH,\nstop/checkpoint/restart, repeat SSH, PAM/sudo checks and completed deletion.\nThe seven-family CLI milestone retains its earlier exact image IDs; neither\nthe isolated native browser fixture nor CLI access claims a production browser\nsession or physical isolation.\nExisting writable disks retain their original manifest and require a new machine\nfrom an editor-bearing image. See the\n[foreign Linux editor review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-foreign-linux-browser-editor.md)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:7272
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Relocation retires old-placement terminal authority. Reconnection is required;\nPTY reattachment across a move is not promised. Authorized SSH, published web\napplications and editor requests can wait through resume and automatic relocation\nwhile renewing their authentication and publication/account authority. Snapshot\ntransport reset does not preserve established sockets."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:7276
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Relocation uses one full disk copy, with a separate sealed memory/device\ncheckpoint for a running or paused source\n([ADR-0199](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0199-memory-suspension-and-demand-wake.md)).\nFresh destination connectivity precedes source quiescence. A running machine is\npaused, its coherent disk and memory state are copied, and the destination resumes\nthe same guest boot after committed handoff. An already paused machine remains\npaused after an explicit move. A stopped machine remains a disk-only move.\nThere is no pre-copy: downtime includes full transfer and restore, but does not\ninclude a guest reboot. The coordinator reserves destination disk, checkpoint\nand required compute capacity plus endpoint transfer slots before handoff."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:7463
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Required refusal: Foreign account updates and malformed Git identities refuse; settings changes cannot replace an existing machine snapshot. Images without version-1 Git identity support refuse new admissions."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:7471
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Required refusal: Foreign or revoked authority, missing/corrupt/incompatible checkpoints, replayed restore and arbitrary relay targets refuse. Network activity cannot override explicit stop intent; a user prompt or explicit start may start a stopped VM. Full compatible-fleet capacity leaves wake pending; it never cold boots to replace memory."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:7927
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Required refusal: The accepted windows-v1 editor capability remains false. Missing or mismatched image descriptors, foreign gateway or execution, stale roster account, ambiguous package/PATH custody, wrong SID/process/job/pipe and exhausted pending-I/O capacity must refuse. A foreign process cannot be terminated or adopted without exact custody; a portable test or cross-link cannot qualify native Windows browser admission."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8122
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Rotation invalidates the previous gateway generation and its browser cookies and\nstreams. Schedule this credential cutover together, encrypt each new token into\nits existing `HOST-audi` SecretSpec document, run `secrets -- check`, then deploy\nthe matching gateway configuration and restart Audi. Never print tokens or place\nplaintext output in the checkout. The four-argument gateway rotation form leaves\nrelay enrollment disabled; it is insufficient for automatic cross-host wake.\nDNS credentials and existing fleet WireGuard keys do not need rotation."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8274
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Settings defaults are Git username Overalls, Git email noreply@overalls.dev and enabled commit trailers. New machine admission captures identity atomically; cloned machines capture the creating account’s settings. Existing machines without a snapshot retain their configuration. Identity values are not diagnostic metadata.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8340
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Stop, delete and failure may discard a paused process left by interrupted capture\nor restore. This requires an active host lease, a reread terminal intent at least\nas new as the journal's authority, the exact local checkpoint binding and current\npaused-process confinement. Cleanup never promotes `capturing` to usable RAM.\nConfirmed unit/cgroup absence remains necessary before networking or checkpoint\nstorage can be reclaimed; a failed kill reply alone proves neither outcome."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8352
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Structured planning questions remain visible after reconnect. Current writers\ncan answer the active request; viewers cannot. A completed plan is rendered as\na sanitized card with Implement plan and Revise, while partial output cannot\nbe implemented. The server rechecks authority and the exact proposal identity\nbefore admitting a Work turn on the same machine. The composer stays above the\nnewest-first transcript throughout these interactions."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8365
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Suspended-machine discovery retains publication and identity lookup without\npretending the guest is reachable. Audi admits wake only after current SSH key,\nweb publication/login or editor authority. A waiting connection joins one durable\nwake and polls with bounded local work; it does not receive an execution grant.\nWhen compatible capacity is unavailable, the wake stays pending rather than\nsilently rebooting or exhausting a short retry budget."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8369
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Suspension adds no new deployed service. Marshall's existing PostgreSQL stores\nwake demand, idle clocks and current execution authority. Lilly retains sealed RAM\nand device state in private sibling ZFS datasets beside each owned VM disk, with\nhost-local single-use journals outside transferred storage. A suspended VM has no\nVMM/cgroup compute but still consumes disk/checkpoint capacity."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8373
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Suspension adds sensitive RAM storage and continuation authority. Checkpoint\nmemory, manifests and hashes never enter ordinary diagnostics, backup/clone\nstreams or public capabilities. Exact image/VMM/device and CPU/KVM compatibility\nmust be proven before loading; guest continuation uses the captured boot tuple\ninside a separately fenced current placement/epoch. A durable consumed marker\nprecedes possible execution, and ambiguous execution forbids replay."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8455
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: Account preference, codec and immutable snapshot property coverage; guest acceptance remains separately recorded."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8519
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: Ash state machines and Hegel histories exercise BEAM admission, timer boundaries, coalescing and execution fences. Native hostd tests cover API-only restore launch and fixed checkpoint stream derivation. The real fleet test definition asserts repeated capture, RAM-only nonce/PID/boot continuity, cgroup absence, running/paused moves and original authorized connections through measured source memory exhaustion; successful changed-source execution is not yet recorded here."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8779
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: Historical pre-observability records: SQL-backed Marshall selection passed 52 checks; full gate passed 1,532 checks including 256 properties and forced Dialyzer with zero errors. Protocol/Lilly/Audi component gates passed. Complete NixOS Marshall/Audi/Lilly/Firecracker browser login, unsafe Origin refusal, sign-out/WebSocket closure and fresh sign-in recovery passed. Ubuntu failed after expected-origin handoff at initial workbench visibility. The later exact image eligibility/workspace/domain/controller/SSH selection passed 58 checks (5 properties, 53 tests), seed 369052, in 19.9 seconds. The merged Marshall full gate passed 1,424 checks (237 properties, 1,187 tests; two excluded), seed 309061, with zero-error forced typing; later cosmetic toolbar verification is separate. Corrected merged NixOS completed the full native journey in 742 seconds total build plus execution; corrected Ubuntu reproduced initial workbench failure. This status review records no current-revision NixOS or Ubuntu browser sign-in, edit/save/readback and recovery receipt. Application gates do not establish native or production acceptance."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8895
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: Protocol tests pin the capability table and the windows-v1 manifest and bundle vectors; Lilly tests pin the agentless jail configuration, the vsock addition for windows-v1, the recorded readiness budget, the image configuration shape and template cacheability; Marshall tests bind the capabilities to the exact image ID for every agent set and exercise the per-capability surfaces; the windows-guest-unit evaluation checks the viosock driver, the agents directory, the probe task, the manifest arguments and the launcher's vsock device; the lyra check builds and type-checks the Windows target. Recorded on 2026-10-14: lyraWindows built overalls-winprobe.exe (PE32+, 320 000 bytes) through zig. The windows-guest machine test dials the probe through the launcher's vsock socket after the SAC prompt and reads its published log. The agents composition now also inspects the live Windows EFI log for the closed agentd startup line; this is present evidence only until the composed check runs. The staged Windows editor runtime and source recipes retain editor_capability=false; the editor-target-boundaries and windows-editor-runtime evaluations pass. The broker has 26 passing portable library and 7 Linux binary tests, Linux/Windows clippy and production Windows cross-link evidence. Thirty actual Windows library tests cover named-pipe timeout/cancellation and ACL descriptor/refusal checks; personal worker, task and browser lifecycle remain unrecorded. The separate editor candidate uses manifest v7/windows-editor-v1 and bundle v4 while this existing windows-v1 row remains editor false. The prior selected sealed e9ed22f1 image in live run 075200 reported ready while sshd was stopped and CLI SSH failed; that current failure is distinct from earlier dated fleet proofs."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9019
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: Structural gate over templates, stylesheets and presentation JavaScript; Playwright probe against the disposable fixture covering both themes, 390-pixel layouts, token contrast, JetBrains Mono availability, grid alignment on workspace routes, drawer focus containment and Escape, creation draft identity across reload, reduced-motion compliance, administration navigation, empty overview, session-free public documentation and the absence of any request to another origin. The machine header check asserts the bordered heading box holds the name, state label, operating-system label (image display name), machine ID and power actions on desktop and phone widths, and that the machine-ID and installer Copy controls write only the displayed text. The status-label check asserts the one-row inverse-video Failed, bold Running and plain Stopped presentation on the Overview and Machines pages in both themes and at phone width."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9027
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: The Windows runtime/source recipes, broker model/native source, bundle importer, fixed-task installer and native acceptance harness are present. Focused protocol and Lilly tests cover immutable v7/v4 identity, release preservation and old-row refusal. Portable tests cover finite identity, path and capacity decisions. Thirty native Windows library tests pass, including actual named-pipe timeout/cancellation and Windows ACL descriptor/refusal checks. No completed native Code OSS bundle, admitted Windows image, Win32 listener/account/worker/task lifecycle or authenticated Windows browser journey is recorded."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9075
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: The shared Alma/Arch builder packages the pinned editor/broker/direnv, exact descriptor, registered offline closure and GC roots. Final evaluation and actual-filesystem custody gates passed, along with two exact-release/image property and absent-descriptor checks. Alma's isolated nine-node nested-KVM suite passed all twelve provisioning, editor/terminal/webview, policy, recovery, restart and current-authority subtests. Its fixture image90eca504 differs from production65e7700b by private roots and policy files. Both production Arch0b3da3e8 and Alma65e7700b passed fresh strict personal CLI SSH, stop/checkpoint/restart, repeated SSH and owned deletion, plus four PAM/sudo smokes. Arch's captured same-image diagnostic replay passed all twelve native stages in618.2s; the earlier broker-restart502 remains unexplained. Both native sources precede the later trunk merge. No current-revision NixOS or Ubuntu native browser sign-in, edit/save/readback and recovery receipt is recorded here."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9147
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The FreeBSD static driver retains interrupt-visible resources until VM disposal,\nrefuses runtime detach/unload. Valid transport resets preserve listeners;\nfatal configuration changes still fence the device. Illumos has separate `drv/viosock` and `socketmod/sockvsock` modules plus\na sockfs reset path: valid reset preserves listeners while fencing connected\nand unbound sockets. Earlier receipts below describe the previous behavior. Its modern virtio ABI requires rebuilding\nall five consumers with the framework. Cross-compiled objects do not establish\nnative module linkage, native boot or reset safety. The native\n[link/install helper](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/guest/native-kernels/illumos/native_candidate.py)\nsubsequently linked all nine modules in the matching OmniOS guest, verified them\nbefore installing into a separate scratch ZFS boot environment and rebuilt its\nboot archive. The original boot environment was preserved and the guest shut\ndown cleanly. Its actual linked-hash and installation receipts retain runtime\nvalidation and native admission false. Reproducible full-disk assembly and\nloaded-module acceptance remain open. Native loading exposed Clang's default\nuserland syscall renaming; the corrected build undefines `_XOPEN_SOURCE`, checks\nactual ordinary syscall exports and binds the build recipe into its token. The\ncorrected clone boots QEMU and passes a native TCP bind and seven helper tests.\nThe common C frame/credit\nproperties and sanitizers execute on Linux and do not execute these wrappers."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9188
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The VM profile/address slot is reserved globally across Lilly hosts and remains\nunchanged across relocation. A restored guest retains its IP/MAC; a destination\nplacement does not allocate a new address. Checkpoint admission independently\ncompares the retained guest workload shape and slot with current authority."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9192
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Windows `windows-v1` contract deliberately excludes the browser editor.\nReadiness, roster SSH, ConPTY, workspace and agent evidence therefore remains\nscoped to those capabilities. The staged editor runtime/source keeps\n`editor_capability = false`; the portable broker model and Win32 launcher add\nno authority by their presence. If later admitted, identity would be checked\nat every layer: current MMDS execution and roster pair, the SSH authorizer's\nshared account lock, authenticated gateway preface, canonical local SAM SID and\nprofile, SYSTEM-owned job, exact process/PID-time/executable/pipe custody, and\nan immutable receipt before reuse. Foreign, stale or incomplete custody refuses\nwithout terminating processes or deleting personal files. No native Windows\nlistener, image admission or browser origin has been run, so sign-in,\nedit/save/readback, revocation and restart recovery remain gaps. Enabling an\neditor on Windows is a new architecture decision and requires reviewed image,\nagent/protocol, broker and browser evidence rather than an inference from\nexisting guest readiness."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9196
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Windows editor work is staged separately from the `windows-v1` capability\nrow. [`windows-runtime.nix`](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/vscode-web/windows-runtime.nix)\nprovides pinned Windows Node and ripgrep executables and writes a digest\nmanifest; its metadata is runtime-only, has no store references and keeps\n`editor_capability = false`. [`windows-source.nix`](repo:nix/domains/artifacts/vscode-web/windows-source.nix)\nstages the pinned Code OSS source, Windows headers/import libraries, embedded\nPython, extension adapters and offline build scripts. A source payload or a\nsuccessful compile alone does not alter the selected image manifest, add an\neditor descriptor or publish a browser origin. The native bundle, image admission and a Windows\nbrowser sign-in/edit/readback/recovery run remain unrecorded."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9449
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The authenticated `/settings#git-settings` section edits Git username, Git email\nand optional agent commit trailers for the current account. It is the fourth\nsection of the single Account page, behind the same fresh-login gate as profile,\npasskey and SSH key controls. The old settings URLs remain removed. Git email is\nseparate from sign-in email. Invalid or foreign-account changes refuse. Identity\nchanges apply to newly created machines.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9485
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The browser-fleet fixture retries focus custody before submitting a palette\ncommand and stops its fixture-owned personal worker after the policy probe. Those\nactions prevent stale Chromium focus and worker processes from contaminating a\nlater fixture, but they add no product authority, transport or editor evidence."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9525
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The current exploration repair restores ordinary successful properties to the\nengine's default 100 generated cases and retains existing larger budgets.\nEach property has one runner: labelled draws share its case, and nested\n`check all` execution options are rejected. Unsupported StreamData options\n`max_runs` and `max_shrinking_steps` are rejected rather than silently ignored.\nFinite exhaustive matrices may use ordinary tests when every matrix cell and\nassertion remains; one-case negative and replay controls are separately scoped."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9573
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The editor-bearing capability is currently limited to the reviewed version-4\nLinux image contract. Windows `windows-v1` and native FreeBSD/OmniOS\n(`freebsd-v1` and `illumos-v1`) rows are refused before this flow because their\nprotocol capabilities set `editor: false`; their terminal, SSH, readiness or\nagent receipts do not widen that boundary. The Linux package and authority\nchecks also do not constitute a current-revision browser journey: no current\nNixOS or Ubuntu sign-in, edit/save/readback and recovery receipt is recorded in\nthis status review, and the captured Alma/Arch receipts retain their earlier\nimage and source scope."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9577
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The evaluated `editor-target-boundaries` check independently asserts that the\neditor and broker package metadata target `x86_64-linux`, while the reviewed\nWindows v5 and native v6 package metadata has no editor field or capability.\nThis is an artifact-boundary check only; it does not establish a booted guest or\nbrowser journey."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9681
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The following records retain their original pins and source scopes. In particular,\nthe earlier one- and two-case clamps and ignored compatibility options described\nbelow are superseded by the current exploration repair; they are not current\nrunner guidance. Historical passes do not qualify the current changed tests."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9693
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The frontend preserves original BEAM failures when the native engine reports a\nflaky run. Its native installer stages and atomically renames the shared library:\ncompilation must not truncate an inode mapped into another running BEAM. The\nupstream native-backed suite and all eight Nix production release checks passed\nat `b0dec2a`. A separate test-build probe loaded the packaged NIF without an\noverride, counted 100 draws and reproduced a deliberate failure. Complete\napplication-suite qualification remains pending. Every Hegel revision change\nrequires all eleven Mix declarations and locks plus all eight Nix Mix dependency\nhashes. A pin, hash refresh or historical passing suite alone does not qualify\nthe installed NIF or the current consumers. The\n[exploration review](repo:docs/architecture/reviews/2026-10-18-hegel-exploration-and-native-custody.md)\nrecords the repair, exact intermediate results and remaining checks."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9770
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The machine controls expose Pause and Resume. Resume retains guest memory and\nmay wait for compatible capacity. A stopped machine still needs Start. Opening\na browser terminal rechecks current Cedar access and the image's terminal\ncapability before joining wake demand. Pending wake grants neither guest\ncontrols nor terminal tickets. Boot-log viewing, passive viewer checks and\nserver refresh timers do not wake a machine. A fresh authorized connection or\nexplicit terminal open may admit demand."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9982
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The same closure carries four reviewed Open VSX builtin extensions, immutable AI\npolicy and project language adapters. No global language toolchain or server is\nadded by the editor. Extension pins, verified extraction recipe, adapters and\npolicy contribute to the release identity; see [browser behavior](#interactive).\nUbuntu copies, registers and GC-roots their complete Nix closure during offline\nassembly; this is an explicit application exception under\n[ADR-0153](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0153-official-source-browser-editor.md).\nImage manifests are version 4 only. An editor-bearing manifest includes the exact\neditor protocol, official source commit and release hash in image identity; a v4\nmanifest without the editor descriptor remains a valid image that cannot open an\neditor. [Image manifest validation](repo:protocol/lib/overalls_protocol/image_manifest.ex)\nrefuses every other version and never infers the capability from package presence,\nso new host software cannot add it to a retained disk."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10046
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The signed-in sidebar groups Overview through Teams under \"Your workspace\";\nAccount, AI usage and Browser extension under \"Your account\"; and Field notes,\nHLDD and Source code under a separate \"Reference\" section. In the Control room\nthe sidebar shows only the administration tree; neither \"Your account\" nor\n\"Reference\" is rendered there. Each SSH key on the\naccount page carries an inline Description field with Rename."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10078
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The staged broker is a SYSTEM-owned boundary around that future payload. Its\nportable model binds boot, descriptor, placement, epoch, GuestAccount UUID,\nroster UID, canonical account name, local SAM SID and editor origin; identity\nderived pipe/job names and process creation time prevent foreign reuse. The\nWin32 launcher resolves the current local account, obtains an explicit S4U\ntoken, uses the SID-backed profile, starts a direct Node process suspended in a\nSYSTEM-only job, and verifies PID/time/SID/executable/job and named-pipe peer\ncustody before forwarding. `windows_main.rs` fetches MMDS identity, runs the\nclosed SSH authorizer and serializes one account startup; durable receipts\npermit only exact adoption after broker restart. Ambiguous paths, incomplete\ncustody, stale roster pairs and foreign processes refuse without cleanup. The\nbroker's 26 portable library and 7 Linux binary tests passed, with one deliberate\nsubprocess fixture ignored; both Linux and Windows clippy checks passed. A\nproduction Windows cross-link and 30 actual native Windows library tests passed,\nincluding named-pipe timeout/cancellation and Windows ACL descriptor/refusal\nchecks. These do not establish a listener/personal-worker lifecycle, candidate\nimage or browser acceptance. The\n[native harness](repo:nix/verification/tests/machines/windows-editor/README.md)\nhas six passing portable boundary/relay tests and static/type checks; its real\nWindows/browser assertions have not run."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10150
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "These are **desired states**. The VM resource permits idempotent start/stop\nrequests. Deletion is terminal intent. Relocation has additional quiesced-source\nhandoff and restoration transitions guarded by its coordinator."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10162
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "These native-family boundaries are also editor boundaries. FreeBSD and OmniOS\nhave no admitted editor package, broker, descriptor or browser acceptance, and\ntheir explicit false capability rows remain false even where a sealed image has\nreadiness, terminal, workspace, SSH or agent evidence. Those receipts cannot be\nused as a browser-editor result. A future native editor would require a new\nreviewed image and kernel/agent contract, protocol capability, broker admission\nand browser sign-in/edit/readback/recovery receipt."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10174
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "This capability remains an explicitly Linux-only v4 contract. At the current\nrevision there is no recorded native browser sign-in, edit/save/readback or\nrecovery receipt against the current NixOS or Ubuntu image; the historical and\ncaptured Alma/Arch results retain their source, image and fixture scope. Package\nclosure, descriptor, broker, CLI, terminal or SSH evidence cannot be promoted\nto current browser acceptance. Windows `windows-v1`, FreeBSD `freebsd-v1` and\nOmniOS/illumos `illumos-v1` rows keep `editor: false`; their readiness, agent,\nterminal or SSH evidence does not create an editor capability. A future\nnon-Linux editor requires a separately reviewed image, agent/protocol contract,\nbroker and browser journey."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10222
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Thread reads require current creator, team or canonical thread-grant authority. Creation checks\ncurrent team membership before Compute admission; prompting, terminal access,\narchive and deletion recheck current machine/Cedar authority. A foreign thread\nID, removed team membership, stale mounted session, failed provisioning state or\narchived machine must refuse prompt and terminal mutations. A current authorized\nreader can still read an archived thread and its transcript;\nthe transcript helper validates that the retained agent thread belongs to the\nbound root before reading events. Archive, delete and reopen require current\nthread-management authority, including manager grants. Reopen clears the retired binding and\nuses a fresh machine key; stale provision jobs cannot mutate that generation.\nPrevious transcript generations remain retained and an authorized reader can\nselect their generation through the read-only `/threads/:id?generation=N` path;\nthe selected binding repeats root, owner and thread checks and never grants\nmachine mutation. Thread diagnostics contain only thread identifiers and closed\noutcome atoms; objective text, model content,\nterminal bytes, machine payloads and credentials remain in authorized data.\nArchival invalidates a pending planning barrier with the durable `archived`\nreason before lifecycle deletion completes."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10682
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Wake admission and terminal outcomes, and relay issue/consume, emit catalogued\nevents only after the outer database transaction commits. Their metadata contains\nresource UUIDs, never capabilities, requests or guest content. Relay admission\nand renewal recheck the origin host's enabled, current authority as well as both\ngateway generations; retiring or disconnecting that origin ends its authority.\nAn enabled evacuating origin may keep forwarding while its workloads move.\n"
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10686
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Wake admission follows identity and fresh authorization, then durable demand,\ncapacity admission and physical readiness, and a fresh connection grant. A pin\nnever grants access. Waiters renew authority; revoked keys, sessions or\npublications must stop waiting. Relay capabilities are single-use and bound to\nboth current gateways and the exact admitted stream over fleet transport.\nDisabled, retired or stale origins cannot admit demand or consume/renew relay\nauthority. Caller input cannot choose arbitrary upstream hosts or ports."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10812
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Whether to enable single-hop authorized ingress over the host fleet WireGuard network."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10822
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Windows has only a staged editor runtime/source and broker boundary. The\nruntime and source recipes keep `editor_capability = false`; the portable model\nand Win32 launcher bind workers to MMDS boot identity, descriptor, placement,\nepoch, GuestAccount/UID, local SAM SID and origin, with SYSTEM-only jobs,\nprofile-backed paths, exact process and named-pipe custody, and refusal of\nforeign or incomplete recovery. The listener and roster authorizer code has no\nimage admission or recorded native Windows execution. A browser origin, native\nbundle, sign-in, edit/save/readback and restart recovery remain open."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10930
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0199](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0199-memory-suspension-and-demand-wake.md)\nadds desired and observed `paused` states. Pause is a durable lifecycle operation;\nits physical outcome requires a sealed full memory/device checkpoint and proven\nVMM/cgroup absence. Only then can compute reservations be released. Resume is a\ncoalesced durable `WakeRequest`, rather than an acknowledgement of execution.\nIts state machine waits for capacity, relocates if necessary, resumes, and reaches\nready only after current execution readiness. Failed or ambiguous restore never\nfalls back to cold boot. Stop explicitly discards suspended memory; delete also\nrequires checkpoint reclamation. Start cannot bypass a retained checkpoint."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10934
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0200](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0200-windows-browser-editor-candidate.md)\naccepts the account-bound native Windows candidate: a source-built Code OSS\nbundle, SYSTEM gateway broker, verified local SAM/S4U workers, exact job/pipe\nrecovery and a supervised fixed task. Implementation remains partial. The\nexisting `windows-v1` false editor row is unchanged. Only the separate\n`windows-editor-v1` candidate uses manifest v7 with a required content-bound\neditor tuple and portable bundle v4 preserving that tuple. The candidate is\nnot selected in the production catalog; exact native/browser qualification\nremains unrecorded."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10938
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "[ADR-0200](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0200-windows-browser-editor-candidate.md)\nrecords the accepted Windows candidate design, including bounded cancelled-pipe custody,\npackage/PATH ACL checks and the fixed SYSTEM task. Its separate\n`windows-editor-v1` manifest v7 requires an immutable editor descriptor; bundle v4\nretains it across placement-local starts. Existing `windows-v1` stays false and\nthe candidate remains outside the selected production catalog. Native account,\ntask restart, sustained cancellation under worker lifecycle and authenticated\nbrowser execution remain unrecorded. Thirty actual Windows library tests cover\nnamed-pipe timeout/cancellation and ACL descriptor/refusal checks only; source\nor harness presence cannot grant deployed image acceptance."
msgstr ""

