#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6276
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Language headers and session values are untrusted, bounded and validated against the supported locale set. Only the current user may persist their override; reset restores browser negotiation. Neither locale nor text direction changes login, Cedar, execution fences, storage ownership or recovery authority. The Porsche envelope carries a canonical locale for human prose, with English fallback for older peers; machine identifiers and terminal byte streams stay unchanged. Bidi isolation protects interpolated identifiers from misleading direction changes. See [ADR-0197](repo:docs/architecture/decisions/0197-localized-human-surfaces.md); native/browser execution and linguistic review remain gaps until recorded."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6500
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Marshall publishes editor VM owners and the neutral webview owner only through\nhealthy gateways with separate live five-second asset receipts matching service\ngeneration, host session and every configured target's exact approved package.\nThe per-release receipt identity allows Linux and Windows tuples to coexist\nwithout replacing or extending each other's expiry. Neutral webview DNS cannot\nroute by release, so a missing or expired member withdraws that gateway from\nboth editor and neutral webview discovery. Before each receipt, Audi validates all\nfixed asset files and requires a fresh verified TLS HEAD through its configured\nlocal IPv4 HTTPS listener, with operator roots, exact hash SNI/Host and the current\nrelease path. The bounded proof refuses suspended acceptors and wrong trust,\nrelease or immutable response headers. A stale/missing receipt withholds discovery; DNS never grants\neditor authority. Current signed-in write and exact release/execution leases still\ngovern every connection. Customer's exact-owner fixed editor is the sole new\ningress exception; ordinary guest web remains refused even for its owner."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:6528
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Marshall's wrapper owns `MARSHALL_INGRESS_EDITOR_RELEASES`, a closed JSON object\nwith `version: 1` and a `targets` object mapping those target names to exact\n`protocol`, `commit` and `release` descriptors. Unknown fields or targets,\nmalformed descriptors and duplicate release digests refuse startup. The wrapper\nalways sets the registry, including the empty value; inherited environment files\ncannot supply editor authority. There is no singular-option or singleton-reader\nfallback. Audi's versioned configuration maps `tls.editor_packages` targets to\nimmutable store roots, validates each package and runs a separate readiness\npublisher for each release."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation.ex:69
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Public documentation for Overalls browser extensions and SSH machine management (Porsche v1)."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8003
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Required refusal: Withhold editor/webview records when any configured target lacks a live five-second matching receipt or the current route is unhealthy. A tuple approved only for the other image target grants no capability. Refuse malformed/foreign/duplicate registry entries, wrong release, service role, stale credentials, unknown paths, symlinks and unsafe asset methods."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:8451
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Tested scope: Accepted design in ADR-0197; no implementation execution is claimed by this scenario."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9163
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The Overview machine preview, Machines list and ready Threads workspace reuse one\neligibility-gated projection for the same **Open VS Code** action alongside\ntheir terminal actions. The Machines\ntable groups both controls in one **Connect** column. An eligible running machine\nopens the personal workbench in a new browser tab. Stopped,\nunsupported-image and temporarily unavailable machines show a disabled action\nwith the reason; unsupported images direct users to create a new machine using\nthe current image. Users without editor authority do not receive the control. These\nlist views are read-only projections, and opening the link still performs fresh\nEditor admission under the current login, write grant, account and execution.\nThe displayed eligibility is a bounded authorized view, not a grant. Opening\nstill performs fresh Editor admission, and disabling an image for future launches\ndoes not revoke an already running machine's matching editor capability.\nEditor admission selects the approved tuple from the validated image target:\nLinux/x86_64 kernel images use `linux-x64`; the separate Windows/x86_64\nfirmware `windows-editor-v1` candidate uses `win32-x64`. Approval of a tuple for\nthe other target grants no capability. DNS publication requires manifest4 for\nLinux or candidate manifest7 for Windows and the exact target's commit, release\nand protocol. Current route publication and each gateway asset receipt must\nagree with the VM's current execution and host session. Every advertised gateway\nalso needs current receipts for the entire configured release set; expiry of\neither release withdraws its editor and neutral webview discovery. These records\nremain discovery only and do not authorize editor access.\nSSH `ls`/`show` keep their existing machine response fields; the workspace status\nis not added to their public command protocol."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:9436
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "The approved editor registry is finite and target-specific. A valid Linux tuple\ncannot authorize a Windows image or vice versa. Each immutable asset receipt is\nbound to its full protocol/commit/release tuple as well as current service\ngeneration and host session. Concurrent reports preserve independent expiries.\nPublication requires every approved release on an advertised gateway; a missing,\nstale or foreign report cannot leave a neutral webview address that serves only\npart of the configured set. SQL-backed tests establish these database rules;\nnative multi-gateway DNS, TLS and publisher-fault histories remain separate."
msgstr ""

#: lib/marshall/documentation_messages.ex:10290
